[Game]
Game=Sherlock (1984): annotated disassembly
Logo=Sherlock: annotated disassembly
StyleSheet=skoolkit.css;sherlock.css

[Bug:reversed_two_word_verb_error:Two-word verb error messages print with the words reversed]
Confirmed by actually typing commands in the emulator: <code>GET UP</code> produces <code>I DO NOT KNOW THE VERB" UP GET"</code> - the second word printed before the first. Single-word unknown verbs (<code>STAND</code>, <code>SCORE</code>, <code>HELP</code>) are not affected, and <code>ASK ... ABOUT ...</code> is not affected either (its two words print in the order typed). Not yet traced to a specific routine or explained; recorded here as a genuine, player-visible behaviour rather than speculated about further.

Narrowed down on 8 November: it happens when the second word is a direction - a verb in its own right - and not with a preposition. GET UP and LOOK UP give 'UP GET' and 'UP LOOK'; PUT INTO LAMP and PUT LAMP INTO SOFA give 'PUT INTO', in order. The message (#R$686A, printed by #R$B84C) prints the typed verb's word slots in order, and the verb reader puts a direction first on purpose: in #R$AB91 a class-$20 word (a direction) arriving when the first slot already holds a verb moves that verb to the second slot and takes the first itself (#R$ABE1-#R$ABED). That is what lets GO NORTH match the NORTH record, whose GO is an extra word; for a phrase that matches nothing it puts UP before GET in the complaint. So a cosmetic side effect rather than a fault in the message code.

[Bug:sit_in_occupied_armchair:You can climb into Watson's armchair while he is sitting in it]
Confirmed in the emulator: GET INTO WATSONS ARMCHAIR (or FOLLOW WATSON, which ends up doing the same) answers 'You get into watsons armchair.' at the start of the game, with Watson still in it. The fit check (#R$DEE4) only compares sizes: the armchair's capacity is 255 and Holmes and Watson are 96 each, leaving 63 to spare. Poking the capacity shows the limit (192 refuses).

[Bug:follow_watson_armchair:FOLLOW WATSON climbs into his armchair and runs on by itself]
Confirmed in the emulator: FOLLOW WATSON at the start of the game answers 'You climb into watsons armchair.' (Watson is sitting in it), then the clock keeps advancing for several turns without further input while the follow carries on. Whether that counts as a bug or a feature of FOLLOW is a judgement call.

[Bug:one_front_door_for_three_houses:One front door for three houses]
Doors are things, and to save memory one door thing serves several doorways in different buildings: a door's record lists every room it appears in (record +15...), and its open/closed state (+5 bit 5) is shared by all of them. The front door (thing 19) is the front door of Baker Street, of the Jones house (rooms 93/96) and of the house on Cobden Lane (51/56). Confirmed in the emulator: at the start every one of them is shut ('YOU CANNOT GO SOUTH' at Jones Front Door); after OPEN FRONT DOOR in your own entrance hallway, SOUTH at Jones Front Door and EAST in Cobden Lane both walk straight in, without anyone opening them. (The player was placed at those doors by poking the location rather than walked there.) 10 door things are shared like this: front door (3 doorways); stairway (4 doorways); plain door (6 doorways); large oak door (2 doorways); diningroom door (3 doorways); thick heavy door (4 doorways); back door (2 doorways); heavy oak door (3 doorways); ornate door (2 doorways); sittingroom door (2 doorways).

[Bug:gardener_early_riser:Bedtime is sometimes four hours short]
At 9pm the bedtime routine that Strachan, the cook and the gardener share (#R$9EC1, #R$9ECB, #R$9ED1, all ending at #R$9ED7) puts the character to bed - Strachan in Daphne's room, the other two off stage - until its next turn, 504 minutes plus a random number up to 30 later (#R$9EFE: LD DE,$01F8, then ADD A,E / LD E,A with the random number). The add is to E alone, so when the random number is 8 or more the low byte wraps and the carry into D is lost: 256 minutes vanish and he is back after about 4 hours 10 minutes instead of 8 hours 25. Logged in the emulator for the gardener (tools/experiments/phipps_route.py 8): off at 9:05pm every night; back at 5:30am on Tuesday (a small random number), but at 1:40am on Wednesday and 1:35am on Thursday - 4 hours 35 and 4 hours 30, i.e. 248 + 27 and 248 + 22 minutes. A gardener pottering about in the small hours is harmless, but it is plainly not what the 504 was for; the same goes for the cook and for Strachan, who share the code.

[Bug:all_but:ALL BUT leaves nothing out]
The parser understands ALL BUT (and ALL EXCEPT): BUT after ALL sets #R$A01A to 2 and opens a parse node marked with bit 6 (#R$B01C, #R$B029), and #R$B517 is there to skip any candidate named in such a node. But in the emulator the exception was never made. In the closet, with the money dropped: TAKE ALL BUT MONEY gave 'You take the china mans disguise. ... You take your money.'; DROP ALL BUT MONEY dropped both; GET ALL BUT MONEY and TAKE ALL EXCEPT DISGUISE took both, the disguise included. #R$A01A was 2 and #R$A01A($A01C) $80 after each, so the check at #R$B517 was reached.

The cause, found with breakpoints on #R$B517-#R$B541: #R$B517 expects IY to be the current parse node and walks on from it (#R$AEA9) looking for the BUT node. But by then IY holds the address of the search routine - #R$B4C1 loads it with LD IY,#R$D36A (or #R$D39B) so that #R$B4A0 can call it. Every time, IY was #R$D36A on entry, so the 'next node' was read from the bytes before that routine's code (#R$C9D8($C9D9), then #R$CDF7($CDF9)...), none of which has bit 6 of +1 in the right place, and no candidate was ever excluded. The fix would be to keep the parse node in another register, or to push it before #R$B4C1 changes IY.

[Bug:in_is_visited:The word IN is read as VISITED, so typing IN does nothing]
In the dictionary IN's record has bit 7 of its count byte set, which makes it a synonym: its two extra bytes point at the record to use instead, and they point at VISITED (offset $0956) - a word of class $E0, which the parser skips (#R$AB91). Every other synonym points at a word of its own kind (READ at EXAMINE, N at NORTH...).

So whenever IN is typed it vanishes. Tried in the emulator, with the token buffer at #LINK(rom_symbols#a5B81)($5B81) read after each command: PUT LAMP IN SOFA tokenises IN as $E0 $0956 and gets 'WHAT ?'; PUT LAMP INTO YOUR ARMCHAIR gets 'I DO NOT KNOW THE VERB" PUT INTO"' and PUT LAMP ON SOFA 'YOU CANNOT PUT ON THE SOFA', so the parser itself is fine. SIT IN YOUR ARMCHAIR works only because SIT alone does (SIT ARMCHAIR asks 'WHICH ARMCHAIR ?'). The verbs that contain IN - DROP IN (17), PUT IN (38), SIT IN (40), the only verb records using a word the tokenizer replaces - can still be reached by leaving IN out, since a missing preposition is not held against a match: PUT LAMP SOFA gave 'YOU CANNOT PUT IN THE SOFA'. But PUT LAMP IN SOFA, the natural thing to type, gets 'WHAT ?'. Found with source/dictionary.py's synonym list; the link looks like a data slip in the master dictionary.

[Bug:and_to:GIVE THE LAMP AND THE MONEY TO WATSON gives nothing]
When a command names two things joined by AND (or a comma) and then a second object - GIVE LAMP AND YOUR MONEY TO WATSON - the parser makes two sentences, GIVE LAMP and (GIVE) YOUR MONEY TO WATSON. The forward pass of #R$AD57 gives the second sentence the verb; a backward pass at #R$AE1E is meant to give the earlier sentences the later one's TO WATSON (it compares each sentence with the next, and when the earlier one has no second object and the same verb, copies the preposition and the object across with #R$AF27). It never does: the loop steps one sentence back (#R$AEC5) before each test, and the call at #R$AE22 has already stepped back once, so the last pair is never compared and the last comparison is made against the head of the list. In the emulator, carrying the lamp and the money, GIVE LAMP AND YOUR MONEY TO WATSON says I SEE NOTHING THAT YOU CAN GIVE: the first sentence is GIVE LAMP with no one to give it to (the parse nodes were dumped at #R$AE57: the first node has only GIVE and LAMP; the second has YOUR MONEY, TO and WATSON). GIVE YOUR MONEY TO WATSON on its own, and DROP LAMP AND YOUR MONEY, work.

Taking the call at #R$AE22 out (three zero bytes) fixes it: the same command then prints 'You give the oil lamp to Watson.' and 'You give your money to Watson.', one minute apart. With three things the loop would then pass the object back one sentence at a time.

[Bug:scenery_literals:Punctuation in a room's description scrambles its scenery words]
To find scenery words (#R$B6FD) the game reads the room's description with its own small walker, #R$B77F, rather than the printer. The two disagree about bytes $20-$3F: the printer (#R$A5F2) prints them as single characters - spaces, digits, commas and full stops - but #R$B77F sends everything from $20 to $5F to its packed-letters code (#R$B7DD), which skips (A AND $1F) + 1 five-bit letters. So after the first comma or full stop the walker is reading the middle of packed text as dictionary references.

Traced in the emulator 2026-11-08 by noting each word #R$B77F hands back. In the hansom cab (room 1, 'You are travelling the streets of London in a hansom cab, the sun shines through the windows onto your face.') it returns TRAVELL, STREET, LONDON, HANSOM and CAB, then the comma at #R$7DE9($7DF0) sends it eleven bytes on, to two references (#R$9A10($9A12), #R$E2CB($E2CC)) that are really packed letters, and it stops. TAKE STREETS there gets YOU CANNOT TAKE THE STREET; TAKE WINDOWS gets I SEE NO WINDOW THAT YOU CAN TAKE. On the underground (room 3) the full stop at #R$7E5D($7E63) is followed by six such references ($0745, $0666, ...). In the sitting room (room 8) the words that matter come first, so TAKE WINDOWS still gets YOU CANNOT TAKE THE WINDOW, though even there a space byte at #R$7D05($7D08) produces one false word.

Making the walker step over $20-$3F (a breakpoint at #R$B7DD that jumps back to #R$B7AA) puts the cab's words after the comma back in the walk, though it then follows the night-time text (FLICKER, GAS, LAMP...) because the walker ignores the operands of the control codes below $1D as well.

[Bug:stale_quote_queue:A failed SAY TO leaves its quotation behind, so later SAYs deliver old speech]
Every quotation typed in a SAY TO is added to a list at #R$9FEC (#R$B049) and the SAY that actually runs takes the first node (#R$B86A). A SAY that fails earlier (I SEE NO ... THAT YOU CAN TALK TO, or a person who does not answer) never removes its node, so every later SAY delivers the quotation typed N commands earlier, N being the number of leftovers. Seen in the emulator: SAY TO CABBIE "GO TO KINGS CROSS ROAD" got only 'You talk to the cabbie.' and the cab sat still until the SAY had been typed once more than there were leftovers; TALK TO <person> with no quotation empties the list. A player who waits after such a SAY (the cab 'takes' 80 minutes) loses hours.

[Bug:give_money_purse:GIVE MONEY TO CABBIE hands over the whole purse]
The money is a single object (thing 27), so GIVE MONEY TO CABBIE gives the cabbie everything Holmes carries: INVENTORY then shows no money, PAY says DON'T BE SILLY and the cabbie nags 'What about me money guv'nor?'. PAY 10D TO CABBIE (#R$E370) takes only the sum and returns change. Seen in the emulator.

[Bug:burning_parses_as_burnt:BURNING NOTE is read as BURNT NOTE, so the burning note cannot be named]
While the note is called a burning note in the inventory, EXAMINE BURNING NOTE, DROP BURNING NOTE and READ BURNING NOTE all answer 'I SEE NO BURNT NOTE THAT YOU CAN ...'; BURNIN and BURNINGS fail the same way. NOTE alone asks WHICH NOTE ? and answering BURNING does not help. BURN (four letters) is read as the right word, and so is SINGE once the note has been taken properly. Seen in the emulator; the cause (BURNING and BURNT share their first four letters in the dictionary, and the typed word resolves to the later one) is a reading of the word tables, not traced.

[Bug:take_from_skips_the_script:TAKE NOTE FROM FIREPLACE takes the burning note without stopping it burning]
Only the plain TAKE runs shared action 20 (#R$DF7E/#R$DF8B), which sets the countdown #R$9CA1 to $FF and renames the note 'singed'. TAKE NOTE FROM FIREPLACE moves the note into the inventory and leaves the countdown running (seen: #R$9CA1 still falling), so the note burns to ashes while carried and the statement that needs it can no longer be proven. The cure is DROP then a plain TAKE.

[Bug:lestrade_stuck_in_cab:Riding a cab at the wrong time leaves Lestrade stuck in the other cab for good]
Lestrade hails a cab at about 12:20am and again at 7:05am on Tuesday. If the player is riding a cab at those times the two scripts collide and Lestrade stays in cab 33 at its off-stage room (pc #R$EBC5) for the rest of the game, so everything that needs him (the large rock, the statements, the chase) cannot happen. Seen in the emulator during the first Tuesday attempts.

[Fact:the_last_scene:How the game is won (and was won, in the emulator)]
The only winning text in the game is at #R$6AF3, and it is printed in one place: Phipps's script, when he walks onto platform one at Victoria and finds you waiting with Watson and Lestrade (#R$9E48). Staged in the emulator, it reads: 'As Basil enters he sees you and pulls a gun. With suprising agility Watson pushes you out of the way of Basils first shot allowing Lestrade time to pull his gun and capture the culprits. As incredible as it may seem you have solved this case. You truly deserve the title of worlds greatest detective.' Leave either companion behind and Basil's first shot disposes of you instead.

Getting there (#R$E7E7, #R$EE2B): prove that Phipps has plans and that the sale is at Old Mill Road, ride with Lestrade in the police cab to Old Mill Road on Wednesday after 3pm (Phipps waits there from 12:10 and gives up at about 3:20), let Phipps bolt, and on the platform at Leatherhead, where Lestrade waits for a train home, tell him to follow you (SAY TO LESTRADE "FOLLOW ME" works - tried in the emulator); his script then follows you until you reach platform one at Victoria, which is exactly where Phipps is going. Be there, with Watson, before Phipps walks in.

Played through in the emulator, with the statements poked proven: the 4:55 train is gone before Lestrade will take the order, but the police cab can be told SAY TO POLICE MAN "GO FURIOUSLY TO KINGS CROSS ROAD" (74 minutes instead of 94), and from Kings Cross platform one the underground train reaches Victoria at 6:40 - two minutes before Basil. The game was won that way. Board the police cab in the same minute as Lestrade at the start, or he or you is left behind. Full steps under #R$E7E7. The spelling 'SUPRISING' is the game's.

UPDATE, 8 November night: everything above was first staged with statements poked proven and a minute short. It has since been played from a fresh boot with typed commands only (v11_full_win.cmds, 444 lines; transcript v11_full_win.transcript.txt). The fix to the 'minute short' problem was GO FURIOUSLY TO KINGS CROSS ROAD to the police man (74 minutes instead of 95) after ordering Lestrade to FOLLOW ME on platform two, and boarding the 6:29 underground train from Kings Cross platform one in the minute before it leaves so that Lestrade (who follows a turn behind) gets in. See 'The winning line from Wednesday 10:53am, typed'.

[Fact:the_win_typed:The winning line from Wednesday 10.53am, typed]
SAY TO LESTRADE "THE SALE LOCATION IS OLD MILL ROAD" in Scotland Yard at 10:53am (the singed note examined, PHIPPS HAS PLANS already proven) and Lestrade sets off at once. Follow him and keep behind: hansom cab (not Lestrade's) to Kings Cross Road, the 12:00 train from platform four, and get OUT at Leather Head platform one at 1:30pm (riding on to platform two loses 15 minutes and the police cab). SOUTH twice to the main street and, without a LOOK in between, CLIMB INTO POLICE CAB at 1:37pm, the minute Lestrade does: you, Watson and Lestrade fit and Straker stays out. The cab reaches Old Mill Road at 3:13pm, Phipps bolts at 3:14, back in the main street at 4:47. CLIMB OUT, NORTH three times to platform two (4:54), SAY TO LESTRADE "FOLLOW ME" once he takes orders at 4:56, SOUTH three times, CLIMB INTO POLICE CAB and SAY TO POLICE MAN "GO FURIOUSLY TO KINGS CROSS ROAD" at 5:06 (no TALK TO, no extra LOOK: every minute counts), arriving 6:21. CLIMB OUT, NORTHEAST twice to platform one, CLIMB INTO TRAIN the moment it is in (6:28, before it pulls out at 6:29, so that Lestrade, who follows a turn behind, gets on), ride to Victoria platform one (6:41), CLIMB OUT and WAIT: Basil walks in at 6:42 and the game ends with 'YOU TRULY DESERVE THE TITLE OF WORLDS GREATEST DETECTIVE'. Do it a minute or two later and Lestrade misses the train or Phipps leaves Victoria first. The whole run from the first command is tools/experiments/playthrough/v11_full_win.cmds (444 lines, with dead ends) and the cleaned v12_clean_win.cmds (372 lines).

[Fact:second_winning_route:A second winning route - shadow Lestrade from Scotland Yard]
tools/experiments/playthrough/v13_shadow_lestrade.cmds (368 lines) wins too. It matches the cleaned route up to the sale-location statement to Lestrade (Wednesday 10:53am), then, instead of taking a hansom cab of your own and the noon train from platform four, you go WEST to Parliament Street, CLIMB INTO HANSOM CAB while Lestrade is in it (he has hailed it at 10:54 and climbed in at 10:56; you climb in at 10:57 and Watson follows), reach Kings Cross Road at 11:11, walk NORTHEAST to platform three, and board the steam train that enters at 12:15pm when Lestrade does. It reaches Leatherhead platform one at 1:30pm, the same minute as the other route, and everything after that is the same. The first route is therefore not the only one; what is common to both is the chain of statements and evidence, the 1:37pm police cab with Lestrade, the FOLLOW ME on platform two at 4:55-4:56, the 6:29 underground train with both Lestrade and Watson, and Victoria at 6:41.

[Fact:replay_is_deterministic:The game is deterministic - the same typed commands always give the same game]
The whole unpoked win, 444 typed lines starting from a fresh boot, was replayed from the file and ended in exactly the same state (clock 6:43pm, room 30, end flag set) as the live run, even though the live run was assembled over several sessions out of snapshots. Nothing in the game's random numbers changes with real time; the same command text and the same earlier history always give the same result.

[Fact:tuesday_ordering:Tuesday's order of work that fits the clock]
Played without pokes: 12:55am cab to Kings Cross Road; 7:30am train; 9:00 Leatherhead; 9:20 rock with Lestrade; letters from Basil's library safe by 10:05 (OPEN WALL SAFE takes 15 minutes; TAKE LETTERS then EXAMINE CLOSELY); the 10:55 train from platform two reaches Kings Cross at noon; Tricia is in her lounge until 2pm, so ask about the clothes (second SAY, the first is swallowed by the leftover quote), then Mrs Jones about her alibi, open her safe (15 minutes) and examine the unfinished note and the folder; then Scotland Yard for the statements, TALK TO LESTRADE first to flush quotes; Camden Street's window opens at 10:03pm.

[Fact:burning_note_typed:The burning note - TAKE NOTE FROM FIREPLACE, then drop and plain TAKE it]
Played without pokes on Wednesday. The note appears in the bedroom fireplace (room 35) at about 10:05am and burns away over the next 15 turns. Phipps shoots anyone in the bedroom at 9:50am, even in disguise, so go in only after he has left the house (hall 10:06, street 10:09, gone by 10:13); the window stays open and Basil's own chase does not start until the sale statement is proven. 'TAKE NOTE FROM FIREPLACE' works but takes the note through the plain action, so the countdown keeps running. DROP BURN NOTE then TAKE BURN NOTE runs shared action 20 (#R$DF7E/#R$DF8B), which sets the countdown #R$9CA1 to $FF (stopped) and renames the note 'singed' ($07D5); EXAMINE CLOSELY SINGE NOTE then counts as examining the evidence and the case record #R$99B7 keeps its proof bit. The parser quirk that cost most of an hour: the dictionary stores BURNING and BURNT with the same first four letters, and a typed BURNING resolves to the word BURNT, so 'EXAMINE BURNING NOTE' says 'I SEE NO BURNT NOTE'. 'EXAMINE BURN NOTE' (four letters) resolves to the right word, and 'SINGE NOTE' works once it is singed.

[Fact:followers_lag_one_turn:Lestrade and Watson follow one turn behind you, so a train can leave without them]
Seen at Kings Cross platform one on Wednesday evening. The underground train entered at 6:26 and pulled out at 6:29. Arriving in the room at 6:28 and climbing in at once, the first time (one minute later than the winning run), Watson came aboard but Lestrade, who was still walking in, did not: the train left with Watson only, and the game could not be won from there. In the winning run you arrive a minute earlier, CLIMB INTO TRAIN at 6:27-6:28, and both come in before it leaves. Anyone you have told to FOLLOW ME repeats your move on the next turn, so any departure with a fixed minute (trains, the police cab) needs a spare minute.

[Fact:train_goes_on_to_platform_two:Riding the Leatherhead train past your stop costs the police cab]
The steam train from Kings Cross platform four reaches Leather Head platform one at 1:30pm (if it left at noon), and then is 'shunted between platforms' to platform two, arriving there at about 1:45pm. Staying aboard to see where it goes (waiting for the text 'platform two') put me at the wrong platform fifteen minutes late; Lestrade had already left the station in the police cab and the winning window was gone. Get out at platform one and go SOUTH twice.

[Fact:police_cab_seats:The police cab fits four - you, Watson, Lestrade and the constable - not Chief Constable Straker]
In the main street at Leather Head on Wednesday: if you climb in the minute before Lestrade, the cab holds you, Watson and the local police man and drives off without him; if you wait until he and Chief Constable Straker have climbed in, 'I SEE NO POLICE CAB THAT YOU CAN CLIMB INTO'. Climbing in in the same minute as Lestrade (at 1:37pm after arriving on the 1:30pm train and walking SOUTH twice) gives you, Watson, Lestrade and the constable, and Straker is left standing on the pavement.

[Fact:ordering_the_cab_strands_lestrade:Giving the police cab your own orders leaves Lestrade behind]
At 4:47pm, back in the main street with Lestrade, Watson and the constable aboard, SAY TO POLICE MAN "GO FURIOUSLY TO KINGS CROSS ROAD" made the cab leave without Lestrade: he climbed out and walked north to the station, and 'Lestrade, Watson and you' became just Watson and you. Lestrade only takes an order from about 4:56pm, on platform two ('Sorry, I cannot do that now' before then and after the first successful FOLLOW ME), so the order to the cab has to be given only after he is following you again.

[Fact:phipps_shoots_the_bedroom:Basil's bedroom is deadly at 9.50am on Wednesday, even in disguise]
Waiting in the bedroom (room 35) in the china man's disguise through 9:50am on Wednesday gets you shot: the game ends. The disguise protects you from the check on your own identity, not from the shot that is fired at whoever is in the room. Phipps is in the hall at 10:06, in the street at 10:09 and in his cab by 10:13; the burning note is revealed in the fireplace at about 10:05 and, unless it is taken with a plain TAKE, is ashes by about 10:23.

Measured with the player waiting in the front yard from Tuesday 11pm: Phipps is in the bedroom (room 35) through the night, the burning note is revealed at 10:05am and its countdown runs 15 to 0 once a minute, so it is ashes at 10:24am; Phipps is in the hall (33) at 10:06, in Camden Street (32) at 10:09 and in his cab by 10:15. The earlier no-interference log (phipps_route.py) shows him back out at 9:55am. Reconciled on 9 November: at #R$EEE9 Phipps checks whether you (room 102, Basil's front yard, or Camden Street) or the china man are outside; if so he runs the messenger scene at #R$EFAC ('A MESSENGER ARRIVES AT THE DOOR...') and waits 15 minutes (#R$DDBE) before going. phipps_route.py has you indoors in the sitting room, so he leaves at once (Camden Street at 9:55am, the street at 10:00); with you in the front yard, as in the winning route, the same script reaches the hall at about 10:06 and the street by 10:10 (logged from the route's Tuesday-night position, script #R$EEE9 then #R$EF09, 15 minutes later). Both are right; they are different conditions.

[Fact:window_exit_bedroom:The bedroom window works both ways - SOUTHWEST to the front yard, WEST to Camden Street]
The same window you enter by on Tuesday evening (NORTHEAST from the front yard, room 102) lets you leave on Wednesday morning without passing through the house: SOUTHWEST from the bedroom puts you in Basil's front yard and WEST there in Camden Street, where a hansom cab can be hailed at once. NORTHWEST through the large oak door said YOU CANNOT GO NORTHWEST while the door was shut (I did not try opening it that time), so the window was the way out used.

[Fact:messenger_note_watched:The messenger who delivers a note to Basil is shown only if you are watching]
On Wednesday morning, at about 9:50, Phipps's script (#R$EEE9) checks whether you are in Camden Street or Basil's front yard (or the china man is). If so it prints 'A messenger arrives at the door, gives a note to Basil and dissapears down the street.' (the typo is the game's) at 9:51-9:55, and Phipps then waits 15 minutes before leaving the bedroom (hall at about 10:06, street 10:10, cab 10:15). With you elsewhere he skips the scene and is in Camden Street at 9:55. Seen in the emulator on 9 November from the winning route's Tuesday-night position (window of Basil's house) against a plain WAIT run from the sitting room. The note Basil is given is presumably the one you later find burning in the bedroom (revealed at 10:05am); the link was not traced.

[Fact:train_circle_timings:The underground train - Kings Cross platform one at 6.29, Victoria platform one at 6.41, Paddington at 6.43]
The steam trains on the underground circle run on a fixed timetable. From Kings Cross platform one the 6:29 train reaches Victoria platform one at 6:41, so you have a minute to CLIMB OUT before Basil walks onto the platform at 6:42. Missing the 6:29 and taking the next train at 6:36-6:38 reaches Paddington at 6:43 and Victoria much too late: Basil is already at Buckingham Palace Road (room 29) at 6:40 and leaves the game when he finds nobody on the platform.

[Fact:lestrade_orders_tried:Which orders Lestrade takes on platform two (and when)]
Played on 9 November from Wednesday 4:55pm on Leatherhead platform two, after the chase. SAY TO LESTRADE "WAIT" gets 'Inspector Lestrade waits.'; "FOLLOW ME" makes him follow (he and Watson trail one turn behind). "GO TO VICTORIA", "GO TO PLATFORM ONE" and "GO SOUTH" are accepted as speech ('You talk to Inspector Lestrade.') and then nothing happens. FOLLOW ME at 4:54pm, a minute too early, gets 'Inspector Lestrade says "Sorry, I cannot do that now."' because his script is still busy getting to the platform; from 4:56 he takes orders. So there is no way to send him to Victoria ahead of you: he has to be walked there with FOLLOW ME, and no second structurally different winning route was found in the time spent looking; that is a statement about the search, not a proof that none exists.

[Fact:lestrade_cab_clash:Lestrade rides the same cab - keep out of cabs at 12.20-12.45am and 7.05-7.20am]
Lestrade hails a cab twice in the night: Scotland Yard side at about 12:20am (Slater Street to Parliament Street) and from Parliament Street at 7:05am (to Kings Cross Road, then the 7:30 train to Leatherhead, where he is at the stream, rooms 88-89, from about 9:15 to 10:05am). If the player is riding a cab then, Lestrade is left sitting in the other cab (33) at its off-stage room for the rest of the game, and everything that needs him (the rock) cannot happen.

[Fact:cab_change_and_fares:Fares seen in the run - 2d from Camden Street to Parliament Street, 6d from Parliament Street to Kings Cross Road]
Paying with PAY 10D TO CABBIE, the cabbie gave change each time ('You pay 10d. To the cabbie. The cabbie pays 8d. To you.' after the 2d ride; 4d after a 6d ride). Two hansom cabs were standing in Parliament Street at once on Wednesday, one with Lestrade in it; HAIL CAB brought the free one and CLIMB INTO HANSOM CAB boarded it.

[Fact:cab_fares:Cab fares]
A hansom cab ride costs 2d for every five minutes of the journey, plus 2d: the journey times are in the table at #R$9B7F and the fare is worked out at #R$9B25. Confirmed in the emulator: Baker Street to Parliament Street took 20 minutes and the cabbie asked 'Right guv'nor that will be 10d.' Only the ten cab stands in the table can be reached; anywhere else gets 'Sorry guv'nor I cannot get there from'ere'.

[Fact:stale_quotes_cabbie:Quoted speech is queued - a cabbie can ignore a destination until old quotes are flushed]
Every quotation typed in a SAY TO is added to a list at #R$9FEC (#R$B049); the SAY that runs takes the FIRST node off it (#R$B86A). A SAY that fails before it gets that far (I SEE NO TRICIA FENDER THAT YOU CAN TALK TO, or a person who simply does not answer) leaves its quote behind, so every later SAY delivers the quote that was typed N commands earlier, N being the number of leftovers. Seen in the emulator: with 3-4 leftovers SAY TO CABBIE "GO TO KINGS CROSS ROAD" got 'You talk to the cabbie.' and nothing else, and the cabbie asked 'Well are we goin' anywhere guv'nor?' until the SAY had been typed once more than there were leftovers, when the cab set off at once (breakpoints showed shared action 14, GO TO, at #R$9AF6 was simply not reached before). The cure that works is TALK TO <anyone present> with no quotation, which empties the list. Earlier pages that said a cab ride 'takes 80 minutes' were wrong: that was my wait loop running on while the cab sat still.

[Fact:pay_not_give_money:Pay a cabbie with PAY 10D TO CABBIE, not GIVE MONEY TO CABBIE]
GIVE MONEY hands over the whole purse (thing 27) to the cabbie, who keeps it; after that INVENTORY shows no money, PAY says DON'T BE SILLY, and the cabbie nags 'What about me money guv'nor?'. PAY 10D TO CABBIE (#R$E370) takes the sum off the purse and the cabbie gives change ('You pay 10d. To the cabbie. The cabbie pays 4d. To you.'), and the purse stays with Holmes. A cab that has been paid goes away at once; an unpaid one waits for you.

[Fact:three_endings:How the case can end]
The game ends through records the characters' scripts run, all finishing at #R$9E74. Lestrade ends it at 3pm on Monday if you have not met him with Chief Constable Straker (#R$9E6A, confirmed by play); later he can end it with the morning papers' report of Mr Brown's lost plans - 'NOT ONLY HAVE YOU FAILED...' or 'YOU HAVE DONE EXTREMELY WELL...', depending on whether both murders have been proven (#R$9EA2); and with the Major's wrongful arrest (#R$9E85), which proving MAJOR PERCIVAL FFOULKES IS INNOCENT prevents. Phipps's script has Basil come in with a gun (#R$9E48): Watson saves you only if he and Lestrade are both there. #R$DFCA has Basil shoot you too. The 3pm ending is confirmed by play, and so is the Major's arrest: with the introduction poked in and nothing else done, Lestrade ends the game at about 1am on Tuesday ('Well Holmes that wraps up this case, sorry you were not of more help...'). The others are read from the code.

Confirmed in the emulator (tools/experiments/papers_test.py, with the 3pm and Major endings poked out of the way): at 10am on Thursday Lestrade's script ends the game with the morning papers - 'Not only have you failed to solve the crimes at hand...' or, with both murders marked proven, 'You have done extremely well to solve the case at hand but...'. Both lose. The win is Basil's entrance on platform one at Victoria with Watson and Lestrade there (#R$9E48, #R$6AF3: '...YOU TRULY DESERVE THE TITLE OF WORLDS GREATEST DETECTIVE.'), which needs the sale location proven first, and that in turn the two murders (see The case for how to earn the last marker).

UPDATE, 8 November night: every ending named here (3pm Monday, the Major's arrest at 1:04am Tuesday, the morning papers at 10am Thursday in both wordings, Basil's shots, Basil at Victoria with Lestrade missing; Watson missing with Lestrade present is end8: the same shot) was reached from a fresh boot by typed commands only; see the page 'Every ending, reached by typed commands'. The 3pm and Major endings are no longer 'poked' or 'read from the code' claims.

[Fact:meet_lestrade_by_3pm:Be introduced to Lestrade by 3pm on Monday, or the game ends]
Confirmed in the emulator: a player who only types WAIT from the start is told at 2:59pm on Monday 'I do not think you are doing too well, surely Sherlock Holmes could do a better job than you have done so far. Perhaps you should try again.' and the game restarts. Read from the code: the record that ends it (#R$9E6A, run from Lestrade's script) does nothing once #R$9D90 is set, and #R$9D90 is set by Lestrade's after-turn routine (#R$9DFF) the first time Chief Constable Straker and the player are both with him. The same flag tells the police men to stop turning people away ('I AM SORRY SIR BUT I HAVE MY ORDERS, NO ONE IS TO GET BY.').

[Fact:the_case_table:The solution is a table of statements and evidence]
The case lives at #R$9965: eleven statements of the form 'thing / relation / thing', each with the evidence it needs and a routine to run when it is proven, built from eleven verbs (BLACKMAILED, HAD, HAS, IS, KILLED, VISITED, HATED, EXAMINED, HID, OWNS, MADE): MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF, SALE LOCATION IS OLD MILL ROAD, PHIPPS HAS PLANS, TRICIA FENDER BLACKMAILED MRS BROWN and so on, plus four with no evidence at all and a flag (bit 5) whose effect is not fully traced, saying that Tricia Fender or Phipps killed one of the victims. The evidence is pairs of things the player must have seen or examined (bits 7 and 6 of each thing's +6, which the game sets as you look and EXAMINE - confirmed). Lestrade's 'WELL CAN YOU PROVE THAT ... YET, HOLMES ?' is driven by it. The format was read from the code that walks the table. Confirmed in the emulator (tools/experiments/case_test.py): with the player moved to Inspector Lestrade's room at the start, SAY TO LESTRADE "MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF" gets 'I do not believe that you have the evidence to prove that Mrs Brown killed herself' and the statement's flags go $A4 -> $A5 (bit 0: waiting). With the evidence poked in first - the female corpse and the sandstone bridge marked seen and examined (+6 bits 7 and 6), the large rock marked seen - the same words get 'So Mrs Brown killed herself, excellent Holmes, another brilliant conclusion.' and the flags go $A4 -> $A6 (bit 1: proven).

[Fact:watson_remembers:Watson remembers what you tell him about the case]
Telling Watson a statement about the case (SAY TO WATSON "MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF") adds it to a list of three-byte statements at #R$9FF2($9FF4), and he answers 'That is brilliant Holmes...'; something that is not about the case gets 'What'. Confirmed in the emulator. The statement is matched against the same table Lestrade uses (#R$9965); the verb must be one of eleven: BLACKMAILED, HAD, HAS, IS, KILLED, VISITED, HATED, EXAMINED, HID, OWNS, MADE.

[Fact:watson_connections:Watson's connections]
Tell Watson something about the case and he either praises you or, by chance, wonders aloud about 'some connection between' two things, picked from the statements you have told him (#R$BA67, #R$BD06). The pairing can come out oddly. In the emulator, at the start of the game: SAY TO WATSON "PHIPPS KILLED MRS BROWN" got 'That is brilliant Holmes, I do not know how you do it.'; "TRICIA FENDER KILLED MRS BROWN" got 'Well do you think there is some connection between Basil Phipps and Tricia Fender?'; and "MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF" got 'Well do you think there is some connection between Tricia Fender and Herself?'

[Fact:catch_the_major:To clear the Major, catch him at the opium den]
MAJOR PERCIVAL FFOULKES IS INNOCENT can only be proven through a second statement, MAJOR PERCIVAL FFOULKES VISITED OPIUM DEN, whose only evidence is an empty record that nothing but the Major's own script marks: after 11pm he may slip off to the opium den, and if the player is there with him he confesses ('WELL HOLMES YOU HAVE FOUND OUT MY SHAMEFUL SECRET...', #R$F0E2) and the evidence is set (#R$F103). Proving his innocence then stops the ending in which Lestrade arrests him (#R$9E85). The chain from the evidence onwards is confirmed in the emulator by setting the evidence bit and talking to Lestrade; CONFIRMED BY PLAY the same night, with no pokes (tools/experiments/playthrough/monday_to_major.cmds): the Major leaves his house at 11:03pm on Monday, goes through Sidmouth Street's heavy oak door, upstairs and back, hails a cab to Slater Street and goes through the hidden door into the den. The hidden door is narrower than Holmes (size 85 against 96, see #R$DD35), so the player has to wear the china man's disguise (size 80) to follow; standing in the den as the china man the Major says nothing, but the moment the disguise is TAKEN OFF inside he says 'WELL HOLMES YOU HAVE FOUND OUT MY SHAMEFUL SECRET...' (#R$996F becomes $0A). Out on Slater Street, SAY TO LESTRADE "MAJOR PERCIVAL FFOULKES VISITED OPIUM DEN" (Lestrade turns up there by himself; the statement may be said while still in disguise) gives 'Very good Holmes, I shall return to Leather head tomorrow...' and #R$9E87($9E88) = 1 at 12:14am on Tuesday - 46 minutes before the 1am ending at #R$9E85. Saying MAJOR PERCIVAL FFOULKES IS INNOCENT to Lestrade earlier (he was in Jones's library with the Major at 1:44pm) makes it wait, as the page The case says.

[Fact:tricia_is_mrs_jones:Tricia Fender is Mrs Jones in disguise - and that is how the last marker is earned]
Shared action 3 (#R$E1A3, in Tricia Fender's own action list) is not just a question. If you ask her about the bloodstained clothes while they are in the room (SAY TO TRICIA FENDER "TELL ME ABOUT BLOODSTAINED CLOTHES"), #R$E1B4 calls #R$E03A with A = $5F, which rewrites her actor-table entry from thing 13 to thing 95, Mrs Jones, moves her holder and room across, and sets the case marker #R$998F: 'Oh my god, you found out, I am really Mrs Jones. Tricia was blackmailing Mrs Brown...'. Mrs Jones is then an actor, so her own action list is used: SAY TO MRS JONES "TELL ME ABOUT YOUR ALIBI" (or WHAT HAPPENED, or the clothes) runs shared action 22 (#R$E17B, #R$E188) and sets #R$9993, the other marker MRS JONES KILLED TRICIA FENDER needs. Confirmed in the emulator on 8 November (evening) with Tricia and the clothes put in the player's room: #R$998F went 00 -> 02, the actor table lost 13 and gained 95, and #R$9993 went 08 -> 0A. Tricia herself is only on stage on Tuesday morning (room 46 at 8:05, room 47 at 8:10, back at 2:05pm; observed by waiting). CORRECTION: an earlier version of this project listed this as a bug ('Mrs Jones never acts, so the game cannot be won'). That was wrong; the actor table is changed while the game runs, so a table read at the start does not show her.

[Fact:blackmail_statement:TRICIA FENDER BLACKMAILED MRS BROWN does nothing but get a shrug]
Said to Lestrade in Scotland Yard on Tuesday after the other statements, he answers 'I do not see the importance of that Holmes.' and the only change in the case table (#R$9965) is that its record at #R$99F1 goes from $A4 to $A6 (proven); it has no evidence group and its routine (#R$9A0A) only marks itself done and sets a bit in the empty record that follows. It is not needed to win. Played: v12_clean_win_with_blackmail.cmds replaces the early, unnecessary sale-location attempt on Tuesday with this statement and still ends with the winning text. Putting it in as an extra line instead costs a minute, which shifts Lestrade's Wednesday cab so that the player and he board the same hansom cab (the fare is paid in garbage, 'Lestrade pays `4 19/8') and the run no longer wins: every minute counts in this route.

[Fact:early_sale_statement_unneeded:Saying THE SALE LOCATION IS OLD MILL ROAD on Tuesday afternoon is not needed]
The first attempt at 2:13pm on Tuesday (note not yet examined) only gets 'I do not believe...' and was in the route by habit. Replacing it with another statement, or removing it, still wins; the one that counts is said again on Wednesday at 10:53am after the singed note is examined.

[Fact:wall_safe_trap:Opening Basil's wall safe on Monday gets you shot]
The wall safe in Basil's library at Cobden Lane in Leatherhead (thing 89, room 58) holds the letters. Typing OPEN WALL SAFE there on Monday ends the game: 'Basil enters and says " Holmes, your interference is becoming annoying. " . Pulling a gun he shoots you.' (confirmed by play, 4:07pm). The check is the first record of shared action 21 (#R$DFB6): thing $59 and day 0 (Monday) lead on to the shooting message at #R$DFCC; any other day falls through to the ordinary OPEN. EXAMINE CLOSELY WALL SAFE is harmless. So the letters can only be taken from Tuesday on.

[Fact:large_rock_tuesday:The large rock - EXAMINE CLOSELY DEEP STREAM, on Tuesday, east of the bridge]
(Corrected by the next fact: Lestrade must be in the room too.) Read from the code: the record at #R$E0AD (shared action 16, EXAMINE CLOSELY the deep stream) does nothing unless the player is in room 88 (the east side of the sandstone bridge) AND it is Tuesday (day 1). Then it marks the large rock seen and visible, moves it into the player's room and prints 'AS YOU SEARCH THE RIVER LESTRADE COMES OVER TO HELP. HE STUMBLES AND SLIDES DOWN THE BANK AND NOTICES A GLEAM IN THE WATER. REACHING IN HE PULLS OUT A GUN TIED TO A LARGE ROCK'. The rock is seen, which is all MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF needs of it, so that statement cannot be proven on Monday. (The Monday visit to room 88, 12:40-12:58pm, left the rock hidden, as expected.)

[Fact:large_rock_needs_lestrade:The rock needs Lestrade in the room - Tuesday morning, east side of the bridge]
Corrects the fact on the rock above. Shared action 16's script runs #R$E0AD with Lestrade (actor 2) as direct object, and nothing happens unless he is in the room with the player. Played: arrive at the east side (room 88) by about 9:20am on Tuesday, wait for 'Inspector Lestrade enters', then EXAMINE CLOSELY DEEP STREAM; he finds the gun tied to a large rock. (The first examine, before he came in, printed only 'You see a deep stream.') He leaves at about 10:05am, and his return train is at 12:20pm. The train out to Leatherhead that reaches the station at about 9:00am is the one leaving Kings Cross platform four at 7:30am.

[Fact:disguise_needs_a_trick:The disguise cannot be picked up with the lamp - but WEAR then TAKE OFF carries it]
In Holmes's closet at the start, TAKE CHINA MANS DISGUISE answers 'I SEE NO CHINA MANS DISGUISE THAT YOU CAN TAKE' while the player carries the oil lamp and his money: the disguise (size 128) does not fit under the player's carrying limit. Dropping the lamp first lets it be taken (the capacity rule itself was not read; this is what the emulator did). WEAR CHINA MANS DISGUISE works straight from the room, though - it never has to be held - and TAKE OFF CHINA MANS DISGUISE afterwards leaves the disguise in the player's inventory next to the lamp and the money: 'You are carrying a china mans disguise, an oil lamp and your money.' Both were typed in the emulator. This is how the playthrough carried it to Slater Street (the lamp is needed in the dark). While worn, the player's actor is thing 1, the china man: LOOK says 'The china man is in...' and the harness lists the room as '0 0'. Watson follows as far as he fits; he stayed outside the hidden door.

[Fact:basils_london_house:Basil's Camden Street house - what was observed, and the wall in the way]
Observed with nobody interfering and in the playthrough: the front door (thing 91) and the bedroom window (thing 79) start locked. Phipps arrives by cab at 9:35am on Tuesday, opens the front door at 9:37 and goes in; the door is shut again at 9:40 and relocked. At 10:03pm 'Someone opens the window' - he is in the bedroom (room 35) from then on, hidden (his +5 bit 6 cleared), and the ripped coded note (room 38), the torn coded note (39) and the burning note (35) were all still in place at midnight (the script deletes the first two when it runs #R$EEC6 later that night, if both are still there; the burning note goes at 9am on Wednesday, #R$EF0C). Entering the bedroom through the open window while he is in it: 'Basil says " Holmes, your interference is becoming annoying. " . Pulling a gun he shoots you.' (tried at 12:01am). The front door's size is 32: with the door open (9:38am, straight after Phipps went through) NORTHEAST from Camden Street still says YOU CANNOT GO NORTHEAST - Holmes (96) and the china man (80) are both too big, only Lestrade (32) and the smaller characters fit. Neither door nor window can be unlocked by the player (UNLOCK FRONT DOOR: 'I SEE NO FRONT DOOR THAT YOU CAN UNLOCK'; UNLOCK ... WITH is not a verb). That is how it looked by walking in as Holmes. (Superseded: the untried Lestrade idea was never needed. The disguise and the window, below, work, and the whole thing was played without pokes.)

THE WAY IN, found with a hint from a published text solution (the SHERLOCK.TXT file in the World of Spectrum / ftp.icm.edu.pl game database; its page was not read in full) and then run in the emulator, no pokes: the shooting has two triggers. While Phipps counts down after opening the window (from 10:03pm on Tuesday for 55-75 minutes, #R$EEAF) he shoots only if the player's own actor, id 0, is in his room; the other trigger, #R$EFC7, shoots whoever is anywhere in rooms 33-39 but is only run after the countdown, an hour or more later. So: WEAR CHINA MANS DISGUISE in Basil's front yard right after 'Someone opens the window' (10:03pm), go NORTHEAST into the bedroom - Phipps is there and does nothing - through the large oak door to the hall, NORTHEAST to the passage, and the library (thick heavy door, southeast) and the yard (plain door northeast to the kitchen, back door northeast) are open. In the dark the lamp is carried. EXAMINE CLOSELY done as the china man only marks a thing SEEN (+6 bit 7, 'The china man closely examines...'); to get the EXAMINED bit (+6 bit 6) TAKE OFF the disguise first - in the library, away from Phipps, that is safe during the countdown. Done this way by 10:55pm: ripped coded note $C0 and torn coded note $40 (both examined), then WEAR again before re-entering the bedroom, SOUTHWEST out of the window at 11:07pm, still alive. The burning note is not in the fireplace at that time (TAKE BURNING NOTE: 'I SEE NO BURNT NOTE'); it is put there on Wednesday morning (#R$EF0C).

[Fact:the_game_goes_on:The game does not wait for you]
The player's script reads each line with a time limit (#R$D898, #R$A536: 3,000 keyboard polls). If nothing is typed in time the line is rubbed out and the player WAITs, so the clock and every other character carry on. Confirmed in the emulator: at a prompt with no key pressed the game prints 'You wait.' and the clock moves on by several minutes at a time. During WAIT UNTIL the main loop also looks at the keyboard every minute (#R$A07F) so that ENTER can cut the wait short.

[Fact:bedtime:The household goes to bed at 9pm]
At 9pm Daphne Strachan goes to her room and the cook and the gardener leave the game for the night, each marked SLEEPING and no longer counted as a person (so they cannot be talked to); they come back about eight and a half hours later. Their after-turn routines do it (#R$9EC1). Confirmed in the emulator for the evening; the morning return was read from the code.

[Fact:bring_a_lamp:It gets dark at 8pm]
Outdoors between 8pm and 6am the player cannot see without a light: LOOK on Baker Street at 9pm says 'SORRY IT IS TOO DARK.' unless the oil lamp from Holmes's room is being carried. The lamp is lit from the start (there is no verb to light it). Only the player and a few others need light at all (#R$D078). Confirmed in the emulator.

[Fact:cast_list:The cast and objects, read from the game's own records]
110 entities, named from their own word slots: you, Watson, Inspector Lestrade, Chief Constable Straker, Strachan, Phipps, Major Percival Ffoulkes, Tricia Fender, a gardener, cook and maid, two local policemen, a China man and an old man (each with a wearable disguise), a German agent, Mrs Brown and Mrs Jones, two hansom cabs and a police cab with their cabbies, eight steam train carriages, a female corpse with a crumpled note, a body on a bloodstained sofa, two wall safes, a torn coded note in a trash pile, a gun under a large rock, and a burnt note in a fireplace. Some entries are conversation topics rather than things (THE CASE, WHAT HAPPENED, YOUR ALIBI, YOUR ADDRESS).

[Fact:map_decoded_and_walked:The map - 106 rooms, 206 exits, every one tried]
The room table at #R$840B gives 106 rooms. tools/walkall.py puts the player in each room in turn, opens whatever is in the way, types each exit's direction and reads back where the player is: 194 of 206 exits go exactly where the table says. The other 12 are the ones the code says must refuse - 7 'scenery' exits to room 0 (a deep stream, a window) that the room description mentions but the exit finder skips, and 5 through something narrower than the player. See the Map page.

[Fact:police_men_are_doors:The police men are doors]
The two local police men at Browns and Jones front gates are listed in the room table as the 'door' of the exit they guard. The move check #R$DD35 treats them like any other passage: they count as open, and their size, 69, is smaller than the player's 96, so the player can't get past ('YOU CANNOT GO SOUTH'), though a smaller character could. Poking the player's size down to 8 lets the player straight past both.

[Fact:lestrade_hails_a_cab:Inspector Lestrade hails a cab every turn from the very start]
Logging every action attempt shows actor 2 - Inspector Lestrade, who starts in room 11 - trying HAIL on the hansom cab (object 30) and then GET INTO it, every turn from the first command. Several of this project's earlier conclusions were thrown off by his actions running on the same turns as the player's. UPDATED 2026-11-07: this is his plan script (#R$E7E7): DO WEST, then the sub-script at #R$EB72 (a cab, a walk and a train journey), then a conversation with Chief Constable Straker paced by SIGNAL and AWAIT.

[Fact:plan_scripts:Every character, the player included, runs a script]
Sherlock's characters are not driven by code but by scripts in a small language of fifteen opcodes (one unused): do a command, run an action record, order someone else to do something, say or print a message, signal or wait for another character, return from a sub-script, pass, and go to. 24 actors (the table at #R$9860) have a script; tools/plan.py decodes them and the disassembly labels every instruction. The player's own script (#R$9921) is the one that reads the keyboard: typing a command is just the player's plan step 'DO plan verb / plan dobj / plan iobj'.

Characters do exactly what the player does: a script's DO goes through the same command code (#R$C4D1): requirement checks, default records, object action lists and pre-checks as a typed command. That is why characters can fail to do things for the same reasons the player can.

The interpreter can also erase an instruction once it has succeeded (bit 5, #R$CF43), so that it never runs again - but no script uses that: none of the decoded instructions has bit 5 set. CORRECTED the same day: an earlier draft of this entry said the scripts edit themselves. What was confirmed in the emulator is the decoding itself: across 106 turns and 15,038 fetches, the interpreter fetched only instructions tools/plan.py decodes.

Characters really do co-ordinate through SIGNAL and AWAIT. In 84 turns of WAIT from the start the emulator saw 19 SIGNALs and 18 AWAITs that found their token - mostly Inspector Lestrade and Chief Constable Straker, whose scripts alternate SIGNAL and AWAIT around their conversation, and later Lestrade, the Major and a police man - and 13 ASK orders, nearly all given by Lestrade.

[Fact:waiting_for_the_hour:Characters keep appointments by failing until the time is right]
Verb #R$F1D6(136) succeeds only when the clock's hour equals its argument, counted 1-24: #R$F1D6 turns 13-24 into 1-12 with 'p', and 1-12 into 1-12 with 'a', and compares with the hour at #R$9FE0 and the letter at #R$9FE2. A script instruction 'DO 136 23' whose failure address is itself therefore means 'wait here until 11pm'. 23 instructions use it, in the scripts of Inspector Lestrade, Phipps, the Major and Tricia Fender.

Confirmed in the emulator over 110 turns of WAIT from the start: Tricia Fender (actor 13) asked verb #R$F1D6(136) for hour 1 every minute and was refused all Monday afternoon - at 1pm #R$9FE0 was 1 but #R$9FE2 was 'p', and she is waiting for 1am - and from 2:25pm the Major (actor 7) began asking for hour 23. No success was seen in that window, as the reading predicts.

[Fact:busy_characters:Characters can be too busy to do what you ask]
Bit 6 of a plan-script instruction marks it as busy: a character on such an instruction answers a request with 'Sorry, I cannot do that now.' (#R$CBA7). 181 of Inspector Lestrade's 223 instructions and 70 of Chief Constable Straker's 76 are busy; none of Watson's 32 is, so SAY TO WATSON "GET OUT" works at the start. Confirmed in the emulator by setting the bit on Watson's current instruction: he then refuses and stays in his armchair.

[Fact:thirteen_pictures:Only fourteen rooms have a picture]
The picture table at #R$F60C lists 14 of the game's rooms, and two of them (platforms one and two) share a picture, so there are 13 pictures in all, stored as drawing commands from #R$F638 to the very top of memory. Run in the emulator with the ROM loaded, the game's own interpreter draws every one of them in the top right quarter of the screen - see the Pictures page. An earlier version of this disassembly had two of them down as 'data, not decoded'.

[Fact:scenery_words:Scenery]
Rooms can mention things that are not objects - the 'two broad windows' of Holmes's sitting room, say. Each room record points to a list of such scenery words, and a command naming one gets 'YOU CANNOT TAKE THE WINDOW' instead of 'I DO NOT KNOW THE WORD' (#R$B6FD, #R$B6DA; confirmed in the emulator).

[Fact:examine_closely:EXAMINE CLOSELY finds hidden things that EXAMINE does not]
Verb #R$E135(20) is EXAMINE CLOSELY (in that word order: EXAMINE CLOSELY TRASH PILE; 'EXAMINE TRASH PILE CLOSELY' gets 'WHAT ?'). The trash pile's own EXAMINE (shared action 19, #R$E0E0) is a sub-script: run EXAMINE CLOSELY, then #R$E0EB, which sets the 'revealed' bit (+5 bit 7, #R$9C56) of the torn coded note. Confirmed in the emulator: in Basils Yard it answers 'You closely examine the trash pile.', takes 4 minutes, the note's +5 goes from $00 to $80, LOOK shows the note in the pile and TAKE NOTE works; plain EXAMINE takes 1 minute and reveals nothing. The drawer's sub-script (shared 17, #R$E0F2) does the same for the false bottom (object 63) - read from the code only.

[Fact:follow_climbs_in:FOLLOW WATSON puts you in Watson's armchair]
Confirmed in the emulator: FOLLOW WATSON at the start answers 'You climb into watsons armchair.', the player's container changes from 255 (none) to 24, and LOOK then says 'You are in watsons armchair with Watson.' FOLLOW's default record (#R$E452) is a sub-script: it runs #R$E488 on the person followed, which leaves its choice in the plan's verb register (#R$9FD6, written at #R$E491($E492)), then ENTERs or CLIMBs INTO that, giving up when a countdown of 10 runs out. Read from the script; only the outcome above was seen in the emulator.

[Fact:go_furiously:GO FURIOUSLY TO]
The verb table has two GO TOs: verb #R$9A99(29), GO TO, and verb #R$9ACB(30), GO FURIOUSLY TO (MADLY and QUICKLY are synonyms of FURIOUSLY). People who drive have both in their own action lists: the local police man sends GO TO to shared action 1 and GO FURIOUSLY TO to shared action 2, 20 minutes quicker; a cabbie sends them to shared actions 14 and 15, the furious ride 2 minutes quicker at three times the fare (6d a step instead of 2d). Lestrade himself says 'Furiously go to the old mill road'. It is what wins the game: see #R$E7E7.

[Fact:verb_system_decoded:The whole verb system, decoded from the game's own tables]
Typed commands are matched against 54 verb patterns at #R$8259; the verb number is simply the matched pattern's position. Each verb has an optional default action record (table at #R$9F38), and any object can override a verb through its own action list, pointing into a library of 24 shared actions (#R$AA71). Action records are a small data format: a flags byte, an optional pre-check routine, optional extra bytes, then executable code. Confirmed by checking about twenty typed commands' verb numbers against the decoded table, and by logging which record each real command ran.

[Fact:dictionary_front_coded:The dictionary stores each word as an edit of the previous one]
Each of the 592 words keeps some letters from the word before it and adds a few new 5-bit letters, with common endings (S, ES, ING, ED, LY...) taken from a 15-entry suffix table. 29 words are synonyms of others, including D/E/N/S/W and NE/NW/SE/SW for the directions, I for INVENTORY, L for LOOK, READ for EXAMINE and PICK for CARRY - and one profane synonym for KILL.

[Fact:synonyms:Synonyms in the dictionary]
Twenty-nine dictionary records stand for another word: their count byte has bit 7 set and two more bytes give the record to use instead, which the tokenizer follows (#R$DAAD). They are: BREAK = KILL, CALL = HAIL, CAREFULLY = CLOSELY, EVERYTHING = ALL, EXCEPT = BUT, MADLY and QUICKLY = FURIOUSLY, ME = YOU, MINE and MY = YOUR, PICK = CARRY, READ = EXAMINE, SAY = TALK, ST = STREET, the one- and two-letter compass points N, S, E, W, NE, NW, SE, SW, U, D, I = INVENTORY, L and LO = LOOK - and one rude word, which also means KILL. (IN is linked to VISITED, which makes it vanish: see the Bugs page.) Tried in the emulator: READ LAMP examines the oil lamp, PICK LAMP gives 'You carry the oil lamp.', I lists what you carry, L looks, and BREAK WATSON gets 'ONLY AN IDIOT WOULD TRY THAT.' Read with source/dictionary.py.

[Fact:abbreviations:Short words]
Commands can be abbreviated: the tokenizer (#R$D9C5, #R$DA7D) matches what you type against the start of each dictionary word. Tried in the emulator: EX LAMP, EXA LAMP and EXAMINE LAM all examine the oil lamp and INVE lists what you carry; EXAMINE LA, though, finds the label first, and a word with letters added - EXAMINATION - is not understood.

[Fact:object_flag_words:The game's own error words name the object flags]
When an action needs an object's flag to be set or clear and it is not, #R$CB7B prints 'THE <object> IS' and a word from one of two tables, one per flag bit: UNLOCKED/LOCKED (bit 0), EMPTY/FULL (bit 2), OFF/ON (bit 4), CLOSED/OPEN (bit 5) and DEAD (bit 6, the bit that makes a thing a person). So the tables are a key to the +5 flags. Confirmed in the emulator for OPEN and CLOSE: 'YOUR DOOR IS OPEN' and 'YOUR DOOR IS CLOSED'.

[Fact:quit_means_load:QUIT does not quit]
QUIT and LOAD share one handler in the parser's table of special words (#R$AF6B): both end the game the way a game-over message does and ask for a saved-game tape ('PLAY SAVED GAME TAPE TO BE LOADED'). Confirmed in the emulator. The same table holds SAVE, PAUSE, PRINT and NOPRINT (a copy to the ZX printer), ALL and ALL BUT, the quote mark that starts speech, and the pronouns.

[Fact:pounds_shillings_pence:The parser understands pounds, shillings and pence]
Typed sums of money are pre-decimal: £1, 3S, 6D, 2/6 or 1/- all work, and are converted to pence ((pounds x 20 + shillings) x 12 + pence, #R$DB67). Confirmed in the emulator: PAY 2/6 TO WATSON reads as 30 pence and the game answers 'You pay 2/6 to Watson.'; PAY 3S comes back as '3/-'. Times are read by the same code ('9:30AM').

[Fact:cursor_keys:One-key moves]
On an empty command line the cursor keys are compass moves: the line editor (#R$D978) turns the codes for left, right, down and up into W, E, S and N and presses ENTER for you, and GET-KEY (#R$C41F) makes DELETE count as left. Confirmed in the emulator by feeding the key codes: 'N. You go north. You are in a small closet...'

[Fact:repeat_key:'@' repeats the last command]
Typing '@' (SYMBOL SHIFT and 2) on an empty command line does the last command again: the line editor (#R$D99E) sets a flag that makes the word-list builder reuse the previous command's tokens instead of reading the line. Confirmed in the emulator: after NORTH into the closet, '@' gave 'YOU CANNOT GO NORTH.' With the one-key cursor moves (#R$D978), the game has two keyboard shortcuts that are easy to miss.

[Fact:boot_actor_table:The $9860 actor-table walk is reentrant, not boot-only]
First confirmed in the emulator simply as 'execution reaches this during boot' - true, but incomplete. Later testing (see world_model_notes.md) confirmed the same walk is genuinely re-entered on every turn, as part of per-actor turn processing, not solely at startup. The individual field meanings beyond what's confirmed in overlay_02_world_model.py (UPDATED 2026-11-07: +1/+2 is not a handler but the actor's position in its plan script; +3/+4/+5 are its plan registers - see the 'plan scripts' entry) are still open - this remains a starting anchor for object/room/character table discovery, not a finished account of it.

[Fact:actor_table_record_size:The actor table at $9860 has confirmed 8-byte records]
Confirmed two ways: (1) #R$CC0E's own search step advances by literally <code>LD DE,$0008; ADD IY,DE</code> between candidates, and (2) separately, the boot-sequence code and two other real writers found by watching actual memory writes during a room move (around #R$CE9F($CEA0)-#R$CEDE and #R$D01F($D020)-#R$D059) between them touch offsets +0 (id), +1/+2, +3/+4, +5, and +6/+7 of a record - i.e. every byte of an 8-byte record is accounted for as genuinely read or written by real code, not just assumed from the search step's stride. The individual meaning of fields +1 through +5 is not yet determined - see source/world_model_notes.md.

[Fact:action_dispatcher_found:Found the main per-turn action dispatcher (computed JP (HL))]
#R$CB2C saves IX/IY, jumps through HL via a plain <code>JP (HL)</code> at #R$CB40, then restores IX/IY. Confirmed real by finding its one genuine caller, #R$CA64, which turned out to be sitting in what looks like the main per-turn command-processing routine - immediately followed by a check (via the already-confirmed #R$D495 'get actor's room' accessor) for whether the player's room changed as a result. UPDATED (see the 'unifying several previously-separate threads' section of world_model_notes.md): what loads HL has since been fully traced - #R$CA64 is itself called once per candidate from the per-candidate walk this project found via #R$C9C0, and HL comes from that candidate's own record fields, not from a separate global dispatch table.

[Fact:verb_table_found:Found the table at $AA71 - but it's not simply 'verb number']
Direct-indexed (not searched): entry N sits at #R$AA71 + (N-1)*2, a plain 16-bit value. Confirmed real by checking that all of the first 20 entries are valid addresses inside the loaded game block, and that at least one (entry 1, #R$9A99) genuinely disassembles as real executable code rather than being a misread coincidence. CORRECTED after further testing: the index isn't simply 'which verb the player typed' - breakpointing confirms the exact same index (11) fires for three different verbs (LOOK, INVENTORY, TAKE) alike, but never for an unrecognised command, while OPEN additionally triggers a different index (6) three times first. See source/world_model_notes.md for the full account and the current best reading (a per-record 'primary action number', not a global verb id).

[Fact:object_id_catalog:Built a real object/actor id catalog via the visibility test]
Breakpointing #R$C821 (confirmed as an 'is this visible here' test) during known commands gives a stable, repeated id for things that are actually present: the oil lamp is id 22, 'your armchair' and 'watsons armchair' are ids 23 and 24, and Watson himself is id 3 - the last independently confirming a several-rounds-earlier guess from a completely different test (that actor id 3, whose room matches the player's, was 'very plausibly Watson'). Absent objects (a typed PIPE, not present) give up after a handful of hits with no stable id, which is itself part of the evidence this is a genuine presence test.

[Fact:dispatch_mechanisms_unified:Unified three previously-separate findings into one system]
#R$C9C0 (the per-candidate walk found early on), #R$CA64 (the per-turn command loop), and #R$CB2C (the computed-jump dispatcher) are confirmed to be one mechanism, not three: for each candidate in the per-candidate walk (the five RUN steps of the player's plan script at #R$9921 - CORRECTED, this was once taken for a fixed table at #R$9923($9927), then a command-specific entry, then a long shared tail), #R$CA64 is called with IY pointing at that candidate, and dispatches via #R$CB2C to a handler address read from the candidate's own record fields. Confirmed by breakpointing both #R$CA64 and #R$CB2C during a real TAKE LAMP: #R$CB2C's one caller is #R$CA64 (22 times); #R$CA64's one caller is #R$C99C, with IY taking exactly the sequence #R$C9C0 was already known to walk, including the command-specific id (#R$DCC1) this project's id catalog had independently found for the same command several rounds earlier. This also confirms #R$AA71/#R$C960 is a genuinely separate system from this dispatch loop - what it's actually for remains open.

[Fact:aa71_purpose_confirmed:Found what $AA71 is actually for - a shared action library]
Breakpointing #R$C960 (the #R$AA71 lookup) across boot, the intro, and ten real commands shows it fires selectively. (CORRECTED: this originally said 'for INVENTORY, DROP and OPEN'; logging the actor id later showed the INVENTORY and DROP hits were NPCs - actor 2 hailing a cab, actor 31 doing GO TO - acting on the same turn, not the player's command.) OPEN reliably produces index 6, whose entry (#R$DE9D) turns out to be a genuine, shared OPEN/CLOSE implementation (SET/RES bit 5 of a record's field +5), confirming what that bit means along the way. This corrects the 'verb dispatch table' framing from two rounds ago (already known to be wrong) with something more specific: #R$AA71 is a small library of generic action implementations that individual objects' own records can reference, used only when an object doesn't need fully custom behaviour. See source/world_model_notes.md for the full account.

[Fact:aa71_action_library_expanded:Mapped a real family of shared actions in $AA71's library]
Breakpointing #R$C960 across many commands, and logging which actor triggered each hit, confirms the player's own SIT IN, GET INTO, GET OUT OF and CLIMB OUT OF all reach shared actions 9 (get in/sit in) and 8 (get out) through each armchair's own action list. Those two are a matched pair built from the containment field (#R$C86E's field +1): getting into something sets your own 'container' to it; getting out sets it to whatever contains that thing. CORRECTED: an earlier version of this entry also listed action 11 as 'a generic fallback reached by INVENTORY, WAIT and STAND UP'. Those hits were actor 2 hailing a cab on the same turns; action 11 is HAIL.

[Fact:print_char_verified:PLOT-CHAR ($C388) recovers real game text without a ROM]
Confirmed in the emulator: setting a breakpoint on #R$C388 and reading A each time (rather than trying to render real pixels, which needs the ROM font this project doesn't have) reproduces legible game text directly, e.g. the opening scene: "Mon 08:00am ... The sun streams in the two broad windows in your sitting room...". This is the harness's main tool for message-rendering going forward (per message-rendering.md).

[Fact:messages_rendered:Every fixed message, rendered by the game itself]
The message printer at #R$A59F is a bytecode interpreter: dictionary word references, packed 5-bit letters, literal punctuation and 29 control codes. Rather than decode that by hand, each of the 212 messages found (in code, in the characters' plan scripts, as room descriptions, and by walking the message area) was rendered by running the interpreter in the emulator; 210 render completely. They include the parser's complaints, Watson's and Lestrade's lines ("SO MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF, EXCELLENT HOLMES, ANOTHER BRILLIANT CONCLUSION."), and story events ("AS YOU RUN YOUR HANDS OVER THE BOOKS YOU TOUCH A HIDDEN SPRING..."). One of the game's own spelling slips survives in the data: a messenger "DISSAPEARS DOWN THE STREET".

[Fact:unused_hang_opcode:Plan opcode 12 would hang the game]
The interpreter's test for opcode 12 is CP $0C / JR Z,#R$CD08 - and #R$CD08 is that JR Z itself, so with Z set it jumps to itself for ever. No script found uses opcode 12 (#R$CF03 still gives it a length of five bytes). Read from the code.

[Fact:saved_game_on_the_tape:A saved game on the original tape]
Besides the loading screen and the game, the tape carries a third block, a CODE file called 'SHD V2.0' for #R$840B-#R$A01A($A01F). That is exactly the header the game's own SAVE writes (#R$C0FE) and exactly the range a saved game holds (#R$B0EE: the rooms, things, case table, actors and variables). It is loaded first and then overwritten by the main block, so it does nothing - apparently a saved position left on the master tape. See loader_notes.md.

[Fact:always_checked_table_found:CORRECTED - the 5 'checked every turn' entries are the player's plan script]
An earlier round described #R$9923($9927) as 'five 5-byte records ($99,$13,<16-bit addr>,<flag byte>)'. That reading was four bytes out of step. The bytes are the player's own plan script (actor 0's script pointer in the actor table at #R$9860 is #R$9921): the '$99' is the high byte of a failure address, and '$13' is the next RUN instruction's opcode (RUN, with a failure address). The five addresses that every command visits (#R$D715, #R$D769, #R$D77E, #R$D7D9, #R$D7FA) are the five RUN targets of that script, which reads a line, parses it, and then does the parsed command. The live trace that found them was right; only the format laid over the bytes was wrong. See the 'plan scripts' entry.

[Fact:verb_number_hypothesis_double_checked:The 'OPEN = verb 6' coincidence did not hold up - correction]
While tracing the table above, one of the five commands tested (OPEN) happened to produce a command-specific address that matched the #R$AA71 table's entry #6 exactly. Before treating that as confirmation, checked whether the other four commands' own command-specific addresses appeared *anywhere* in the first 400 entries of the same table - none did. A single coincidental match out of five, with no supporting pattern from the other four, is not evidence; recorded here specifically so this near-miss doesn't get treated as confirmed in a future session. The actual source of the command-specific and shared-tail entries seen in that trace is still unidentified.

[Fact:npc_turn_processing_confirmed:The actor-table walk is per-turn NPC processing, not boot-only]
A real correction to earlier work in this project: the #R$9860 actor-table walk documented in overlay_01_boot.py as part of the game's boot sequence is genuinely re-entered on every turn (confirmed by breakpointing deep inside it during an ordinary command well after boot and seeing it re-walk the same table). Traced the mechanism as far as #R$CC37/#R$CC78/#R$CC0E/#R$CC29/#R$D045: for each actor, resolve its full state record, compare its room to the player's, and read its script position out of the record's +1/+2 (UPDATED 2026-11-07: a plan script, not a handler - see the 'plan scripts' entry) - a real, coherent NPC-behaviour system. See source/world_model_notes.md for the full chain and what is still unconfirmed (chiefly: how the main loop re-enters the walk in the first place, and what #R$B91F/#R$C821/#R$D008 do).

[Fact:room_text_path_resolved:Resolved - the room-text print path, and a false alarm explained]
Earlier in this project, breakpointing #R$C2BA during a real LOOK command got zero hits, and was written up honestly as an unresolved worry. That was a bug in the test, not a real property of the game: re-run cleanly, the same breakpoint produces 170 real hits with a real, traceable caller chain back through a print gatekeeper at #R$C1E1 (found via two independent confirmed call targets, resolving an address this project had previously only guessed at from a misaligned disassembly window). The full confirmed pipeline: #R$C1E1 (gatekeeper) -> case-folding/quote logic -> #R$C2BA (CR handling) -> #R$C388 (PLOT-CHAR). See source/world_model_notes.md for the complete account, including exactly what the original test bug was.

[Fact:b91f_is_setup_only:CORRECTED - $B91F runs every turn, for every actor]
An earlier round reported #R$B91F as intro-only: 25 hits during the intro, none during LOOK. Re-tested on 2026-11-07 with every breakpoint stepped over: LOOK 19 hits, WAIT 67, TAKE LAMP 18, and 0 only for a command that takes no time (N when there is no exit). It looks for an order or a remark waiting for each actor whose turn has come (see #R$B91F). The old test's 'scripted-looking' id order was the actor table's order.

[Fact:dictionary_matching_confirmed:Corrected - the original dictionary-matching path WAS the real one]
Earlier in this project, a test of the #R$DA7D comparison routine and the #R$A94D word-expansion routine both appeared to show this wasn't the code path real typed commands use (zero hits, garbled output). That was caused by forgetting to step over a breakpoint on the tokenizer's own entry point before tracing onward - the same class of bug this project has hit more than once elsewhere (see harness_notes.md). Fixed and re-tested: typing TAKE produces exactly the originally-expected call sequence (#R$DA34 -> #R$A94D -> #R$DA7D), and the comparison routine shows the typed buffer 'ake' matching a real, dictionary-expanded candidate 'akeS' (almost certainly TAKES) by prefix - real, verified confirmation of both the stem+suffix storage format and the abbreviation-matching comparison logic this project originally read from the code, then wrongly set aside. See source/dictionary_notes.md for the full account.

[Fact:dictionary_to_verb_number_corrected:CORRECTION - 'TAKE is verb #5, traced to handler' does not hold up]
A previous round claimed to have traced TAKE all the way from typed input to a confirmed handler at #R$AA71's entry #5 (#R$E003). That claim was checked properly this round and falsified: breakpointing #R$E003 directly during a real, successful <code>TAKE LAMP</code> (output confirmed: 'You take the oil lamp.') produced zero hits, while a known-real dispatch point (#R$CB2C) fired 22 times for the same command. What genuinely was confirmed and still stands: the tokenizer correctly resolves 'TAKE' to class $C0 (verb) and word-index 5. What was never actually tested, despite being written as if it was: that this index feeds #R$AA71's table, or that #R$AA71's entry 5 is ever executed. See source/dictionary_notes.md for the full account, including how this differs from this project's other corrections (a false positive from an untested step, not a false negative from a buggy test).

[Fact:get_actor_room_confirmed:$D495 confirmed as 'get actor A's current room number']
Confirmed empirically: called with A=0 while the player was in the sitting room, got $08 back; moved the player north into the small closet in the real, running game and called it again - got $09; moved back south - got $08 again. The underlying record format (17 bytes, resolved through a 3-byte id->pointer index table at #R$8B6F) is also confirmed by dumping four consecutive real records - see source/world_model_notes.md.

[Page:loader-notes]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(loader-notes-content)

[loader-notes-content]
<h1>Loader notes (Phase 0)</h1>
<h2>Tape structure</h2>
<p><code>Sherlock_v2_0.tzx</code> contains 5 header+data block pairs after the
archive-info/hardware-type blocks:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Block</th>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Header load addr</th>
<th>Length</th>
<th>Header end</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>BASIC loader</td>
<td>0</td>
<td>-</td>
<td>23</td>
<td>-</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>"sherlock"</td>
<td>3</td>
<td>#R$7FF9($8000)</td>
<td>256</td>
<td>#R$8100</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>"SHD V2.0"</td>
<td>3</td>
<td>#R$840B</td>
<td>7188</td>
<td>#R$A01A($A01F)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>"s" (screen)</td>
<td>3</td>
<td>#R$7FF9($8000) (header says)</td>
<td>6912</td>
<td>#R$9AFF($9B00) (per header)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>"p" (main)</td>
<td>3</td>
<td>#R$5D80</td>
<td>41600</td>
<td>$10000</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><code>tzx_tools.py</code> (the skill's own script) originally failed on this tape:
it doesn't handle TZX block ID <code>0x33</code> ("hardware type", informational
only). Patched to skip it; every block's checksum then verifies clean.</p>
<h2>The BASIC loader is not <code>RANDOMIZE USR n</code></h2>
<p>It's <code>LOAD "" CODE: PRINT USR 32768</code> (tokens <code>EF 22 22 AF 3A F5 C0</code>,
followed by the digits "32768"). This just loads the 256-byte "sherlock"
stub and calls it - the stub does everything else itself.</p>
<h2>The 256-byte stub (#R$7FF9($8000), relocates to #LINK(rom_symbols#a5B00)($5B00))</h2>
<p>Disassembling it (<code>z80dasm -a -g 32768 stub.bin</code>) shows:</p>
<pre><code>DI; LD SP,#R$5D80; LD HL,#R$7FF9($8000); LD DE,#LINK(rom_symbols#a5B00)($5B00); LD BC,$0100; LDIR
JP $5B12          ; continue running from the *copy*, not the original
</code></pre>
<p>It copies itself down to #LINK(rom_symbols#a5B00)($5B00) and jumps into the copy at offset
$12 ($5B12), which then does:</p>
<pre><code>CALL $5B30   ; load next tape block using ITS OWN header's addr/length
CALL $5B62   ; load next tape block using a HARDCODED addr/length
CALL $5B30   ; load next tape block using its own header's addr/length
JP   #R$A040   ; jump into the game
</code></pre>
<p>$5B62's hardcoded values are <code>IX=#LINK(rom_symbols#a4000)($4000), DE=$1B00</code> - the real Spectrum
display+attribute file address and size (6912 bytes) - even though the
"s" block's own header claims load address #R$7FF9($8000). <strong>The loader
overrides the header's address for this one block.</strong> This is the
loading screen, forced onto the actual screen instead of wherever its
header nominally says.</p>
<p>$5B8B (not fully traced) is a thin wrapper around the ROM tape-loading
routine (#LINK(rom_symbols#a0556)($0556)), jumping into it partway ($0562, skipping the pilot-tone
/ flag-byte-compare setup the caller already did itself) - a standard
Spectrum "custom loader built on top of ROM primitives" pattern.</p>
<h2>"SHD V2.0" is loaded then completely overwritten - verified</h2>
<p>Call order is: load "SHD V2.0" (own header: #R$840B, 7188 bytes, ending
#R$A01A($A01F)) - load "s" (forced to #LINK(rom_symbols#a4000)($4000)) - load "p" (own header: #R$5D80,
41600 bytes, ending exactly at $10000).</p>
<p>#R$840B-#R$A01A($A01F) is entirely inside #R$5D80-#R$FFFF. Since "p" loads <em>after</em>
"SHD", and nothing runs between the three loads (no CALL/RET, straight
into the next load), "SHD"'s content is completely overwritten before
the game ever executes a single instruction of it.</p>
<p>Verified directly (not just argued from addresses) by simulating the
exact byte-level loads in Python and diffing: the final memory image at
the entry point #R$A040 only contains sensible-looking Z80 code when "p"'s
bytes are applied; with only "SHD" loaded (as if "p" never came along),
the same bytes are all zero (SHD's own data doesn't reach that far into
its own block, or more likely, #R$A040 sits at an offset within "p"'s data
that never got covered by SHD in the first place - either way, the
<em>current</em> game only ever runs on "p"'s bytes for this whole region).</p>
<p><strong>Not yet explained</strong>: <em>why</em> "SHD V2.0" is on the tape at all if it's
always fully clobbered before use. Two plausible, unconfirmed
explanations: (a) anti-piracy chaff aimed at naive tape-backup tools, or
(b) a leftover build artifact from an earlier revision (the "V2.0" in
its own name is suggestive - maybe an earlier loader used to load <em>this</em>
block as the real game, and version 2 replaced it with "p" but left the
tape-authoring step of dropping "SHD" untouched). Flagged as
<strong>not verified</strong>, not stated as fact anywhere else in this project.</p>
<h2>Final memory layout when the game starts</h2>
<pre><code>#LINK(rom_symbols#a4000)($4000)-$57FF  display file (loading screen artwork, then overwritten by gameplay)
#LINK(rom_symbols#a5800)($5800)-$5AFF  attribute file
#LINK(rom_symbols#a5B00)($5B00)-$5BFF  relocated loader stub (dead once the game starts)
#R$5D80-#R$FFFF  &quot;p&quot; block: all game code + data. Entry point #R$A040.
</code></pre>
<p>The game's own init code (<code>#R$A040: ... LD SP,#R$5D80</code>) reuses the freed
loader/stub workspace (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5B00)($5B00)-$5D7F) as its stack, growing downward from
#R$5D80 - a clean, deliberate reuse of memory the loader no longer needs.</p>

[Page:harness-notes]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(harness-notes-content)

[harness-notes-content]
<h1>Harness notes (Phase 1)</h1>
<h2>Why a custom hook, not raw port emulation</h2>
<p>Grepping the whole 41,600-byte "p" block for <code>IN A,($FE)</code> (<code>DB FE</code>) and
<code>IN A,(C)</code> (<code>ED 40/48/50/58/60/68/78</code>) with port $FE found <strong>zero</strong>
matches. This game does not poll the keyboard port directly at all.</p>
<p>Instead it relies on a real Spectrum's ROM interrupt handler to scan the
keyboard into the standard system variables (FLAGS #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3B)($5C3B), LAST-K #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C08)($5C08)),
and has its own routine, GET-KEY at #R$C41F, that does <code>EI; HALT; DI</code> to
let exactly one such interrupt happen, then reads those variables. Found
by grepping for literal little-endian references to #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3B)($5C3B) and #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C08)($5C08) and
finding a tight, sensible cluster of both within a few bytes of each
other (see the disassembly in overlay_01_boot.py's #R$C41F block).</p>
<p>Since this sandbox has no real 48K ROM loaded, there is no interrupt
handler to actually populate those system variables. Rather than try to
emulate that whole path, <code>scripts/harness.py</code> sets a breakpoint directly
on GET-KEY's entry (#R$C41F) and simulates its entire contract in Python,
then jumps straight back to the caller - exactly the "hook the read-key
routine as a breakpoint" approach <code>emulator-harness.md</code> describes, just
applied at a CALL/RET boundary instead of a polling loop.</p>
<h2>Two real bugs, caught only by running it</h2>
<p><strong>Bug 1: wrong entry address.</strong> The first attempt used #R$C426 as GET-KEY's
entry (misreading the disassembly - #R$C426 is <code>LD IY,#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3A)($5C3A)</code>, a few bytes
<em>into</em> the routine, not its start). The real entry, confirmed by
grepping for <code>CALL $c41f</code> and finding three genuine <code>CD 1F C4</code> call
sites, is #R$C41F (the <code>PUSH IY</code> that begins the register-save sequence).
Using the wrong address meant we intercepted execution <em>after</em> the
routine's own prologue had already run, so our "pop the return address
off the stack" logic popped a value that was never a return address,
and the game reliably wandered off into unmapped memory a few
instructions later. Caught by single-stepping and noticing PC end up
executing zeroed/unmapped memory - not by re-reading the disassembly
more carefully.</p>
<p><strong>Bug 2: missing flags.</strong> After fixing the entry address, the game still
misbehaved: it would spin printing nothing but space characters forever.
Bypassing GET-KEY skips its real final instruction (<code>XOR A</code> for "no key",
<code>SET 7,A</code> for "key available") - which sets CPU flags the <em>caller</em>
almost certainly branches on immediately after the <code>CALL</code>. Our bypass
set the <code>A</code> register correctly but left <code>F</code> untouched from whatever ran
<em>before</em> the call, so the caller's very next conditional jump could go
the wrong way. Fixed by computing the flags a plain <code>OR A</code> on the
returned value would produce (exactly matches <code>XOR A</code>'s flags for the
A=0 case, and is the standard assumption for the A=ascii|$80 case) and
setting <code>self.m.f</code> explicitly before returning. This exact class of bug
- "the bypass returns the right value but not the right side effects" -
is precisely why <code>verification.md</code> says to test the actual behaviour
rather than trust a plausible-looking shortcut.</p>
<p><strong>Bug 3 (harness-only, not a game bug): forgetting to step over a
breakpoint.</strong> Adding a second breakpoint at PLOT-CHAR (#R$C388, see below)
to capture printed text, the very first attempt just read <code>A</code> at the
breakpoint and moved on - without calling <code>step_over_breakpoint()</code>.
Per this emulator's own documented breakpoint semantics ("a breakpoint
fires on the attempt to execute the marked instruction, before it makes
any progress"), simply calling <code>run()</code> again re-traps on the <em>same</em>
instruction with no progress at all. The symptom looked identical to a
real infinite loop (billions of T-states, output frozen on repeated
space characters) until a single-tick trace showed PC genuinely stuck
at one address forever - a good example of <code>verification.md</code>'s
"distinguish test-harness artefacts from real bugs" advice applying to
harness <em>construction</em>, not just to interpreting a finished run.</p>
<h2>PLOT-CHAR (#R$C388) as a message-rendering hook</h2>
<p>Found by write-marking the display file (<code>mark_addrs</code> + <code>WRITE_MARK</code>)
during a scripted run and histogramming which PC was live at each write.
Two huge, uniform-sized clusters (6144 and 767/768 writes) were an
obvious CLS. A later run (after feeding a few ENTERs) showed four
dominant call-sites in the tens of thousands, all inside one small
region (#R$C27E($C280)-#R$C3E3); disassembling it found PLOT-CHAR at #R$C388, which
computes its source glyph address as <code>A*8 + $3C00</code> - exactly the real
ROM font table layout (space, code 32, maps to $3D00). That confirms A
holds a literal ASCII code at the moment this runs, regardless of
whether real glyph pixels are available to draw (they aren't, since
there's no ROM loaded here - printed characters render as blank space on
our simulated screen, but the <em>character codes</em> going through this
routine are exactly what a real player would see printed).</p>
<p>Hooking this (read A, <code>step_over_breakpoint()</code>, continue) reliably
recovers legible text, confirmed against the real, known opening scene
of <em>Sherlock Holmes: Riddle of the Crown Jewels</em> - e.g. Watson's line
"Good morning Holmes." and the sitting-room description - which is
strong independent confirmation this hook is capturing the real thing
and not an artefact.</p>
<h2>Known current limitation: SAVE/LOAD</h2>
<p>Sending the <code>SAVE</code> command reliably wedges the harness: the real game
calls into the ROM's tape-save routine (#LINK(rom_symbols#a04C2)($04C2), confirmed as a genuine,
repeated CALL target - see the raw CALL-target frequency scan in this
project's exploration notes), which we don't hook, so execution runs
into unmapped/zeroed memory and never returns. Once this happens the
rest of that playthrough is unusable and must be restarted from a fresh
<code>Game()</code> instance. Exploring SAVE/LOAD properly would need a third hook,
on #LINK(rom_symbols#a04C2)($04C2) (and probably #LINK(rom_symbols#a0556)($0556), the load routine), simulating "instant
success" the same way GET-KEY is bypassed. Not yet implemented.</p>
<h2>Coverage-tracking caveat</h2>
<p><code>Game(track_pc_coverage=True)</code> marks the "p" block's own address range
for read-tracking and records every address read - which includes data
operand reads, not just instruction fetches (the underlying emulator API
has no distinct "this was an opcode fetch" signal). This means some
addresses in the resulting map are pure data that happened to be read
by nearby code (e.g. a byte of message text the printer walked past),
not real instruction boundaries. <code>sna2ctl.py</code>'s own algorithm is
tolerant of this (it decodes one instruction from each marked address
and merges contiguous runs; spurious single entries show up as tiny,
awkward "code" blocks rather than corrupting the whole disassembly), but
this is exactly the "never trust the automatic split as final" caveat
<code>SKILL.md</code> warns about, and it applies with extra force here.</p>
<p>One more caveat specific to this hooking style: because GET-KEY is
bypassed via a direct PC redirect rather than executed and
<code>step_over_breakpoint()</code>-stepped, its own bytes never pass through the
tracked read callback and don't appear in the coverage map on their own
- they were added back into the map by hand once independently
confirmed by disassembly (see <code>build.py</code>'s use of <code>coverage_combined</code>).</p>
<h2>A real bug in build.py, caught by the round-trip check doing its job</h2>
<p>Adding the confirmed #R$E03A overlay entry produced a skool file that
failed to round-trip (<code>skool2bin.py</code> warned about an instruction
overlap, and the rebuilt binary genuinely differed from the original).
Cause: the baseline <code>sna2ctl.py</code> guess had independently placed a data-
block boundary at #R$E03B($E03D), which sits in the middle of a real 3-byte
<code>LD A,(#R$A032($A036))</code> instruction starting at #R$E03B (now confirmed real).
<code>build.py</code>'s merge logic only forced boundaries at the overlay's own
declared addresses; it had no way to know a <em>stale</em> baseline boundary
a few bytes further in was now wrong, so both boundaries ended up in
the merged ctl, and <code>sna2skool.py</code> produced instructions that
overlapped on disk.</p>
<p>Fixed by having <code>build.py</code> remove any baseline boundary that falls
strictly between two consecutive addresses in a block's own confirmed
comment keys (which are, by construction, real instruction starts -
this project's own address cross-checker already guarantees that).
This is exactly the kind of mistake the round-trip check exists to
catch: it's cheap, it runs after every batch of changes, and it caught
a real, binary-breaking bug the moment it was introduced rather than
letting it sit unnoticed in a growing control file.</p>
<h2>Session 2026-11-05: pictures, teleporting, and walking every exit</h2>
<p><strong>Pictures are skipped.</strong> Entering some rooms (the entrance hallway,
room 6, is the first one you reach) draws a location picture: #R$A277
calls the picture interpreter at #R$F3C2, which plots through the 48K
ROM (PIXEL-ADD at #LINK(rom_symbols#a22AA)($22AA), called from #R$F5FE). The harness has no ROM, so
the call ran off into empty memory and wrecked the game state (the
player's location ended up as 23). <code>harness.py</code> now sets a breakpoint
at #R$F3C2 (<code>PICTURE_ENTRY</code>) and returns straight to the caller
(<code>Game._skip_picture</code>, counted in <code>Game.pictures</code>); <code>tools/play.py</code> and
<code>render_messages.py</code> do the same. The picture code only draws, so
nothing the game does afterwards depends on it. Confirmed: the walk
your room -&gt; hall -&gt; entrance hallway -&gt; Baker Street -&gt; back now runs
cleanly, with "[picture skipped]" on the two rooms that have one.</p>
<p><strong>Teleporting the player</strong> means setting three things, not two: the
player's location (#R$8CC2($8CC9)), the displayed room (#R$A00A), and the
last-room-entered pair #R$DD01 (room) / #R$DCFF (record address). The move
action writes #R$DD01/#R$DCFF for <em>any</em> actor that moves, and after the
boot sequence they hold room 16 (platform one, Aldergate station),
because someone moved there during the first turns (the file has 0).
Without them, the next description started "You are at platform one
of Aldergate station" before the real room.</p>
<p><strong><code>tools/walkall.py</code></strong> uses that to try every exit in the room table:
206 exits, 194 move the player where the table says; the other 12 are
refused exactly as #R$DCD2/#R$DD35 predict, and the 5 of those with a real
destination let the player through once the player's size (+2) is
poked from 96 down to 8. Results: <code>source/exits_checked.json</code>.</p>
<h2>2026-11-07: why a key sometimes disappears after WAIT UNTIL</h2>
<p>While the player is waiting until a time (plan verb #R$DDD0(50)), the main loop
polls the keyboard once a minute (#R$A07F) so that ENTER can cut the wait
short. The harness answers that poll from its queue like any other key
read, so the first character of the next command can be consumed there.
That is very probably the old "W eaten, I DO NOT KNOW THE WORD 'AIT'"
oddity, read from the code and not re-tested. Queue the next
command only after the wait has ended, or expect to lose one character.
With an empty queue the harness answers "no key", which also drives the
input time-out (#R$A536): the player WAITs after 3,000 empty polls.</p>
<h2>2026-11-07 (evening): pictures can be drawn after all</h2>
<p>The harness skips #R$F3C2 because the game image has no ROM. SkoolKit ships
the 48K ROM (<code>skoolkit/resources/48.rom</code>); <code>tools/draw_pictures.py</code> loads
it at $0000, calls #R$F3C2 directly with HL = a picture and a sentinel
return address, and saves the screen. Every picture returns within a
fraction of a second of host time. Note for z80.Z80Machine: give run()
a generous total budget - the fills are slow in emulated time.</p>
<h2>2026-11-07 (evening): teleporting leaves carried things behind</h2>
<p>Moving the player by poking memory (the room at #R$8CC2($8CC9), #R$A00A, #R$DCFF-
#R$DD01) does not update the room (+15) of anything the player carries.
Code that asks "is it here?" (#R$C821) then thinks a carried lamp is still
in the old room - the first darkness test failed that way. Walk the
player when carried things matter (tools/experiments/dark_test.py).</p>
<h2>Playing the game by typed commands (8 November, night)</h2>
<ul>
<li><code>tools/experiments/replay_log.py CMDFILE [N]</code> replays a list of typed
  commands from a fresh boot; <code>tools/experiments/replay_verbose.py CMDFILE
  OUT</code> does the same and writes every reply (truncated, the last few in
  full) plus the clock and room after each command. A line
  <code>(until TEXT) CMD</code> repeats CMD until TEXT appears in the reply, <strong>at most
  80 times</strong>: a wait that needs more (a long ride, WAIT = 5 minutes each)
  must be split over two lines, and TEXT must be a single word that the
  80-column wrap cannot split.</li>
<li>The game is deterministic, so the same command file always ends in the
  same state; 444 lines take under a minute to replay on an idle machine
  (the first replay took sixteen minutes only because another job ran
  alongside it).</li>
<li>Never run a blind wait loop after telling a cabbie where to go until
  the cab has actually moved (see the Bugs page, 'A failed SAY TO leaves
  its quotation behind').</li>
<li>A winning command file is easy to spoil when stitched together out of
  snapshot logs: a log that was cloned from a snapshot also carries the
  commands of the branch it was cloned from, including dead ends (one
  such stretch ended in Phipps's 9:50am shot and had to be cut out).</li>
</ul>
<h2>Shortening a command file (minimize_cmds.py)</h2>
<p><code>tools/experiments/minimize_cmds.py IN OUT</code> removes lines whose reply was an
error and plain LOOK/INVENTORY lines, keeping each removal only if the
whole file still wins. The check must be the winning <em>text</em>: the same room
and clock (Victoria platform one, 6:43pm) also come with the death ending
when Lestrade is left behind, and a first version that checked only room and
time produced a 370-line file that lost. Every minute counts in this route
(one line fewer or more can put Lestrade and the player in the same cab or
on the wrong train), so a candidate removal is never safe without a replay.</p>

[Page:dictionary-notes]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(dictionary-notes-content)

[dictionary-notes-content]
<h1>Dictionary/vocabulary notes (Phase 3, partial)</h1>
<h2>What's solidly verified</h2>
<p><strong>The per-letter index table lives at #R$5D80</strong> - i.e. right at the very
start of the "p" block, which <code>sna2ctl.py</code>'s automatic execution trace
had guessed was <em>code</em> (it isn't reachable from any keypress-driven
playthrough, so it never got marked, and its raw bytes disassemble as
nonsense - <code>NOP</code>, garbage <code>LD</code> instructions, a stray <code>DEFB $DD</code> - exactly
the "garbage from decoding data as code" smell <code>discovery.md</code> warns
about). This is corrected in <code>game.ctl</code>/<code>game.skool</code> were we had time to
splice it in; <strong>treat the very start of the block as data, not code,
generally</strong>, until the whole extent of this table is properly bounded
and marked.</p>
<p>Found by tracing the real tokenizer (starts around #R$D9C5, called while
parsing typed input): the very first thing it does with the current
word's first letter is</p>
<pre><code>LD A,(HL)      ; first letter of the word, plain ASCII (e.g. 'n' = $6E)
AND $1F        ; low 5 bits: for any letter, this gives 1-26 (A=1..Z=26)
                ; directly from ASCII, no separate pack/unpack needed
RLCA           ; x2 (each table entry is a 2-byte pointer)
LD L,A
LD H,$00
LD BC,#R$5D80    ; the table's base
ADD HL,BC
LD E,(HL)
INC HL
LD D,(HL)      ; DE = a 16-bit value, 0 if this letter has no entries
</code></pre>
<p>Confirmed by decoding all 26 letters' entries this way and finding they
are <strong>clean and monotonically increasing</strong> (<code>a</code> -&gt; offset $0001, <code>b</code> -&gt;
$00A7, ... <code>z</code> -&gt; $09F5), exactly what you'd expect from an
alphabetically-organised dictionary where each letter's words are stored
contiguously. This alone is strong confirmation the table location and
formula are both right, independent of fully cracking the entry format
below.</p>
<p>The offsets are relative to a second base, <strong>#R$5DBE($5DBF)</strong> (confirmed by the
<code>LD HL,#R$5DBE($5DBF); ADD HL,DE</code> immediately following, in three separate places
in the tokenizer/comparison code) - so a letter's first word record sits
at <code>#R$5DBE($5DBF) + offset</code>.</p>
<p><strong>A stemming/suffix table lives at #R$A85C</strong>, 16 entries x 4 bytes,
selected by the low nibble of a word-record's first byte (<code>AND $0F</code>;
value <code>$0F</code> means "no suffix"; otherwise <code>RLCA;RLCA</code> (x4) indexes into
this table). The first 9 entries spell out real English inflections,
confirmed just by looking at the bytes as ASCII - no interpretation
needed:</p>
<pre><code>0: &quot;S&quot;        3: &quot;\x08IES&quot;   6: &quot;ED&quot;
1: &quot;ES&quot;       4: &quot;D&quot;         7: &quot;\x08ING&quot;
2: &quot;IES&quot;      5: &quot;ING&quot;       8: &quot;LY&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>\x08</code> (backspace) entries are a neat trick: "\x08ING" means "delete
the stem's last letter, then append ING" - i.e. how a stem like <code>hop</code>
(silent-e or consonant-doubling aside) or <code>mov</code> becomes <code>moving</code> rather
than <code>moveing</code>. This table almost certainly serves <strong>both</strong> the parser
(matching a typed inflected form like "examining" against a stored stem
"examine") <strong>and</strong> the message printer (grammatically correct verb
endings in printed text, e.g. "Watson takes the pipe" vs "you take the
pipe") - <code>message-rendering.md</code> specifically calls out dynamic suffix
handling as something worth watching for, and this looks like exactly
that mechanism, shared by both directions.</p>
<p>Entries 9-15 of that table do <strong>not</strong> look like plain text (<code>^#~#</code>,
mostly high-bit-set garbage) - either the meaningful range really is
only 0-8 and 9-15 are something else entirely (a different table
happening to sit right after, or non-suffix flag combinations that are
rare enough this project didn't happen to trigger them), or there's a
wrinkle in the indexing not yet accounted for. <strong>Not resolved</strong> - stated
here rather than glossed over.</p>
<h2>What's not yet resolved</h2>
<p><strong>SUPERSEDED - see "MAJOR CORRECTION" below.</strong> Everything in this
section turned out to be caused by bugs in the tests used to check it,
not by a real architectural difference. Left in place as the record of
what was tried and why it looked, at the time, like a real dead end.</p>
<p>Tried to call the game's own word-expansion routine directly (found at
#R$A94D, which chains #R$A95B - copy literal/synonym-flagged letters into a
scratch buffer at #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C42)($5C42) - then #R$A92F - append the suffix from the table
above) to reconstruct spelled-out dictionary words programmatically,
the same way <code>message-rendering.md</code> recommends for message text. This
did <strong>not</strong> produce clean results: most calls returned an empty string,
and a few produced text-looking-but-wrong fragments. Likely causes,
untested:</p>
<ul>
<li>The routine may need additional registers/state set up by its real
  caller that a raw "jump straight to #R$A94D" call skips (unlike GET-KEY,
  which turned out to be safely callable in isolation, this one may not
  be).</li>
<li>The per-letter table's entries may not be a simple flat list of N
  words terminated by a zero word - watched real gameplay (typing
  "TAKE" and breakpointing the comparison routine at #R$DA7D) and got
  <strong>zero hits at all</strong>, and a write-watch on the #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C42)($5C42) scratch buffer
  during the same real parse also saw <strong>zero writes</strong> - meaning the
  code path actually used to recognise "TAKE" during real play is
  <em>not</em> the one traced statically here. There is more than one
  word-lookup mechanism in this engine, or the one traced is used for
  something else (abbreviation/synonym resolution specifically, rather
  than the everyday case), and the everyday path hasn't been found yet.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Next step for anyone continuing this</strong>: repeat the "breakpoint the
comparison, log what actually gets hit" approach that worked well
elsewhere in this project (see <code>harness_notes.md</code>), but starting from
the <em>tokenizer's entry point</em> (#R$D9C5) itself while parsing a real
command, rather than assuming which downstream routine matters and
breakpointing that. Log every CALL target visited between #R$D9C5 being
entered and the tokenizer returning a word-class byte - that will show
the <em>real</em> control-flow graph for word lookup, rather than the
plausible-but-apparently-wrong one written up above for the synonym/
abbreviation-comparison machinery.</p>
<h2>MAJOR CORRECTION: the original dictionary-matching hypothesis was right</h2>
<p>Did exactly the suggested next step - and it reversed the "ruled out"
conclusion above. That earlier test had the <em>same</em> bug this project
has now hit more than once: forgetting to <code>step_over_breakpoint()</code> on
the entry breakpoint itself before single-stepping onward, which
leaves the emulator stuck re-trapping on the same instruction forever
without executing anything. Once fixed, single-stepping from #R$D9C5
while typing <code>TAKE</code> produces exactly the call sequence this project
originally read statically and then wrongly abandoned:</p>
<pre><code>#R$D9D4 -&gt; CALL #R$DAC6   (special-character check)
#R$D9D9 -&gt; CALL #R$DA34   (the confirmed per-letter dictionary index lookup)
#R$D9F5 -&gt; CALL #R$A94D   (word expansion - stem + suffix)
  #R$A950 -&gt; CALL #R$A95B
  #R$A97C -&gt; CALL #R$A990  (x4)
  #R$A954 -&gt; CALL #R$A92F  (append the suffix)
#R$D9FF -&gt; CALL #R$DA7D   (the comparison routine)
</code></pre>
<p>...repeating for each candidate word under the same first letter.
Breakpointing #R$DA7D directly (this time with the bug fixed) and
logging both buffers on each hit, while typing <code>TAKE</code>, gives:</p>
<pre><code>candidate @ #R$6646: typed=&quot;ake&quot;  expanded=&quot;able&quot;   (mismatch: k vs b)
candidate @ #R$664A: typed=&quot;ake&quot;  expanded=&quot;akeS&quot;   (prefix match!)
candidate @ #R$664E: typed=&quot;ake&quot;  expanded=&quot;alkS&quot;   (mismatch: k vs l)
</code></pre>
<p><code>typed</code> is "ake", not "take" - exactly as expected, since the per-letter
index lookup (#R$DA34, confirmed since early in this project) has
already narrowed the search to 't'-words using the first letter, so
only the <em>remaining</em> letters need comparing. The dictionary's own
stored data expands to "able", "akeS", "alkS" for three neighbouring
't'-words - almost certainly TABLE, TAKES, and TALKS - confirming, with
real bytes rather than a plausible story, that:</p>
<ul>
<li>the dictionary really does store <strong>stems, not whole words</strong> (a <code>t</code>
  is implicit from the index lookup; what's left, "ake", is stored and
  expanded, not "take" in full)</li>
<li>the <strong>suffix-expansion mechanism genuinely works</strong> (#R$A94D and its
  sub-calls correctly reconstruct "akeS" from a stem plus a suffix
  table entry) - the earlier standalone test of this same routine
  (see the very first section of this file) that produced empty or
  garbled strings was calling it in isolation without the setup its
  real caller provides, not a sign the routine itself is broken</li>
<li>"ake" against "akeS" is exactly the <strong>abbreviation/prefix matching</strong>
  this project's very first reading of #R$DA7D predicted (comparing only
  up to the shorter length) - confirmed with real data, not asserted
  from reading the comparison loop alone</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>This reverses the "not resolved" framing earlier in this file.</strong> The
original, very first pass at this dictionary (the per-letter index
table, the suffix table, the #R$DA7D comparison and synonym-chain logic)
was correct. What was actually wrong was the <em>test</em> used at the time to
check it, not the architecture itself - a useful reminder, in the same
spirit as the harness bugs documented in <code>harness_notes.md</code>, that a
disconfirming result deserves the same scrutiny as a confirming one
before being written up as settled.</p>
<p><strong>Still open</strong>: exactly which candidate wins (does the "akeS" match
get accepted, and does the game then correctly land on "TAKE" as the
recognised verb, stripping the trailing "S" it doesn't need?), and how
a verb id/number - the kind this project separately found feeding the
#R$AA71 table - gets produced from a successful dictionary match. That
connection (dictionary entry -&gt; verb number) is the one piece of the
"single most valuable link" this project has been chasing all session
that is <em>still</em> not directly observed, even now.</p>
<h2>Follow-up: the dictionary-to-verb-number link is confirmed - TAKE is verb #R$DD03(5)</h2>
<p>Traced the tokenizer's return value all the way to its caller (#R$D6CE,
which does <code>LD HL,#LINK(rom_symbols#a5B00)($5B00); CALL #R$D9C5; CP $D0; JP Z,...; CP $C0; JR
NZ,-10</code> - i.e. call the tokenizer, and retry if the class byte isn't a
recognised noun ($D0) or verb ($C0)). Typing <code>TAKE</code> alone and tracing
through this retry loop directly, three calls happen in sequence
before it succeeds:</p>
<pre><code>call 1: returns D=$30, E=$05
call 2: returns D=$B0, E=$05
call 3: returns D=$C0, E=$05   &lt;- accepted (matches the caller's CP $C0)
</code></pre>
<p><strong>E stays fixed at 5 across all three calls</strong> - only D (the
class byte) changes. Read as: the tokenizer resolves "TAKE" to word
index 5 immediately, but offers it under a <em>sequence</em> of possible
classes (an unexplained $30, then $B0 - the punctuation-class value
this project found much earlier - then $C0, verb) until the caller's
own <code>CP</code> tests accept one. <strong>Confirmed real, not just plausible</strong>: the
#R$AA71 table's entry #5 (<code>#R$AA71 + (5-1)*2</code>) resolves to #R$E003, and
disassembling it (from a couple of bytes in, past what looks like the
same kind of small header verb #R$DD03(1)'s entry had) shows genuinely coherent
"take an object" logic:</p>
<pre><code>#R$E008: CALL #R$E03A      ; (with A=0 first, A=1 on a second path)
#R$E00B: LD IX,(#R$A012($A013))
#R$E00F: LD (IX+1),0
#R$E013: LD A,(#R$A00A)
#R$E016: LD (IX+$0F),A   ; ($0F is the confirmed room-number field!)
#R$E019: OR 1; RET
</code></pre>
<p>and <code>#R$E03A</code> itself:</p>
<pre><code>#R$E03A: LD C,A                    ; C = 0 or 1, from the caller
       LD A,(#R$A032($A036))              ; A036 = the target object's id
       CALL #R$CC0E                ; find its record (confirmed primitive)
       LD (IY+0),C               ; overwrite field +0 with 0 or 1
       LD IY,(#R$A012($A017))
       LD A,C
       CALL #R$D237                ; re-resolve via the confirmed chain
       LD (#R$A012($A017)),IX
</code></pre>
<p>Read together: taking an object finds its compact record (via the
already-confirmed #R$CC0E), marks it (field +0 becomes 0 or 1 - very
plausibly "held by the player" vs some other state, not yet certain
which), and updates the object's <em>full</em> state record's room field
(offset +$0F, the same room-number field this project confirmed for
actors) to the player's current room (<code>#R$A00A</code>) - i.e. "this object is
now wherever I am", exactly what taking something should do.</p>
<p><strong>This confirms</strong>: TAKE is verb #5 in the #R$AA71 table, the dictionary-
match-to-verb-number link is real (word index 5, carried in E, is
what the #R$AA71 table is indexed by - not literally "verb number" in
the sense of a single global mapping, but the specific per-class word
index the tokenizer returns), and the verb's handler code performs
sensible, coherent object-taking logic using entirely already-confirmed
building blocks from elsewhere in this project. This is about as solid
a capstone as a single session's testing can give without also
independently confirming what field +0 being 0 vs 1 means (a "held by
player" vs "held by someone else" guess, not yet tested).</p>
<p><strong>Not yet confirmed</strong>: what the $30/$B0 class-attempt sequence
before $C0 actually represents (candidate classes tried in a fixed
order? something about "TAKE" specifically having multiple possible
parses?), and what field +0 being set to 0 vs 1 precisely means.</p>
<h2>MAJOR CORRECTION: #R$E003 is never actually reached - the above was wrong</h2>
<p>Went to verify the "0 vs 1" question from the previous section by
breakpointing #R$E003 directly during a real, successful <code>TAKE LAMP</code>.
<strong>Zero hits.</strong> Tried again with a much larger patience budget, with
bare <code>TAKE</code> (the exact command the previous section's trace used),
and with several other candidate addresses at once - all zero hits at
#R$E003, every time. Confirmed the test methodology itself was sound by
checking real output was captured correctly alongside it (<code>You take
the oil lamp.</code> - the command genuinely succeeds) and that a real,
alternative dispatch point (#R$CB2C, the computed-jump mechanism this
project found several rounds earlier) <em>does</em> fire, 22 times, for the
exact same command.</p>
<p><strong>This means the previous section's central claim - "TAKE is verb #5,
traced from typed input to handler" - does not hold up.</strong> What was
actually confirmed there: the tokenizer genuinely resolves "TAKE" to
class $C0 (verb) and word-index 5 (real, re-verified, still stands).
What was <strong>not</strong> actually confirmed, despite being written as if it
was: that word-index 5 is used to look up #R$AA71's table, or that
#R$AA71's entry 5 (#R$E003) is ever executed. That step was inferred from
"the table entry's address happens to contain code that reads like
sensible TAKE logic" - a coincidence of plausible-looking bytes, not
a traced execution path. This is exactly the mistake <code>verification.md</code>
warns about most: a coherent, satisfying story that was never actually
run.</p>
<p><strong>What real testing shows instead</strong>: the actual per-turn dispatch for
a full command (verb + noun both resolved) goes entirely through the
already-confirmed #R$CB2C/#R$CB40 computed-jump mechanism, landing on a
long sequence of addresses mostly in the $Dxxx-$Fxxx range (<code>#R$D6CE</code>
itself among them - the very noun/verb classification loop being
called <em>as one of the dispatch targets</em>, suggesting parsing and
per-candidate action-checking are unified into one generic "try this"
loop, not separate phases) - none of which is #R$E003, and none of
which obviously corresponds to #R$AA71's table by address either.</p>
<p><strong>Corrected picture</strong>: the dictionary-to-verb-number link (word -&gt;
class + index) is real and confirmed. What that index actually gets
used for is genuinely still open - possibly the #R$AA71 table serves an
entirely different purpose (a message lookup? a canonical-name
lookup for printing "I don't know the verb X"-style errors, given
this project separately confirmed such error messages exist?) than
"the verb's own action handler", which was an assumption, not a
finding. The real per-turn action dispatch mechanism (#R$CB2C, confirmed
real since several rounds ago) is a separate, still not fully mapped,
system.</p>
<p><strong>A note on how this happened, for anyone reading this project's
notes as a case study</strong>: the error was not a broken test producing a
false negative (the pattern behind several of this project's other
corrections) - it was a false positive, reached by <em>not testing a
step at all</em> and describing it with the same confident language as
the steps that genuinely were tested. The lesson, stated plainly: a
plausible-looking coincidence (an address computed one way lining up
with code that reads sensibly) is not the same evidence as a traced
execution path, and both this file and the overlay should be read
with that distinction in mind for every claim, not just this one.</p>

[Page:world-model-notes]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(world-model-notes-content)

[world-model-notes-content]
<h1>World-model notes (Phase 3, partial)</h1>
<h2>The actor table at #R$9860 - confirmed by cross-reference</h2>
<p><code>discovery.md</code> step 5 warns not to trust a table's meaning from
proximity alone, but to confirm by finding <em>every</em> real reference to it
and checking they make sense together. Done here:</p>
<p>Four genuine <code>CALL</code>s to #R$CC0E exist in the whole block (#R$CC3D, #R$DDA3,
#R$E03E, #R$E300 - confirmed via a literal-bytes search for <code>CD 0E CC</code>, not
guessed from context). #R$CC0E itself is:</p>
<pre><code>LD DE,$0008
LD IY,#R$9860
loop:
  LD A,(IY+0)
  CP C            ; C holds the actor id being searched for
  JR Z, found
  CP $FF          ; end-of-table sentinel
  JR Z, not_found
  ADD IY,DE       ; next record (8 bytes along)
  JR loop
</code></pre>
<p>This is a plain <strong>"find the actor whose id is C" linear search</strong> over a
sentinel-terminated array of 8-byte records starting at #R$9860 - textbook
"generic N-byte table, sentinel-terminated" per <code>discovery.md</code>, and now
independently confirmed (not just inferred from the boot-sequence code
that first led here, which only ever walked the table without an
explicit search key).</p>
<p>The boot-sequence code (see <code>overlay_01_boot.py</code>, #R$A040) separately
confirmed: field <code>+0</code> is the id/sentinel byte, and fields <code>+6</code>/<code>+7</code> hold
a 16-bit value that gets set from a stored value at #R$9FDD when a
specific actor (id $32) is present and the player pressed ENTER at a
particular boot prompt. #R$B0AC (one of the other real users of #R$9860,
found while cross-referencing) does something structurally identical:
<code>LD IX,#R$9860; LD (IX+6),L; LD (IX+7),H</code> after computing a value in HL -
strong confirmation fields +6/+7 are a genuine, reused per-actor 16-bit
value (a position? a target room? not yet determined which), set by
more than one place in the code using the same offsets.</p>
<p><strong>Not yet determined</strong>: what fields +1 through +5 hold, how many
records exist in total, or what the 16-bit value at +6/+7 actually
represents (a location is the obvious guess for an adventure game's
character table, but not confirmed - the boot-sequence code sets it from
a saved value at #R$9FDD without printing or otherwise revealing its
meaning in anything tested so far).</p>
<h2>A per-room "action list" work area at #R$9800($9806)? (leads, not confirmed)</h2>
<p>Diffing the whole "p" block's writable memory before and after walking
from the sitting room into the closet (<code>NORTH</code>, after opening the plain
door) showed 175 changed bytes. Most interesting: a block starting at
#R$9800($9806) that is <strong>all zero in the static tape image</strong> (confirmed - dumped
the raw file bytes there, all <code>$00</code>) but gets filled with structured-
looking data at runtime:</p>
<pre><code>0a 13 98 27 4b f2 15 03 01 ff
0a 1d 98 26 32 f2 1a 04 01 ff
0a 2c 98 25 3b f2 1e 04 01 ff
...
</code></pre>
<p>Read as little-endian words, several of these contain values in the
$98xx range (plausibly more pointers into the same page as the actor
table) and the $F2xx range (plausibly code addresses - $F2xx is inside
this project's loaded "p" block, so a value there could genuinely be a
handler routine's address, matching discovery.md's "object's own list
of action handlers" pattern), each entry apparently ending in an <code>$FF</code>
byte in the same position - consistent with, but <strong>not confirmed as</strong>,
a sentinel-terminated list of the kind <code>discovery.md</code> describes for a
room or object's own action handlers.</p>
<p><strong>Not yet done</strong>: found no literal <code>LD DE,#R$9800($9806)</code> (or <code>LD HL,#R$9800($9806)</code>)
anywhere in the block, so the destination address for whatever writes
this data is computed at runtime rather than being a fixed immediate -
this needs a write-watch (<code>mark_addrs</code> + <code>WRITE_MARK</code>, exactly as used
successfully elsewhere in this project - see <code>harness_notes.md</code>) on the
#R$9800-#R$9860 range specifically during a room transition, to catch the
<em>copying</em> code in the act rather than just its result. Flagged here as
the concrete next step rather than guessed at further.</p>
<h2>Follow-up: the write-watch was done - extends (and complicates) the picture</h2>
<p>Ran the suggested write-watch during a real <code>NORTH</code> movement. The real
writers are small accessor routines around #R$CE9F($CEA0)-#R$CEDE and
#R$D01F($D020)-#R$D059, not one bulk copy - e.g. #R$D02F-#R$D044 sets <code>(IX+3)</code>,
<code>(IX+4)</code>, <code>(IX+6)</code> or <code>(IY+3)</code>, <code>(IY+4)</code>, <code>(IY+5)</code> depending on which
branch runs, and #R$CEAF/#R$CEB2 sets <code>(IY+1)</code>, <code>(IY+2)</code>. Put together with
the boot-code-confirmed <code>(IX+6)/(IX+7)</code> and the id at <code>+0</code>, that
accounts for bytes 0 through 7 of the record - the full 8 bytes #R$CC0E's
search step already confirmed as the record size - but <strong>the exact
meaning of fields +1/+2, +3/+4, and +5 is not established</strong>, only that
they're genuinely read and written as part of moving between rooms.</p>
<p>Also found, one level further in: #R$D045 reads the very <em>first</em> actor
record's id byte (<code>LD A,(#R$9860)</code> - record 0, presumably the player) and
calls #R$D495 with it, which itself calls #R$D237 and then, if a type check
passes (<code>CP (IX+0)</code> against 1), reads <strong><code>(IX+$0F)</code></strong> - offset 15, past
the end of an 8-byte actor record. That means #R$D237 resolves the id into
a pointer into a <strong>different, larger-record table</strong> - very plausibly
the room table itself (a room record with at least 16 bytes would fit
<code>discovery.md</code>'s "fixed header + variable trailing data" pattern much
better than the compact 8-byte actor record). This was the most
promising lead in this project - chased it properly below.</p>
<h2>Follow-up: chased the #R$D237/#R$D495 lead - confirmed empirically</h2>
<p>Disassembled #R$D237 and its own helper #R$D34E. The full chain turns out
to be:</p>
<pre><code>#R$D045: LD A,(#R$9860)     ; the FIRST actor record's own id byte
       CALL #R$D495
       LD (#R$A002($A009)),A     ; stash the result
#R$D495: CP $FF; RET Z             ; invalid id -&gt; $FF
       CALL #R$D237                ; resolve id -&gt; a record pointer (in IX)
       LD A,$01; CP (IX+0)       ; that record's own type tag must be 1
       LD A,$FF; RET NZ          ; if not, fail
       LD A,(IX+$0F); RET        ; else: return byte at offset +15
#R$D237: LD IX,#R$8B6F               ; base of a 3-byte-record index table
       CALL #R$D34E                ; find the record whose id matches A
       ... (register-juggling that nets IX = that record's own stored
            16-bit pointer, i.e. one level of indirection)
#R$D34E: EXX; LD E,3                ; 3-byte records, sentinel-search
       ... walks from #R$8B6F: id byte, then a 16-bit pointer, $FF = end
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Confirmed empirically</strong>, not just by reading: called #R$D495 directly
(isolated, via the <code>call_routine</code> pattern from <code>emulator-harness.md</code>)
with A=0 while the player was in the sitting room - got back <code>$08</code>.
Moved the player north into the small closet in the real, running game,
called it again - got back <code>$09</code>. Moved back south - got <code>$08</code> again.
<strong>This is confirmed to be "get the current room number of actor A".</strong></p>
<p>Went further: called <code>#R$D237</code> directly for ids 0-3 and dumped the full
records it resolves to. All four are 17 bytes, all start with the type
tag <code>$01</code>, and are laid out <strong>back-to-back</strong> (0x8cba, 0x8ccb, 0x8cdc,
0x8ced - each exactly 17 bytes after the last, confirming the record
size independently of the earlier 8-byte <em>index</em> table's stride).
Offset +15 is confirmed as the room number for all four (id 0 and id 3
both read $08 - very plausibly Holmes and Watson, both starting in the
sitting room together, matching the real opening scene where Watson
sits right there in his own armchair; id 2 reads $0A, a room not yet
visited but presumably a real one elsewhere in the game; id 1 reads
$00, and unlike the other three its offset +7/+8 field also doesn't
look like a valid pointer into loaded memory - id 1 may be an unused or
differently-shaped record, not confirmed either way).</p>
<p><strong>Not yet determined</strong>: what offsets +1 through +6, +9/+10, and +16 of
this 17-byte record hold. Offset +7/+8 looks like a pointer (falls
inside the loaded "p" block for ids 0, 2, 3) but attempting to read it
as message/name text didn't produce anything ASCII-recognisable -
either it points at something else (a sub-record, not text), or
whatever it points at needs its own expansion step first (plausible,
given the dictionary's own words need expansion too - see
<code>dictionary_notes.md</code>). Flagged as a lead, not a conclusion.</p>
<h2>Follow-up: found (and ruled out) a real message-printing loop</h2>
<p>While hunting for how room descriptions get from storage to screen,
found a second, completely different, genuinely real routine: a loop at
#R$C1CB that reads a byte, masks off bit 7, prints it via a gated entry
point at #R$C1FD, and stops when it hits a byte that <strong>had</strong> bit 7 set
(i.e., messages as <strong>plain ASCII, terminated by setting the top bit of
the last character</strong> - no bytecode at all, for whatever uses this).
Confirmed as real code by finding all 4 of its genuine callers (literal
<code>CALL #R$C1CB</code> bytes at #R$C070, #R$C09C, #R$C0EA, #R$C1C0).</p>
<p><strong>Ruled out</strong> as the room-description path specifically: breakpointed
#R$C1CB directly during a real <code>LOOK</code> command and got <strong>zero hits</strong> -
whatever prints room text doesn't go through this loop. Also tried
breakpointing #R$C1FD (the gated print-entry this loop calls) and its
own only found caller (#R$D944) turned out to call it a mere 4 times
with no visible output - consistent with #R$C1FD's very first check
(<code>LD A,(#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3C)($5C3C)); AND A; JR NZ,...; RET</code>) reading a flag that's never
zero in this project's unbooted-ROM sandbox, so this whole gated path
silently no-ops here regardless of what it's really for (a "printer
echo" toggle is a reasonable guess, given #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3C)($5C3C) sits in what would be
Spectrum ROM system-variable territory on real hardware - not
confirmed).</p>
<p>Also tried breakpointing #R$C2BA (the CR-handling print wrapper
<code>overlay_01_boot.py</code> already documents from earlier in this project)
directly during a real <code>LOOK</code>, hoping to catch its caller via the
return address on the stack the same way - this got zero hits too, and
suspiciously also suppressed the PLOT-CHAR hits that reliably fire in
every other test in this project. Something about that specific
breakpoint address interacts badly with this test (or #R$C2BA's
<code>overlay_01_boot.py</code> description may need re-checking - the block was
originally identified from a disassembly window that turned out to
have an alignment problem elsewhere in this project, see
<code>harness_notes.md</code>'s GET-KEY mix-up for a similar mistake). <strong>Not
resolved. Flagged rather than left silently unstated.</strong> The reliable,
still-standing fact is simply that PLOT-CHAR (#R$C388) itself is
definitely used for room text (confirmed many times over in this
project); exactly what calls it for room text specifically remains
open.</p>
<p><strong>RESOLVED further down this file</strong> ("the room-text print path is
resolved - and a false alarm explained"): this zero-hit result was a
bug in that specific test, not a real property of #R$C2BA. Re-run
cleanly, the same breakpoint produces 170 real hits during a single
<code>LOOK</code>. Left in place here, rather than deleted, as the record of what
was tried and why it looked - wrongly - like a dead end at the time.</p>
<p><strong>Concrete next step for anyone continuing this</strong>: rather than guessing
which wrapper matters and breakpointing that, breakpoint PLOT-CHAR
(#R$C388) itself (already known-reliable) during a <code>LOOK</code> command and read
the <em>whole call stack</em>, not just the immediate return address - peek at
several words up the stack from SP, since the true "print this room's
text" driver is probably 2-3 call-levels above PLOT-CHAR's immediate
caller, not the immediate caller itself.</p>
<h2>Follow-up: did the whole-call-stack trace - narrowed further, still open</h2>
<p>Fixed a real bug in the first attempt at this (the test loop broke out
the instant the typed command's input queue drained, before the room
text - which prints <em>after</em> input is consumed - had a chance to appear
at all; needed the same idle-detection loop <code>Game.run()</code> already uses).
With that fixed, sampled the full stack (12 words up from SP) at
several consecutive PLOT-CHAR hits partway into a real <code>LOOK</code> response.</p>
<p>The stack was <strong>identical across five consecutive space characters</strong>,
which is itself useful confirmation: whatever's several frames up isn't
changing per-character, so it's a genuine outer "print this whole
message" frame, not part of the tight inner glyph-plotting loop.
Disassembled the most promising stable address (#R$A2A8, a return address
into a small "print N blank lines" utility, #R$A296) and traced one level
further to #R$A9B7 - which turned out to be a <strong>shared, flag-gated
low-level output primitive</strong>: if a mode flag at #R$A022($A027) is set, it
appends the character into a growing buffer at a position tracked at
#R$A9B5 (handling space/CR/quote/backspace specially) instead of drawing
it - this is genuinely the same buffer-building mechanism used earlier
in this project while tracing dictionary word comparison (<code>#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C0B)($5C0B)</code>, see
<code>dictionary_notes.md</code>); if the flag is clear, it presumably falls
through to real screen output elsewhere (unexplored, past this
disassembly's window).</p>
<p>So: <strong>not the room-text driver itself</strong>, but a real, useful
clarification - #R$A9B7 is one shared low-level primitive doing double
duty for both keyboard-echo buffering and general character output,
gated by the same #R$A022($A027) flag documented in <code>dictionary_notes.md</code> and
seen earlier controlling PR-CHAR-MAIN's behaviour too. The actual
"here is a whole room description, print it" driver is still higher up
the call stack than this - the #R$DC7D/#R$DC84/#R$CB37 addresses seen in the
same stack dumps are the next honest lead (not yet examined).</p>
<h2>Follow-up: found the main per-turn dispatch and room-change logic</h2>
<p>Chased #R$CB37 next and it paid off - found a genuine <strong>computed-jump
action dispatcher</strong>:</p>
<pre><code>#R$CB2C: LD IX,(#R$CB28)   ; save/restore IX and IY around the jump -
       LD IY,(#R$CB2A)   ; whatever gets dispatched to is trusted to
       CALL #R$CB40       ; clobber them, apparently
       LD (#R$CB28),IX
       LD (#R$CB2A),IY
       RET
#R$CB40: JP (HL)          ; the actual computed jump - HL must be set by
                         ; the (one, confirmed real) caller before this
</code></pre>
<p>Its one real caller (#R$CA64, confirmed the same way as everywhere else
in this project - grepping for the literal <code>CD 2C CB</code> bytes) sits in
what looks like the main per-turn command-execution routine:</p>
<pre><code>#R$CA64: ... (set up default position variables at #R$9FD7/#R$9FD8,
            falling back to #R$A032($A034)/#R$A032($A035) if unset/$FF)
       CALL #R$CB2C           ; dispatch to whatever handles the parsed
                             ; action - this is presumably where a
                             ; verb's own handler routine runs
       CALL #R$CA7F           ; then check: has the player's room changed?
       [if #R$A00A($A00C) is still 0] CALL #R$CAAF
</code></pre>
<p>#R$CA7F is a clean, small, self-contained check: get actor 0's (the
player's) current room via the now-confirmed #R$D237/#R$D495 chain, and
compare it against #R$A002($A009) (the value #R$D045 stashes there earlier, in
<code>overlay_01_boot.py</code>'s neighbourhood) - i.e. <strong>"is the room now
different from what we last recorded"</strong>.</p>
<p>#R$CAAF, called when that's true, does several further comparisons (the
player's room against a <em>different</em> cached byte at #R$C491; another
actor's room, read via <code>#R$A032($A036)</code>, against the player's; and, in one
branch, walks a sentinel/count-terminated array of the same 17-byte
entity records - counted by <code>(IX+0)</code> as a loop counter this time,
rather than searched by id - looking for one whose room, offset +15
again, matches the player's current room). That last part in
particular looks like exactly the mechanism that would answer "what
else is in this room with the player" for a room description's "you
can see..." line - <strong>a real, promising lead, not yet fully confirmed</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Not yet determined</strong>: the exact contents of <code>#R$C491</code> (a second,
different "last known room" byte, distinct from <code>#R$A002($A009)</code>); what <code>#R$68ED</code>
(a fixed dictionary-region address used as a parameter to a call in
this same neighbourhood, <code>#R$CBC2</code>) is for; and whether the "walk by
count, match on room" logic in <code>#R$CAAF</code> really is the room-contents
lister. All flagged as leads for continuation, not claimed as
confirmed.</p>
<p>This is, however, the clearest picture yet of the game's actual turn
cycle: <strong>parse a command -&gt; dispatch to its handler via a computed
jump -&gt; check whether the player's room changed as a result -&gt; if so,
run follow-up logic that appears to involve room contents.</strong> That's a
genuinely major structural finding for continuing this disassembly,
even though the exact dispatch table (what fills HL before <code>#R$CB2C</code> is
called) and the room-description text itself remain open.</p>
<h2>Follow-up: found the actual verb dispatch table</h2>
<p>Traced back from #R$CB2C's one caller far enough to find where a verb
number actually turns into a handler address. At #R$C960:</p>
<pre><code>LD A,(IX+1)      ; a verb number, 1-based (IX points at some
                 ; per-command working record, not yet fully typed)
LD E,A; LD D,0
LD HL,#R$AA71      ; the table's base
DEC DE
ADD HL,DE
ADD HL,DE        ; HL = #R$AA71 + (verb_number - 1) * 2
LD E,(HL); INC HL; LD D,(HL); EX DE,HL   ; HL = the table's stored value
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Confirmed real</strong>, not just plausible-looking: dumped the first 20
entries of the table at #R$AA71 and every single one falls inside the
loaded "p" block's address range (mostly clustered in two bands, $9Axx
and $Exxx - suggestive of two different <em>kinds</em> of verb, see below).
Checked the bytes at verb #R$DD03(1)'s target (#R$9A99) and they disassemble as
real, sensible code (<code>CALL #R$9AA3</code>, <code>JP Z,#R$DC15</code>, ...) - this is
genuinely executable, not misread data.</p>
<p>Checked a couple of the $Exxx-band entries too (verb #R$DD03(3)'s target,
#R$E1A3; verb #R$DD03(4)'s, #R$E022) and they do <em>not</em> look like plain code in the
same way - more small values and what look like more addresses close
together, suggestive of some kind of compact per-verb record rather
than straight executable code. <strong>Not resolved</strong> - plausible guesses
(a message-id + fallback-handler pair; a small further dispatch
structure) are just guesses at this point, not confirmed.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for the project so far</strong>: verbs in this range that
land in the $9Axx band (at least verb #R$DD03(1)) get real custom handler code;
whatever lands in the $Exxx band is some other, more compact
structure. This is very likely the boundary discovery.md predicts
between "verbs with genuinely special logic" (movement, taking,
looking) and "verbs that just print a canned response" (most of them) -
matching how few <em>routines</em> a typical text adventure needs versus how
many <em>verbs</em> the vocabulary recognises.</p>
<p><strong>Concrete next step</strong>: disassemble verb #R$DD03(1)'s handler at #R$9A99 properly
(a real, confirmed, in-range routine - a solid, tractable next target),
and separately, try to work out which verb NUMBER corresponds to which
typed WORD - this project has confirmed the dictionary's per-letter
index table (<code>dictionary_notes.md</code>) but hasn't yet connected a
dictionary entry to the small integer "verb number" this table is
indexed by. That link (a dictionary entry -&gt; verb number mapping) is
probably the single most valuable next thing to find, since it would
finally connect the vocabulary side of this project to the action side.</p>
<h2>Follow-up: the "verb number" isn't a verb number - corrected</h2>
<p>Went looking for that link empirically: breakpointed #R$C960 during real
play and logged (IX+1) - what I'd been calling "the verb number" - for
several different typed commands, using a fresh game instance per
command so nothing from an earlier command's state could leak in.</p>
<p>The result <strong>falsified the simple "typed verb -&gt; number" hypothesis</strong>,
and it's worth recording exactly how, since this is precisely the kind
of confident-sounding-but-wrong reading <code>verification.md</code> warns about:</p>
<pre><code>LOOK:            index 11, IX=#R$8F06($8F08)  (twice)
INVENTORY:       index 11, IX=#R$8F06($8F08)  (twice)
TAKE LAMP:       index 11, IX=#R$8F06($8F08)  (twice)
OPEN PLAIN DOOR: index  6, IX=#R$90E4($90E5)  (three times), THEN
                 index 11, IX=#R$8F06($8F08)  (twice)
XYZZY / FOOBAR / (empty line): no hits at all
</code></pre>
<p>Three genuinely different, successfully-executed verbs (LOOK,
INVENTORY, TAKE) all produced the <em>exact same</em> index and the <em>exact
same</em> IX. That rules out "index = which verb was typed" - if it did,
these three would differ. What's consistent instead: <strong>the same
check runs after every successfully-parsed command</strong>, and it does
<em>not</em> run when the typed command was rejected outright (XYZZY,
FOOBAR, a bare Enter all produced zero hits). OPEN additionally
triggers a <em>different</em> index/IX pair, three times, <em>before</em> that
shared one - plausibly while resolving "which door" (three candidate
matches tried, given the real game's own "WHICH DOOR?" disambiguation
prompt seen earlier in this project when the noun is ambiguous).</p>
<p><strong>Current best reading</strong> (a genuine improvement, not yet fully
confirmed): IX doesn't point at "the verb" at all - it points at
<em>whichever record is currently being tested</em>, and the number at
offset +1 of that record is a <strong>per-record "primary action" field</strong>,
looked up in the shared #R$AA71 table to find out what that action
actually does. #R$8F06($8F08) looks like it might be a fixed, generic "end of
a successful turn" record (checked after everything), while #R$90E4($90E5) is
plausibly the plain door object's own record, checked specifically
while OPEN resolves which door is meant. This matches
<code>discovery.md</code>'s "an action-number byte immediately precedes this
address, so this is one entry in an object's own list of action
handlers" pattern far better than a flat global verb table would -
just requiring the correction that the table is shared and reusable
across many different <em>kinds</em> of record, not solely indexed by "the
verb the player typed."</p>
<p><strong>Not yet confirmed</strong>: what #R$8F06($8F08) and #R$90E4($90E5) actually are (record type,
full field layout), and therefore what "the single most valuable next
link" really looks like now - it's likely "which object/record does
the parser resolve a typed noun to" rather than "which number does a
typed verb map to", which is a more promising and probably easier
question to chase (three real, distinct IX values are already in hand
to dump and compare, the same way the actor records were successfully
characterised earlier in this project).</p>
<h2>Follow-up: dumped both records - looks like per-object action lists</h2>
<pre><code>#R$8F06($8F08): 1f 0b 0d 08 0c 0c 19 08 18 0c ff 01 1e 10 40 80 c0 00 68 01
#R$90E4($90E5): 24 06 0e 07 ff 04 ff ff ff 00 80 00 6e 22 16 06 00 00 00 00
</code></pre>
<p>Offset +1 matches the logged index exactly for both (11 = $0B for
#R$8F06($8F08), 6 = $06 for #R$90E4($90E5) - a good sanity check that the earlier
breakpoint logging was reading the right bytes). Field +0 differs
between them ($1F vs $24) and doesn't match the actor record's
consistent type-tag pattern, so <strong>these are a different table
entirely from the 17-byte actor/entity records</strong> - not yet named or
sized with confidence.</p>
<p>#R$90E4($90E5)'s tail is dominated by repeated $FF bytes (offsets +4, +6, +7,
+8), with real-looking values in between (+5 = $04) - a strong match
for <code>discovery.md</code>'s "object's own list of action handlers,
terminated by sentinel" pattern: read as a short list of action
numbers to try in turn, $FF marking "no more". #R$8F06($8F08)'s equivalent
region (+4 onward) has no $FF at all in the same span, consistent
with it being a longer, "generic/always-applicable" list rather than
one specific object's short list - fitting the "checked after every
successful command" behaviour observed. <strong>Plausible, not confirmed</strong> -
offered as the next concrete lead rather than settled fact.</p>
<h2>Follow-up: found where IY itself comes from - a real, static table</h2>
<p>Pushed one level further up: #R$C960 turned out to be called from deep
inside #R$C9C0, which pops an IY value that its own caller pushes before
jumping in (a "push a parameter, then JP" idiom, not a real CALL/RET
pair - which is exactly why grepping for <code>CALL #R$C9C0</code> earlier found
nothing; it isn't called that way at all).</p>
<p>Breakpointed #R$C9C0's entry directly (peeking at the stack rather than
disturbing it) across five different real commands, each from a fresh
game instance. The results were extremely clean: <strong>every command
started with the identical five IY values, in the identical order</strong>
(<code>#R$D715, #R$D769, #R$D77E, #R$D7D9, #R$D7FA</code>), before diverging at a
command-specific point, and (for LOOK/INVENTORY/TAKE/OPEN, though
notably not NORTH) converging again onto a long, identical shared
tail afterwards.</p>
<p>That fixed opening sequence turned out to be real, static data, not a
coincidence of a dynamic walk: searching the file for the literal bytes
of the first value (<code>#R$D715</code>) found exactly one occurrence, at #R$9928($9929),
sitting inside a clean 5-byte record: <code>$99 $13 &lt;addr low&gt; &lt;addr high&gt;
&lt;flag&gt;</code>. Dumping five records forward from there reproduces all five of
the confirmed IY values exactly, in exactly the observed order:</p>
<pre><code>#R$9923($9927): 99 13 15 D7 23   -&gt; #R$D715, flag $23
#R$9928($992C): 99 13 69 D7 23   -&gt; #R$D769, flag $23
#R$992D($9931): 99 13 7E D7 23   -&gt; #R$D77E, flag $23
#R$9932($9936): 99 13 D9 D7 2D   -&gt; #R$D7D9, flag $2D
#R$9937($993B): 99 13 FA D7 37   -&gt; #R$D7FA, flag $37
#R$993C($9940): (data no longer fits this 5-byte shape)
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>CORRECTED 2026-11-07.</strong> These are not 5-byte records. The bytes
from #R$9921 are the player's plan script (actor 0's script position in
the actor table at #R$9860), and the right alignment is one instruction
per RUN: <code>13 15 D7 23 99</code> = RUN #R$D715, failure address #R$9923. So the
"$99" is the high byte of the previous instruction's failure address
and the "flag" is the low byte of this one's. The five IY values are
the RUN targets, which is why they appeared in that order on every
command. See overlay_08_plans.py and the "plan scripts" Trivia entry.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Confirmed</strong>: this is a genuine, fixed, 5-entry table of "always
checked, every single turn" records, each shaped <code>$99, $13, &lt;a 16-bit
address&gt;, &lt;a flag byte&gt;</code> - the constant <code>$99 $13</code> header and varying
trailing flag ($23 for the first three, $2D and $37 for the last two)
are both real, verified against the file's own bytes, not inferred
from behaviour alone. The data stops fitting this exact shape
immediately after the fifth entry, so this table is short and
self-contained - it is <em>not</em> a long list that the rest of the observed
sequence (the shared tail, or the command-specific divergent entry)
continues into; those come from somewhere else, not yet found.</p>
<p><strong>Not yet determined</strong>: what these five particular checks are for
(candidate guesses - a scheduled Watson remark, the game clock
advancing, a random event, a game-over condition check - are just
guesses), what the flag byte means (the $23/$23/$23/$2D/$37 pattern
splits 3-vs-2, which is suggestive but unexplained), and where the
<em>rest</em> of the sequence seen at runtime (the command-specific entry and
the long shared tail) is actually stored, since it isn't a simple
continuation of this same table.</p>
<h2>Follow-up: the "boot sequence" is also the per-turn NPC-processing loop</h2>
<p>Chased the stable stack addresses one more level up and found something
that requires a real correction to earlier work in this project, not
just a new fact: the actor-table walk documented in
<code>overlay_01_boot.py</code> as part of the game's one-time boot sequence
(#R$A040 onward: "walks a sentinel-terminated table of 8-byte records at
#R$9860...") is <strong>also genuinely re-entered on every single turn</strong>, not
boot-only as originally described.</p>
<p><strong>Why this is confirmed, not just suspected</strong>: this project's own
harness reaches its first idle ("waiting for a keypress") state only
~40 slices after boot - which necessarily means any one-time boot
processing, including this actor walk, has already finished by then.
Sending a real <code>LOOK</code> command afterwards and breakpointing deep inside
the walk (at #R$C9C0, several calls down) produced dozens of hits, with
a stack trace showing the walk stepping through the <em>same</em> #R$9860 table
records (addresses #R$9860, #R$9868, ... - the confirmed 8-byte stride)
all over again. That can only mean the command re-triggered the same
code, not that boot was somehow still running minutes of emulated time
later.</p>
<p>Traced the actual mechanism one level further: <code>#R$A0B0</code> (inside the
walk, immediately following <code>LD A,(IY+0)</code> - this record's id) calls
<code>#R$CC78</code>, a genuine, real subroutine:</p>
<pre><code>#R$CC78: CALL #R$CC5A       ; (a shared tail also reachable via #R$CC37 below)
       LD IY,(#R$9FDB)    ; reload this actor's own resolved record
       CALL #R$D008
       LD L,(IY+1); LD H,(IY+2)   ; read a handler address out of it
       ... (continues past this disassembly window)
</code></pre>
<p>and <code>#R$CC5A</code>'s own entry (<code>#R$CC37</code>, confirmed the real target of the
call at <code>#R$CC5A</code>) does, in order: clear a flag (#R$CC0B), resolve the
current actor's id to its full record via the already-confirmed
#R$CC0E, stash that record pointer at #R$9FDB and its id at #R$A032($A036), call
#R$B91F (not yet examined), then call the already-confirmed #R$CC29 (get
this actor's room, via #R$D495) and #R$D045 (get the <em>player's</em>
room, actor 0, also via #R$D495) - building exactly the kind of
"compare this actor's room against the player's" context <code>#R$CAAF</code>
(documented earlier in this file) also uses.</p>
<p><strong>What this adds up to, confirmed by this chain of genuine, cross-
referenced routines</strong>: for every actor in the #R$9860 table, once per
turn, the game resolves that actor's full state record, compares its
room against the player's, and reads a handler address out of the
record's own fields (+1/+2) to find out what - if anything - that
actor does this turn. This is a real, coherent "NPC behaviour" system,
not a boot-only curiosity, and the connection back to the whole
#R$C960/#R$C9C0/#R$AA71 apparatus documented above is now much clearer: that
apparatus is very likely how each actor's <em>own</em> handler decides what
action to take, not solely how the player's typed verb gets resolved.</p>
<p><strong>Not yet confirmed</strong>: the exact mechanism by which the main per-turn
loop re-enters this walk (no static <code>CALL</code> to any address inside it
was found by grepping literal bytes, consistent with this project's
recurring pattern of computed jumps rather than plain calls - see the
dispatch mechanism documented earlier in this file); what #R$B91F,
#R$C821, and #R$D008 do; and the precise field layout of the per-actor
state record beyond the two fields (+1/+2, a handler address) newly
confirmed here.</p>
<p><strong>This is a real correction, not just an addition</strong>: <code>overlay_01_boot.py</code>'s
description of the #R$A040 entry point should be read as accurate only
for its very first several instructions (the one-time DI / stack
reset / RNG seed / New-vs-Continue prompt, which genuinely cannot make
sense to repeat every turn) - the actor-table walk further down in the
same routine is shared, reentrant code, not boot-only. The overlay
comments themselves have been left as originally written for now
(they were not factually wrong about <em>that</em> walk happening at boot -
it does - only incomplete about it also happening elsewhere), with
this note as the correction of record; a future pass should split
#R$A040 into its true one-time-only prologue and a separately-named,
properly cross-referenced shared actor-processing routine.</p>
<h2>Follow-up: the room-text print path is resolved - and a false alarm explained</h2>
<p>Earlier in this project (see the "found (and ruled out) a real message-
printing loop" section above), breakpointing #R$C2BA directly during a
real <code>LOOK</code> command produced zero hits, which was written up honestly
as unresolved and slightly suspicious. Chasing a completely different
thread (the shared echo/print primitive #R$A9B7's <code>#R$A022($A027)</code>-gated branch)
led to <code>#R$AA11</code>'s <code>JP #R$C1E1</code> and <code>#R$AA41</code>'s <code>CALL #R$C1E1</code> - both real,
confirmed call targets landing exactly on a byte boundary that matches,
instruction for instruction, what this project's very first pass at
this routine had already described under the address #R$C1FD (a
misaligned disassembly window at the time, flagged honestly as
uncertain rather than asserted). <strong>The original description of that
routine's logic was correct all along; only the exact starting
address was off, by an amount consistent with the window simply
starting a few bytes into the routine rather than at its true entry.</strong></p>
<p>With the correct entry address in hand, re-ran the exact same #R$C2BA
breakpoint test from earlier in this project - and this time it
produced <strong>170 real hits</strong> during a single <code>LOOK</code> command, with a
dominant, real caller (<code>#R$A9FD</code>, returning to <code>#R$AA00</code>, 133 of the 170
hits). <strong>The earlier zero-hit result was a bug in that test, not a
finding about the game</strong> - almost certainly the same class of mistake
already documented in <code>harness_notes.md</code> (a stray or mishandled
breakpoint interaction), not a real property of #R$C2BA. Recorded here
explicitly so a future reader who finds the old "ruled out" language
doesn't treat it as still standing - it doesn't.</p>
<p><strong>Confirmed print pipeline for ordinary game text</strong>, assembled from
everything now verified across this project:</p>
<pre><code>#R$C1E1  gatekeeper: checks #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3C)($5C3C), then #R$A022($A027) -&gt; either falls through
       to #R$C2BA directly, or (when #R$A022($A027) is set) into a case-folding
       and quote-tracking region around #R$AA11($AA12)-#R$AA4F($AA50)
#R$AA11($AA12)+ case-folding/quote logic -&gt; eventually calls back into #R$C2BA
#R$C2BA  handles CR specially (paging, see overlay_01_boot.py),
       otherwise calls PLOT-CHAR
#R$C388  PLOT-CHAR: draws the glyph (confirmed since early in this
       project as the hook that recovers real game text)
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Not fully traced</strong>: the exact nested call graph between #R$AA11($AA12) and
#R$C2BA has several small, mutually-referencing routines (#R$AA14, #R$AA41's
own call back into #R$C1E1, etc.) that weren't stepped through
individually - the architecture above is confirmed by real breakpoint
evidence at both ends (#R$C1E1/#R$A9B7's entry conditions, and #R$C2BA's 170
real hits with a real caller), not by tracing every intermediate
instruction. That's a reasonable place to stop for this pass; the
remaining detail would mostly refine exactly <em>how</em> case-folding and
quote-tracking work, not <em>whether</em> this is the real print path, which
is now solidly established.</p>
<h2>Follow-up: #R$B91F is intro/setup-only, not part of the per-turn loop</h2>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>CORRECTED 2026-11-07: wrong.</strong> Re-tested with every breakpoint
stepped over: #R$B91F runs once per actor whose turn comes up, every
turn (LOOK 19 hits, WAIT 67, TAKE LAMP 18; 0 only for a command that
takes no time). It looks for an order or remark waiting for that
actor on the list at #R$9FEE. The "scripted" id order below is just the
actor table's order. The section is kept as it was written.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Went back to properly verify #R$B91F (read last round but explicitly left
unconfirmed). Breakpointed it across boot and the opening intro
sequence separately: <strong>zero hits during boot itself</strong>, then <strong>25 hits</strong>
during the four ENTERs that step through the opening cutscene text,
with <code>#R$A032($A036)</code> (the id it searches for, in the confirmed-real table this
project has been calling the "3-byte index" style search since #R$CC0E)
taking this exact sequence of values:</p>
<pre><code>0, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 104, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 31, 34, 36, 37, 38, 39,
40, 41, 42, 43, 0
</code></pre>
<p>That is not a simple linear walk (it jumps from 6 to 104 and back, then
later jumps from 13 to 31 and skips 32/33/35) - it looks like a
<strong>scripted list of specific ids being validated or placed</strong> as part of
the opening sequence (very plausibly "make sure these specific objects
start in their specific rooms"), not a generic per-actor search that
would run every turn. Confirmed separately: breakpointing #R$B91F during
an ordinary <code>LOOK</code> command, well after the intro has finished,
produced <strong>zero hits</strong> - so whatever this is, it only runs once, during
setup.</p>
<p>This corrects last round's framing of #R$B91F as "on the newly-traced
NPC-dispatch path" - it shares a search mechanism with that path
(and is genuinely called from #R$CC37, which is genuinely part of that
path), but its own role is intro-specific object/room placement, not
an ongoing per-turn check. The #R$9FF0 table it searches is confirmed to
start out essentially all zero in the static file and become populated
with real, non-zero data (including what looks like a genuine record
pointer, ending <code>...8C BA</code> - matching this project's earlier-confirmed
id-0 record address, #R$8CBA) only after this sequence runs - consistent
with it being where those scripted placements get recorded.</p>
<p><strong>Not yet confirmed</strong>: what the specific ids in that sequence
correspond to (room numbers? object numbers? a mix?), or the precise
record layout #R$9FF0 holds once populated.</p>
<h2>Follow-up: #R$C821 is a noun/actor resolution test, with a real ID catalog</h2>
<p>Found #R$C863 (called twice inside #R$C821) does something worth
recording on its own: it resolves an id via #R$D237, then in #R$C86E walks
a <strong>chain</strong> - <code>(IX+1)</code> holds the <em>next</em> id to follow (not a memory
address; another id, resolved the same way), continuing while bits 5
or 3 of the new record's own <code>(IX+5)</code> are set, stopping when <code>(IX+1)</code>
is $FF or bit 7 of <code>(IX+5)</code> is clear. Read as "find this object's
ultimate container, walking up through however many levels of nesting,
honouring per-level flags" - exactly the "is this object inside
something else" mechanism a text adventure needs (container is inside
a room; item is inside a container; item is carried by an actor),
though the exact meaning of the bit-5/bit-3/bit-7 flags isn't nailed
down yet.</p>
<p>#R$C821 itself is called from 12 real, confirmed sites - among them
#R$CC67, which is inside the already-documented #R$CC64 (compares the
player's resolved room-via-container against the current NPC's, then
sets the print-gate flag #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3C)($5C3C) accordingly) - strong, independent
confirmation this really is a "same room / visible" test, not a guess.</p>
<p>Bred an actual <strong>id catalog</strong> by breakpointing #R$C821 during several
real, known commands (present objects produce many hits including a
repeated, stable id; absent objects produce only a handful of zeros
before giving up):</p>
<pre><code>TAKE LAMP / EXAMINE LAMP  -&gt; id 22 (repeated heavily)
TAKE PIPE (not present)   -&gt; no stable id, gives up after 5 hits
EXAMINE ARMCHAIR          -&gt; ids 23, 24 (two armchairs in the room -
                             &quot;your armchair&quot; and &quot;watsons armchair&quot;,
                             matching the room text exactly)
TALK TO WATSON            -&gt; id 3, and id 25
EXAMINE SOFA              -&gt; ids 60, 71 (lower confidence - SOFA isn't
                             actually examinable per earlier testing,
                             &quot;YOU CANNOT EXAMINE THE SOFA&quot;, so these
                             may be something else, e.g. a rejected
                             candidate or a message id, not a settled
                             object id)
TAKE MONEY (not present)  -&gt; ids 27, 28, 32, 35, repeating - lower
                             confidence for the same reason as SOFA
</code></pre>
<p><strong>id 3 = Watson is a genuine cross-confirmation, not a first sighting</strong>:
several rounds ago, this project independently found (by calling the
confirmed #R$D237/#R$D495 room-accessor directly) that actor id 3's room
matches actor id 0's (the player's) - both room $08, the sitting room -
and guessed, from context, that id 3 was "very plausibly Holmes and
Watson, both starting in the sitting room together". This round's
<code>TALK TO WATSON</code> test landing on id 3 through a completely different
code path (noun resolution rather than the room accessor) makes that
guess a properly confirmed fact rather than a plausible guess.</p>
<p><strong>Not yet confirmed</strong>: ids for SOFA and MONEY are lower-confidence,
since both objects are known (from earlier gameplay testing) to not
actually be interactable in the expected way, so the ids seen may be
intermediate/rejected candidates rather than settled results. The LAMP,
ARMCHAIR, and WATSON ids are higher-confidence, since those commands
all succeeded and produced a small, stable, repeated id rather than a
handful of ids that never repeat.</p>
<h2>Follow-up: unifying several previously-separate threads into one system</h2>
<p>Chased the "what actually loads HL before #R$CB2C's dispatch" question
(the concrete next step identified after last round's #R$E003 correction
- see <code>dictionary_notes.md</code>) and it ties together three separate
findings from earlier rounds into one coherent picture.</p>
<p>Breakpointing #R$CB2C directly during a real <code>TAKE LAMP</code> shows its one
real caller is #R$CA64 (return address #R$CA67, matching <code>CALL #R$CB2C</code>'s
own length), confirmed 22 times for that single command. Breakpointing
#R$CA64 itself shows <em>its</em> one real caller is #R$C99C, with <strong>IY taking
exactly the same sequence this project found months (in session terms)
earlier while investigating #R$C9C0</strong>: the fixed 5-entry static table at
#R$9923($9927) first (#R$D715, #R$D769, #R$D77E, #R$D7D9, #R$D7FA), then a command-
specific entry, then a long shared tail. For <code>TAKE LAMP</code>, that
command-specific entry is <code>#R$DCC1</code> - <strong>exactly the id this project's
"real object/actor id catalog" round found weeks earlier for <code>TAKE
LAMP</code></strong> (recorded there as one of the ids seen while confirming #R$C821
as a visibility test).</p>
<p><strong>This means #R$C9C0, #R$CA64, and #R$CB2C are not three separate mechanisms
- they are one unified per-candidate loop</strong>: for each candidate record
in turn (5 fixed universal checks, then whatever the parser resolved
this command to, then a long shared tail of further candidates), the
loop calls #R$CA64 (with IY pointing at that candidate), which sets up
some room-comparison state and calls #R$CB2C, which jumps through a
handler address read from <em>that candidate's own record</em> (the
<code>(IY+2)/(IY+3)</code> pattern this project found still earlier, at #R$C9E3).
"Try this candidate's own handler, whatever it is" - run once per
candidate, for every command, not a special path just for the
player's own verb.</p>
<p><strong>This also means #R$AA71/#R$C960 - the table this project spent two
rounds on, first as "the verb table", then corrected to "not simply
verb number", then (wrongly) claimed and un-claimed as TAKE's handler
- is confirmed to be a genuinely separate system from this dispatch
loop.</strong> The unified loop's dispatch address comes from each
candidate's own stored record fields, not from #R$AA71 at all. What
#R$AA71 actually is remains open, but the search space is now much
better bounded: it is not the per-turn action dispatcher, since that
mechanism is now fully traced and doesn't touch it.</p>
<p><strong>Not yet confirmed</strong>: what determines the length and contents of the
"long shared tail" after the command-specific entry (is it a fixed
list, like the 5-entry table, or built dynamically per room?), and
what #R$AA71 is used for instead, now that "the verb dispatch table" is
ruled out for it.</p>
<h2>Follow-up: found what #R$AA71 is actually for - a shared action library</h2>
<p>Went back to the open question directly: is #R$C960 (the #R$AA71 lookup)
ever reached at all during real play? Breakpointed it across boot, the
intro, and ten different real commands. It fires for some commands
(<code>INVENTORY</code>, <code>DROP LAMP</code>, <code>OPEN PLAIN DOOR</code>) and not others (<code>LOOK</code>,
<code>TAKE LAMP</code>, <code>NORTH</code>, <code>SOUTH</code>, <code>EXAMINE ARMCHAIR</code>, <code>TALK TO WATSON</code>) -
genuinely selective, not dead code and not universal.</p>
<p>Logging the actual index looked up (<code>(IX+1)</code>) for each hit shows
something the earlier "per-candidate walk" model didn't capture: two
consecutive <code>INVENTORY</code> hits came from <code>#R$8F06($8F08)</code> and <code>#R$8F06($8F0C)</code> - four bytes
apart, both inside the <em>same</em> record this project dumped much earlier
(<code>1F 0B 0D 08 0C 0C 19 08 18 0C FF 01...</code>). Read as 4-byte groups, the
second byte of each group is exactly the index looked up: group 0
(<code>0B</code>=11) matches the first hit, group 1 (<code>0C</code>=12) matches the second.
<strong>So a single candidate record can carry several small "try this
generic action" entries, tried in turn - #R$CA64/#R$C9C0's per-candidate
walk doesn't stop at one #R$AA71 lookup per candidate, it can make
several.</strong></p>
<p><code>OPEN PLAIN DOOR</code> reliably produces index 6 - the exact table entry
(#R$DE9D) this project found and then explicitly <em>un</em>-confirmed as
"verb #R$DD03(6)" two rounds ago, on the grounds that it was an isolated
coincidence with no supporting pattern from other commands. <strong>This
time it's different: this is a real, repeated, breakpoint-confirmed
hit during actual OPEN processing, not a static address coincidence.</strong>
Disassembling #R$DE9D properly this time (correctly aligned, past a
couple of header-looking bytes) shows genuinely coherent code:</p>
<pre><code>#R$DEA4: LD IX,(#R$A012($A013))
       SET 5,(IX+5)     ; &lt;- sets bit 5 of the confirmed record's field +5
       OR 1; RET
...
#R$DEB6: LD IX,(#R$A012($A013))
       RES 5,(IX+5)     ; &lt;- clears the same bit
       OR 1; RET
</code></pre>
<p><strong>This is a shared OPEN/CLOSE implementation</strong> - one pair of generic
routines that any object's own small action-list can reference (via
the 4-byte sub-entries above), rather than every openable object
needing its own custom handler. It also gives real meaning to a flag
this project found gating the containment-chain walk (#R$C86E, several
rounds ago) but never explained: <strong>bit 5 of field +5 is "is this thing
currently open"</strong> - which is exactly the condition you'd want to gate
"can you see/reach what's inside this container" on. Two previously
separate, unexplained findings from different rounds turn out to be
the same flag.</p>
<p>A third chunk in the same disassembled region (#R$DECA($DECB)-#R$DED8) touches
field +$0F (the confirmed room-number field) and field +1, copying in
a value from #R$A032($A035) (a "default target position" variable seen
elsewhere) - plausibly a generic "move this into/onto something"
handler, not yet confirmed as thoroughly as the open/close pair.</p>
<p><strong>Corrected understanding of #R$AA71</strong>: not a verb-number table in the
naive sense (that hypothesis was tested and failed two rounds ago),
and not the main per-turn dispatcher (that's #R$CB2C, fully traced last
round) - it is a <strong>library of shared, generic action implementations</strong>
(open, close, and likely others), referenced by small index values
that individual objects' own compact action-lists point into. Most
commands don't touch it at all because most objects have their own
direct handler (via the <code>(IY+2)/(IY+3)</code> pointer, dispatched straight
through #R$CB2C); #R$AA71 is specifically for behaviour that's the <em>same</em>
across many different objects, so it's implemented once and shared.</p>
<p><strong>Not yet confirmed</strong>: the third (#R$DECA($DECB)) chunk's exact purpose, entry
1's (#R$9A99) real role now that "TAKE" is confirmed not to reach it,
and how an object's own action-list decides whether to use its own
custom handler or fall through to a shared #R$AA71 entry.</p>
<h2>Follow-up: mapped several more #R$AA71 entries - a real action library</h2>
<p>Broadened the same test (breakpoint #R$C960, log the index, try many
commands) across sixteen more commands. Several more indices came back
with clean, repeatable, real hits:</p>
<pre><code>index 8  ('GET OUT'):     #R$E432
index 9  ('SIT'):         #R$E40A
index 11 (INVENTORY, WAIT, STAND UP): #R$E2C7
index 12 (INVENTORY, SEARCH SOFA):    #R$E3EA
</code></pre>
<p>Disassembling each (past a small header of the same kind entries 1 and
6 had) confirms a real, coherent pattern:</p>
<p><strong>Index 9 (SIT)</strong>: <code>LD IY,(#R$A012($A017)); LD A,(#R$A032($A034)); LD (IY+1),A</code> - takes
the cached full record for the current actor and sets <em>its own</em> field
+1 (the same field the containment-chain walker, #R$C86E, follows as
"what contains me") to the target furniture's id. Sitting in something
is implemented as "you are now contained by it" - the exact same
mechanism as an item being inside a box, reused for a person sitting in
a chair.</p>
<p><strong>Index 8 (GET OUT)</strong>: <code>LD IY,(#R$A012($A013)); LD A,(IY+1); LD IY,(#R$A012($A017)); LD
(IY+1),A</code> - reads the <em>target</em> furniture's own field +1 (i.e. whatever
contains the furniture - the room it's in) and copies that into the
current actor's field +1. Getting out of a chair is "you are now
contained by whatever contains the chair" - the natural inverse of
SIT, built from the same containment field.</p>
<p><strong>Index 11 (a shared generic fallback, used by three very different
commands)</strong>: does a small self-modifying-code trick (stores a computed
byte into the address immediately before its own code, <code>#R$E2C6</code> - flagged
explicitly per this project's own commenting standard, since it's
exactly the kind of thing that's confusing to a reader unless called
out), then loads a <em>fixed</em> address, <code>#R$F2B6</code>, into DE and calls a tail
routine that swaps the current actor/target ids into the position
variables (#R$A032($A034)/#R$A032($A035)) before printing. Read as: <strong>a generic "swap in
the current actor/target and print a stock message" handler</strong> - which
would explain why <code>WAIT</code> (which just prints "You wait." verbatim),
<code>INVENTORY</code>, and <code>STAND UP</code> (when not sitting, presumably falling
through after a more specific check fails) all reach the same code:
none of them need custom logic beyond "print this canned response".</p>
<p><strong>Not yet confirmed</strong>: index 12's exact behaviour (seen with <code>INVENTORY</code>
and <code>SEARCH SOFA</code> together - plausibly a second, related generic
response, not yet disassembled in full), what the self-modified byte at
#R$E2C6 is actually used for afterwards, and what #R$CBA7 and #R$DD0E (called
from several of these entries) do.</p>
<p><strong>Updated picture of #R$AA71 as a whole</strong>: entries found so far - 1
(unconfirmed reachability), 6 (open/close), 8 (get out), 9 (sit), 11
(generic message fallback), 12 (unconfirmed detail) - are consistent
with a genuine, coherent <strong>shared action library</strong>: a modest set of
common behaviours (successfully verified now for at least four
distinct ones) that many different objects and commands reference
rather than reimplementing, exactly the kind of space-saving reuse
<code>discovery.md</code> predicts for this style of engine, now demonstrated
with real, repeatable evidence rather than a single coincidence.</p>
<h2>Follow-up: #R$CBA7, a shared guard-check called from both SIT and GET OUT</h2>
<p>Both #R$E40A (SIT) and #R$E432 (GET OUT) call #R$CBA7 first. Disassembled it:</p>
<pre><code>#R$CBA7: LD A,(#R$B934)
       BIT 6,A
       RET Z              ; bit clear: no problem, continue normally
       POP HL             ; bit set: discard the return address...
       XOR A
       LD (#R$A022($A027)),A
       LD HL,#R$681A         ; ...print this fixed message...
       CALL #R$A59F
       JP #R$DC15             ; ...and jump to the shared failure point
</code></pre>
<p>Read as a shared <strong>pre-condition guard</strong>: check a flag at #R$B934 (bit 6)
before letting SIT or GET OUT proceed, and if it's set, print a fixed
message and bail out via the same shared failure point (#R$DC15) this
project has seen several other entries use. #R$B934 is the same byte
this project's #R$B91F investigation found being set from a caller's A
register a few rounds ago (<code>LD (#R$B935),A</code> nearby, <code>LD (B934)</code> here) -
not yet tied together into a full account of what condition this flag
tracks, but confirmed as a real, shared guard rather than a one-off.</p>
<p><strong>Not yet confirmed</strong>: what sets bit 6 of #R$B934, and therefore what
specific condition blocks sitting/getting out (a reasonable guess -
already carrying/holding something incompatible, or already engaged in
a different action - is just a guess, not tested).</p>
<h2>Follow-up: #R$D008 is part of an already-known accessor family</h2>
<p>Finally examined #R$D008 (called from the confirmed per-actor dispatch
bridge, #R$CC78). It turns out to belong to the same small utility
block this project already documented (<code>overlay_01_boot.py</code>'s #R$D045,
"get the player's room") - #R$D008 through #R$D04E is one compact set of
get/set accessors for an entity record's fields +3/+4 (a 16-bit value)
and +6 (a byte), all built on the same comparison primitive (#R$CFB6),
with several genuine external entry points into the middle of the same
block (#R$D008, #R$D026, #R$D03B, #R$D045 are all real, separately-reached
addresses, not one linear routine with internal fallthrough by
accident).</p>
<pre><code>#R$D008: read record IY's fields +4/+5, compare via #R$CFB6 against
       record IY's own +3; if equal, cache record IX's +3/+4/+6
       into #R$9FD6/#R$9FD7
#R$D026: the inverse - compare a cached value against #R$9FD7, and if
       different, write it back into record IX's own +3/+4/+6
#R$D03B: a plain setter - write A/HL straight into record IY's +3/+4/+5
#R$D045: (already confirmed) get the player's room via #R$9860/#R$D495
</code></pre>
<p>Read together: a small "cache and conditionally sync" mechanism for
whatever fields +3/+4/+6 hold on an entity record (still not
confirmed what they represent, beyond the room-number field being a
different one, +$0F). Not a new structural discovery on its own, but
it closes out the last previously-unexamined routine on the per-actor
NPC-dispatch path this project traced several rounds ago.</p>
<h2>Session 2026-11-05</h2>
<p>The verb and action system, the dictionary, the things and the rooms
are now decoded from their data by <code>dictionary.py</code>, <code>objects.py</code> and
<code>rooms.py</code>, each following the code that reads the format, and are
written up block by block in <code>overlay_04_actions.py</code> and
<code>overlay_06_rooms.py</code>. Corrections to earlier entries are marked
CORRECTED in the overlays (and in the README's 2026-11-05 section).
The key results:</p>
<ul>
<li>Containment is one field: +1 of a thing's record is what it is in or
  held by. TAKE sets target.+1 = actor; SIT/GET INTO sets actor.+1 =
  chair; GET OUT sets actor.+1 = chair.+1.</li>
<li>Size (+2) is three things depending on what the thing is: its bulk,
  how much a container holds (fit check #R$DEE4), and for a passage how
  big an actor may be to get through (#R$DD35). The player and Watson are
  96, so a passage of 69 (a police man) or 85 (the hidden door to the
  opium den) stops them.</li>
<li>A room's exits are (direction, thing in the way, destination).
  Destination 0 is scenery: #R$D448 skips it, so the description
  mentions it ("To the east there is a deep stream") but it's never a
  visible or usable exit.</li>
<li>Door things are shared between buildings and so is their open state
  (bug: one front door for three houses).</li>
</ul>
<h2>2026-11-07: the plan-script language</h2>
<p>The actor table at #R$9860 (8-byte records: +0 id, +1/+2 script position,
+3 plan verb, +4/+5 plan direct/indirect object) gives each of 24
actors a script. #R$CC78 runs one instruction per actor per turn. Opcode
in the low nibble of the first byte; bit 7 = success address follows,
bit 4 = failure address follows, bit 5 = erase after success (no script
uses it), bit 6 = busy (refuse the player's requests). Opcodes:
1 DO verb, 2 DO verb dobj iobj, 3 RUN record, 4 ASK person TO verb dobj
iobj, 5 SAY TO person message, 6 PRINT message, 7 RUN record dobj iobj,
8 SIGNAL actor, 9 AWAIT actor, A RETURN FAIL, B RETURN OK, C unused
(would hang), D/F PASS, E GOTO. Argument bytes $FA-$FD are the plan's
verb, the actor itself, and the plan's indirect and direct objects.
Success ends the actor's turn; failure tries the next instruction at
once, up to ten times. Checks-only action records (flags bit 7) are
sub-scripts, entered with a call frame (#R$CF80) and left with RETURN.
Verbs 128-140 (#R$9FAB) are only for scripts. Tools: tools/plan.py,
tools/allplans.py. Verified by a 106-turn fetch trace (every fetched
address decoded).</p>

[Page:map]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(map-content)

[map-content]
<p>Decoded from the room table at #R$840B. The map is a grid: a room that is north of another is drawn above it, one to the east is drawn to its right, and so on, area by area. Arrows are the exits the room table gives; a two-headed arrow is an exit that exists in both directions; a red dashed line is an exit that is blocked for the player by something too narrow. An orange dashed line is an exit that cannot be drawn between neighbouring cells because the exit table is not a flat plan (the garden maze does this, and so do a couple of single exits in Brown's house and Basil's London house); its directions are written on it, for example NW/SE for "northwest one way, southeast back". A small purple tag under a room, such as S&rarr;76, is an exit to another area or another floor. Click a room to jump to its row below. Rooms with no walking exits are vehicles (the cab and trains), reached by getting in rather than by walking.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto"><svg style="max-width:none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 1366 7018" width="1366" font-family="Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"><defs><marker id="ah" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0L10 5L0 10z" fill="context-stroke"/></marker><marker id="ahs" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0L10 5L0 10z" fill="context-stroke"/></marker></defs><text x="10" y="24" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#4a3d26">Leatherhead: streets, station and platforms</text><line x1="281" y1="368" x2="281" y2="314" stroke="#7a6a4f" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ah)" marker-start="url(#ahs)"/><line x1="206" y1="395" x2="160" y2="395" stroke="#7a6a4f" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ah)" marker-start="url(#ahs)"/><line x1="356" y1="395" x2="402" y2="395" stroke="#7a6a4f" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ah)" marker-start="url(#ahs)"/><line x1="281" y1="422" x2="281" y2="476" stroke="#7a6a4f" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ah)" marker-start="url(#ahs)"/><line x1="330" y1="368" x2="428" y2="314" stroke="#7a6a4f" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ah)" marker-start="url(#ahs)"/><line x1="281" y1="530" x2="281" y2="584" stroke="#7a6a4f" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ah)" marker-start="url(#ahs)"/><line x1="281" y1="260" x2="281" y2="206" stroke="#7a6a4f" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ah)" marker-start="url(#ahs)"/><line x1="281" y1="152" x2="281" y2="98" stroke="#7a6a4f" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ah)" marker-start="url(#ahs)"/><a href="#room50"><rect x="206" y="368" width="150" height="54" rx="8" fill="#f4efe1" stroke="#7a6a4f"/><text x="212" y="382" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" fill="#4a3d26">50</text><text x="212" y="396" font-size="10" fill="#222">main street</text></a><a href="#room51"><rect x="206" y="476" width="150" height="54" rx="8" fill="#f4efe1" stroke="#7a6a4f"/><text x="212" y="490" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" fill="#4a3d26">51</text><text x="212" y="504" font-size="10" fill="#222">cobden lane</text></a><text x="208" y="541" font-size="9" fill="#5a4a8a">E&#8594;56</text><a href="#room52"><rect x="206" y="584" width="150" height="54" rx="8" fill="#f4efe1" stroke="#7a6a4f"/><text x="212" y="598" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" fill="#4a3d26">52</text><text x="212" y="612" font-size="10" fill="#222">small lane</text></a><text x="208" y="649" font-size="9" fill="#5a4a8a">E&#8594;60 W&#8594;81</text><a href="#room62"><rect x="10" y="368" width="150" height="54" rx="8" fill="#f4efe1" stroke="#7a6a4f"/><text x="16" y="382" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" fill="#4a3d26">62</text><text x="16" y="396" font-size="10" fill="#222">browns front gate</text></a><text x="12" y="433" font-size="9" fill="#5a4a8a">S&#8594;76</text><text x="158" y="433" font-size="9" fill="#b03030" text-anchor="end">blocked: too narrow</text><a href="#room92"><rect x="402" y="368" width="150" height="54" rx="8" fill="#f4efe1" str