[Page:cast]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(cast-content)

[cast-content]
<p>Every character runs a plan script (see #R$CC78 for the language). This table is generated from the scripts themselves by tools/plan.py: the instruction count is what is reachable from the actor's starting position, not counting sub-scripts it calls. Hours come from verb #R$F1D6(136) ("is it this hour?"), rooms from verb #R$E5AD(131) ("is this person in this room?") and moves from verb #R$E497(128). Messages are the PRINT and SAY TO instructions, rendered by the game's own interpreter with "you" standing in for the speaker and the oil lamp for the object.</p><p>The scripts were checked by running the game: over 106 turns every instruction the interpreter fetched was one this decoding finds. Who a character is waiting for, and the hours, are read from the scripts; most were not seen happening.</p><p>"Starts in" is the room in the file, before the opening minutes are played. "Busy" instructions make the character refuse what the player asks (#R$CBA7). The after-turn routine runs after each of its turns (#R$9C77).</p><table class="disassembly"><tr><th>Actor</th><th>Starts in</th><th>Script</th><th>Instructions</th><th>After-turn</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td>0 you</td><td>room 8 (your room)</td><td>#R$9921</td><td>11 (0 busy)</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2 inspector lestrade</td><td>room 11 (scotland yard)</td><td>#R$E7E7</td><td>223 (181 busy)</td><td>#R$9DFF</td><td>waits for 1am, 7am, 10am, 9pm, 10pm<br>signals chief constable straker; signals major percival ffoulkes; signals local police man; waits for chief constable straker; waits for major percival ffoulkes<br>checks where people are: room 50 (main street), room 55 (platform two), room 103 (old mill road)<br>moves things: 93 large rock to room 0 (off stage), self to room 0 (off stage), self to room 11 (scotland yard), self to room 12 (slater street)<br>can end the game: #R$9E6A, #R$9E85, #R$9EA2<ul><li>#R$E7EC: &quot;HELLO&quot;</li><li>#R$E7F6: &quot;NOW WHAT SEEMS TO BE THE PROBLEM HERE?&quot;</li><li>#R$E800: &quot;WELL THEN SHOW ME TO THE BROWN&#x27;S ESTATE PLEASE.&quot;</li><li>#R$E81E: &quot;HAVE YOUR MEN SEARCHED THE RIVER FOR THE GUN YET?&quot;</li><li>#R$E850: &quot;RIGHT THEN CONSTABLE WILL YOU SHOW ME TO MISS STRACHAN AND MR PHIPPS PLEASE.&quot;</li><li>#R$E868: &quot;YOU SAY&#x27; I WOULD LIKE YOU TO REMAIN HERE PLEASE.&#x27;&quot;</li><li>#R$E86B: &quot;CHIEF CONSTABLE I WILL SEE THE STAFF NOW.&quot;</li><li>#R$E888: &quot;YOU SAY&#x27; I WOULD LIKE YOU TO REMAIN HERE PLEASE.&#x27;&quot;</li><li>#R$E88B: &quot;WELL I MUST SAY THIS IS ALL VERY CONFUSING, PERHAPS I SHOULD VISIT THE ESTATE OF MRS JONES NOW.&quot;</li><li>#R$E8B9: &quot;WHAT IS GOING ON HERE CONSTABLE?&quot;</li><li>#R$E8C5: &quot;COME WITH ME PLEASE.&quot;</li><li>#R$E8EA: &quot;YOU SAY&#x27; I WOULD LIKE YOU TO REMAIN HERE PLEASE.&#x27;&quot;</li><li>... and 11 more</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td>3 watson</td><td>room 8 (your room)</td><td>#R$E72F</td><td>32 (0 busy)</td><td>#R$9D13</td><td>checks where people are: room 8 (your room)<br>moves things: 26 note to room 8 (your room), self to room 0 (off stage)<ul><li>#R$E72F: &quot;WATSON LOOKS UP FROM THE PAPER AND SAYS&#x27; GOOD MORNING HOLMES.&#x27;&quot;</li><li>#R$E742: &quot;WITH AN EXCITED LOOK ON HIS FACE YOU SAYS&#x27; WELL HERE IS A CASE THAT MAY INTEREST YOU, IT SEEMS THAT TWO CLO...&quot;</li><li>#R$E773: &quot;YOU LOOKS UP AS YOU ENTER SAYING&#x27; HELLO THERE HOLMES, JUST A MINUTE NOW- HERE IS A CASE THAT MAY INTEREST Y...&quot;</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td>4 chief constable straker</td><td>room 53 (leatherhead station)</td><td>#R$EBFA</td><td>76 (70 busy)</td><td>#R$9D30</td><td>signals inspector lestrade; signals strachan; signals phipps; signals gardener; signals cook; signals maid; signals local police man; signals local police man; waits for inspector lestrade<br>checks where people are: room 54 (platform one)<br>moves things: self to room 53 (leatherhead station)<ul><li>#R$EBFE: &quot;HELLO INSPECTOR, GOOD OF YOU TO COME SO QUICKLY.&quot;</li><li>#R$EC08: &quot;DAMNED STICKY BUSINESS THIS, WE RECIEVED A REPORT EARLY THIS MORNING FROM THE GARDENER UP AT THE BROWN&#x27;S ES...&quot;</li><li>#R$EC17: &quot;MRS BROWN&#x27;S HUSBAND WAS KILLED ABOUT FIVE YEARS AGO, HE WAS A TOP SCIENTIST WORKING ON SOME SECRET PROJECT....&quot;</li><li>#R$EC1F: &quot;YES INSPECTOR, UNFORTUNATELY NOTHING WAS FOUND.&quot;</li><li>#R$EC38: &quot;INSPECTOR I WOULD LIKE YOU TO MEET BASIL PHIPPS AND DAPHNE STRACHAN. BASIL, DAPHNE THIS IS INSPECTOR LESTRA...&quot;</li><li>#R$EC69: &quot;MRS JONES WAS A WIDOW, HER HUSBAND HAVING DIED ABOUT EIGHT YEARS AGO. AT THE TIME SHE CAME HERE TO LIVE NEA...&quot;</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td>5 strachan</td><td>room 67 (sitting room)</td><td>#R$F017</td><td>17 (10 busy)</td><td>#R$9EC1</td><td>waits for chief constable straker<br>checks where people are: room 63 (browns front door)<ul><li>#R$F023: &quot;YOU SEEMS AT A LOSS, LOOKING FORLORN AND CRYING QUIETLY TO HERSELF.&quot;</li><li>#R$F031: &quot;YOU SEEMS AT A LOSS, LOOKING FORLORN AND CRYING QUIETLY TO HERSELF.&quot;</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td>6 phipps</td><td>room 67 (sitting room)</td><td>#R$EE2B</td><td>96 (28 busy)</td><td>#R$9CA2</td><td>waits for 7am, 9am, 1pm, 3pm, 4pm, 10pm, 11pm<br>signals german agent; waits for chief constable straker<br>checks where people are: room 23 (platform four), room 32 (camden street), room 38 (basils library), room 39 (basils yard), room 102 (basils front yard), room 103 (old mill road)<br>moves things: 81 torn coded ?0f8 note to room 0 (off stage), 83 ripped coded baker note to room 0 (off stage), self to room 0 (off stage), self to room 33 (entrance hall), self to room 35 (basils bedroom), self to room 50 (main street), self to room 61 (basils bedroom), self to room 103 (old mill road)<br>can end the game: #R$9E48, #R$DFCA<ul><li>#R$EF47: &quot;AS YOU ENTER YOU WHIPS HIS HORSES INTO ACTION AND SPEEDS OFF DOWN THE ROAD.&quot;</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td>7 major percival ffoulkes</td><td>room 0 (off stage)</td><td>#R$F062</td><td>46 (33 busy)</td><td>-</td><td>waits for 1am, 11pm<br>signals inspector lestrade; waits for inspector lestrade<br>moves things: self to room 0 (off stage), self to room 40 (sidmouth street), self to room 92 (jones front gate)<ul><li>#R$F06A: &quot;PLEASE LET ME PAST OFFICER, I MUST SEE MRS JONES.&quot;</li><li>#R$F073: &quot;INSPECTOR I AM YOU A VERY CLOSE AQUAINTANCE OF MRS JONES AND I DEMAND TO SEE HER AT ONCE.&quot;</li><li>#R$F07D: &quot;UPON SEEING THE BODY MAJOR FFOULKES TURNS DEATHLY PALE, THEN, RECOVERING, TURNS AND SAYS&#x27; INSPECTOR LESTRAD...&quot;</li><li>#R$F0E2: &quot;WELL HOLMES YOU HAVE FOUND OUT MY SHAMEFUL SECRET, I AM ADDICTED TO THIS VILE OPIUM AND I CONFESS, THIS IS...&quot;</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td>8 gardener</td><td>room 65 (large kitchen)</td><td>#R$ED4B</td><td>35 (2 busy)</td><td>#R$9ED1</td><td>waits for chief constable straker</td></tr><tr><td>9 cook</td><td>room 65 (large kitchen)</td><td>#R$EDF8</td><td>9 (4 busy)</td><td>#R$9ECB</td><td>waits for chief constable straker</td></tr><tr><td>10 maid</td><td>room 65 (large kitchen)</td><td>#R$EE1F</td><td>4 (2 busy)</td><td>-</td><td>waits for chief constable straker<br>moves things: self to room 0 (off stage)</td></tr><tr><td>11 local police man</td><td>room 92 (jones front gate)</td><td>#R$F110</td><td>15 (11 busy)</td><td>#R$9D48</td><td>waits for inspector lestrade; waits for chief constable straker<br>moves things: self to room 0 (off stage)<ul><li>#R$F114: &quot;INSPECTOR, THIS GENTLEMAN INSISTS HE KNOWS MRS JONES AND WISHES TO ENTER THE HOUSE.&quot;</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td>12 local police man</td><td>room 62 (browns front gate)</td><td>#R$F145</td><td>12 (8 busy)</td><td>#R$9D73</td><td>waits for chief constable straker<br>moves things: self to room 0 (off stage)</td></tr><tr><td>13 tricia fender</td><td>room 0 (off stage)</td><td>#R$F15A</td><td>14 (0 busy)</td><td>-</td><td>waits for 1am, 8am, 2pm<br>checks where people are: room 11 (scotland yard)<br>moves things: self to room 0 (off stage), self to room 45 (portman street)</td></tr><tr><td>31 cabbie, 34 cabbie</td><td>room 0 (off stage), room 0 (off stage)</td><td>#R$F2B2</td><td>2 (0 busy)</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>36 steam train</td><td>room 2 (steam train)</td><td>#R$F21D</td><td>11 (0 busy)</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>37 steam train</td><td>room 2 (steam train)</td><td>#R$F23A</td><td>11 (0 busy)</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>38 steam train</td><td>room 2 (steam train)</td><td>#R$F22D</td><td>11 (0 busy)</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>39 steam train</td><td>room 2 (steam train)</td><td>#R$F246</td><td>12 (0 busy)</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>40 steam train</td><td>room 2 (steam train)</td><td>#R$F268</td><td>12 (0 busy)</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>41 steam train</td><td>room 2 (steam train)</td><td>#R$F25B</td><td>12 (0 busy)</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>42 steam train</td><td>room 3 (steam train)</td><td>#R$F278</td><td>11 (0 busy)</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>43 steam train</td><td>room 3 (steam train)</td><td>#R$F28D</td><td>11 (0 busy)</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>104 german agent</td><td>room 0 (off stage)</td><td>#R$EFF6</td><td>30 (1 busy)</td><td>-</td><td>waits for 3pm, 4pm<br>waits for phipps<br>checks where people are: room 23 (platform four), room 103 (old mill road)<br>moves things: self to room 0 (off stage), self to room 50 (main street), self to room 103 (old mill road)<br>can end the game: #R$9E48</td></tr></table>

[Page:how-it-works]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(how-it-works-content)

[how-it-works-content]
<p>Sherlock (Melbourne House, 1984) is a text adventure written as one block of Z80 code and data that loads at #R$5D80 and starts at #R$A040. The game is a clockwork: every person, including the player, is an <i>actor</i> that runs a small program, and a single loop gives each actor a turn as the minutes go by. Almost nothing is special-cased for the player.</p><h3>Memory map</h3><table class="map"><tr><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>What is there</th></tr><tr><td>#R$5D80</td><td>#R$5DBE($5DBF)</td><td>The dictionary index: where the words starting with each letter begin.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$5DC0</td><td>#R$67B4($67B6)</td><td>The dictionary: 592 words, five bits to a letter.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$67B7</td><td>#R$8258</td><td>Every message the game prints, as bytecode.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$8259</td><td>#R$8409($840A)</td><td>The verb patterns: the verb number is the position in this table.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$840B</td><td>#R$8B6E</td><td>The room table and the 106 room records.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$8B6F</td><td>#R$956A($956F)</td><td>The entity index and the entity records: every person, object and door.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$9570</td><td>#R$9858($985F)</td><td>The node heap, 752 bytes, for the engine's own linked lists.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$9860</td><td>#R$9963($9964)</td><td>The actor table, the player's plan script and the case's relation words.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$9965</td><td>#R$9F37</td><td>The case table, and the routines that run when a statement is proved or a character has taken a turn.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$9F38</td><td>#R$A03A($A03F)</td><td>The default action record for each verb, the table of script-only verbs and the game variables.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$A040</td><td>#R$A59D($A59E)</td><td>Start-up and the main loop, the heap, the narration of commands.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$A59F</td><td>#R$AA6E($AA70)</td><td>The message interpreter, the word printer and the word-wrapping character printer.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$AA71</td><td>#R$B18D($B190)</td><td>The shared action records and the parser.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$B191</td><td>#R$BA1B($BA1F)</td><td>Matching a typed command against the verb patterns; orders and remarks to other people.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$BA20</td><td>#R$BF6E($BF78)</td><td>Watson's and Lestrade's replies to what the player says: the case.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$BF79</td><td>#R$C492</td><td>The clock, saving and loading, the screen layout and the command window.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$C493</td><td>#R$CC0B($CC0D)</td><td>Carrying out a command: action records, checks and the report of what happened.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$CC0E</td><td>#R$D6BD($D6BE)</td><td>The actor's turn and the plan-script interpreter, the random numbers, light, and room descriptions.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$D6BF</td><td>#R$DC13($DC14)</td><td>The player's turn: the line editor, the tokenizer and the number and money readers.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$DC15</td><td>#R$F3C1</td><td>Action records, sub-scripts and every character's plan script.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$F3C2</td><td>#R$F60A</td><td>The picture interpreter.</td></tr><tr><td>#R$F60B</td><td>#R$FFFF</td><td>Which rooms have a picture, and the picture data.</td></tr></table><h3>A turn</h3><p>The main loop (#R$A064) is one minute of game time per pass. It advances and prints the clock (#R$BFD9, #R$BF79), then walks the actor table at #R$9860 and gives a turn to every actor whose next-turn time has come. A turn begins by finding the actor (#R$CC0E) and asking whether an order or remark is waiting for it (#R$CC37, #R$B91F). If so that is dealt with first (#R$B936). Otherwise the actor's plan script runs one step at a time (#R$CC78) until a step takes time. The minutes it took are added to the actor's next-turn time and the actor's own after-turn routine, if it has one, runs (#R$9C77). Typing WAIT costs the player five minutes; TAKE LAMP costs one. When the game ends, #R$9E74 prints the ending and the game starts again.</p><p>The player is actor 0. The player's plan script (#R$9921) reads a line from the keyboard with a time limit (#R$D898) and WAITs if nothing is typed, splits the line into words (#R$D715), parses one sentence (#R$AAB2) and carries it out. A line of several sentences is worked through one a minute. Typed words are first turned into tokens (#R$D9C5): dictionary words, numbers, clock times and old-style money such as 2/6 (#R$DB67).</p><p>A command, typed or from a script, is a verb and up to two objects. #R$B191 matches the words against the verb patterns; #R$C4D1 finds the action record to use. A verb has a default record (#R$9F38) and an object can have its own list of shared records (#R$AA71). The record is run by #R$C9BF: it names what each object must be (here, carried, a person, a door, a number: #R$C628), tests flags on the objects, may call a pre-check routine, and then runs either a body of code or a sub-script. Afterwards #R$CA60 reports what the player sees: the command as a sentence (#R$A2E4), a new room (#R$CA7F) or someone arriving (#R$CAAF).</p><h3>The world</h3><p>Things, people and doors are entity records (#R$8CBA), found through an index (#R$8B6F). Each has a holder (a room, another thing, or a person), flags such as visible, a person, open, locked and seen, and a room. Rooms are records of their own (#R$84E1) with their exits. Whether the player can see is decided by #R$D078: outdoors between eight in the evening and six in the morning the player needs a light, and the oil lamp is lit from the start. #R$D0DD builds the description of the room the player is in.</p><p>The engine also needs lists that grow and shrink while the game runs: orders and remarks waiting for actors, tokens from the typed line, countdowns, sub-script call frames. They are linked lists in a small heap (#R$9570) managed by #R$A11C and #R$A1C7.</p><h3>The characters</h3><p>Every character's behaviour is a plan script in a small bytecode that #R$CC78 interprets: DO a command, RUN an action record, ASK someone to do something, SAY or PRINT a message, SIGNAL and AWAIT another character, RETURN from a sub-script, PASS, GOTO. Each step can branch on success or failure. A step that succeeds after taking time ends the actor's turn; a failure moves on to the next step at once, so a step that fails to itself means "wait here until this works". Verbs from 128 up (#R$9FAB) exist only for scripts: they test where people are, the hour and chance, and move things about.</p><p>Because characters use the same commands as the player, they can do what the player can: open doors, take cabs and trains, follow someone, and be given orders. SAY TO a person hands them what was said (#R$B86A). Some steps are marked busy, and a character on one of them refuses what the player asks (#R$CBA7). Each of nine characters also has an after-turn routine (#R$9C85) for greetings, warnings and the like. See <a href="cast.html">The cast and their scripts</a> for every script.</p><h3>Text</h3><p>Dictionary words are stored five bits to a letter (#R$A880 unpacks them), so a typed word is looked up through the per-letter index at #R$5D80. Messages are small programs (#R$A59F runs them): ordinary bytes are letters, bytes from $60 up stand for common words, and control codes handle things like choosing one of several phrases at random (#R$A6D3), articles (#R$A7EB), capital letters and what a character says. The parameters of a message are pushed on the stack before the call. Output goes through a word-wrapping printer (#R$A9B7) and a character routine that writes to the screen (#R$AA14); the command window echoes what is typed (#R$C1FC).</p><h3>Pictures</h3><p>Fourteen rooms have a picture, drawn when the player arrives from a compact list of drawing commands (#R$F3C2): move the pen, draw a line (#R$F56E), fill an area (#R$F490), colour blocks of attributes. Rooms 54 and 55 share one picture. See <a href="pictures.html">The pictures</a>.</p><h3>Chance</h3><p>The random number generator (#R$D2AD) is seeded from the Z80 refresh register at the first keypress. It steps a pointer through memory and mixes the bytes it finds, never giving the same number twice running. Script chances (#R$F198), countdowns and the greeting delays of the after-turn routines all use it.</p><h3>The case</h3><p>The solution is data. #R$9965 lists statements such as MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF, each with the evidence needed to prove it: things the player must have seen or examined, or markers that only the game can set. Telling Lestrade a statement (#R$BB67) proves it if the evidence is there and runs the statement's routine (#R$99FC); otherwise it waits and is proved as soon as the evidence is complete (#R$BC46). Telling Watson (#R$BA67) only makes him remember it. Lestrade must be met, with Chief Constable Straker, by three on Monday afternoon (#R$9DFF). The endings depend on what has been proved (#R$9EA2, #R$9E85) and on who is with the player when Basil arrives (#R$9E48). See <a href="the-case.html">The case</a> for every statement and its evidence.</p><h3>Saving</h3><p>The game state is one block of 7,188 bytes from #R$840B to #R$A01A($A01F): the room and entity records, the heap, the actor table, the case table and the game variables. SAVE (#R$B0C6) sets a flag, and at the end of that minute the main loop writes the block to tape (#R$B0E4, #R$C040) and checks it. LOAD, and a game that has ended, read the block back (#R$B0D7) and start again from #R$A040.</p>

[Page:pictures]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(pictures-content)

[pictures-content]
<p>The 13 room pictures, drawn by the game's own picture interpreter (#R$F3C2) running in the emulator with the Spectrum ROM loaded (tools/draw_pictures.py; tools/package.sh regenerates them). Each picture fills the top right quarter of the screen; only 14 of the rooms have one (#R$F60C), rooms 54 and 55 sharing a picture. Every one ran to its end marker and returned, which confirms the picture-data boundaries used in this disassembly.</p><p>And the whole game screen, from the emulator with the ROM loaded and pictures switched on (tools/experiments/screen_test.py): the player moved to Baker Street and typed LOOK. The room text wraps in the narrow window beside the picture, the clock sits in the bar, and the commands scroll below.</p><p><img src="pictures/screen.png" width="512" height="384" alt="The game screen on Baker Street"></p><div style="display:inline-block;margin:6px;vertical-align:top;width:260px"><img src="pictures/pic-F638.png" width="256" height="256" alt="picture"><br>#R$F638: room 12 (slater street)</div><div style="display:inline-block;margin:6px;vertical-align:top;width:260px"><img src="pictures/pic-F6ED.png" width="256" height="256" alt="picture"><br>#R$F6ED: room 61 (basils bedroom)</div><div style="display:inline-block;margin:6px;vertical-align:top;width:260px"><img src="pictures/pic-F7BE.png" width="256" height="256" alt="picture"><br>#R$F7BE: room 95 (small room)</div><div style="display:inline-block;margin:6px;vertical-align:top;width:260px"><img src="pictures/pic-F868.png" width="256" height="256" alt="picture"><br>#R$F868: room 92 (jones front gate)</div><div style="display:inline-block;margin:6px;vertical-align:top;width:260px"><img src="pictures/pic-F928.png" width="256" height="256" alt="picture"><br>#R$F928: room 6 (entrance hallway)</div><div style="display:inline-block;margin:6px;vertical-align:top;width:260px"><img src="pictures/pic-F9F0.png" width="256" height="256" alt="picture"><br>#R$F9F0: room 62 (browns front gate)</div><div style="display:inline-block;margin:6px;vertical-align:top;width:260px"><img src="pictures/pic-FAAA.png" width="256" height="256" alt="picture"><br>#R$FAAA: room 11 (scotland yard)</div><div style="display:inline-block;margin:6px;vertical-align:top;width:260px"><img src="pictures/pic-FB6E.png" width="256" height="256" alt="picture"><br>#R$FB6E: room 54 (platform one), room 55 (platform two)</div><div style="display:inline-block;margin:6px;vertical-align:top;width:260px"><img src="pictures/pic-FC3A.png" width="256" height="256" alt="picture"><br>#R$FC3A: room 88 (east side)</div><div style="display:inline-block;margin:6px;vertical-align:top;width:260px"><img src="pictures/pic-FCED.png" width="256" height="256" alt="picture"><br>#R$FCED: room 89 (west side)</div><div style="display:inline-block;margin:6px;vertical-align:top;width:260px"><img src="pictures/pic-FDA8.png" width="256" height="256" alt="picture"><br>#R$FDA8: room 71 (browns library)</div><div style="display:inline-block;margin:6px;vertical-align:top;width:260px"><img src="pictures/pic-FE82.png" width="256" height="256" alt="picture"><br>#R$FE82: room 74 (daphnes room)</div><div style="display:inline-block;margin:6px;vertical-align:top;width:260px"><img src="pictures/pic-FF47.png" width="256" height="256" alt="picture"><br>#R$FF47: room 5 (baker street)</div>

[Page:the-case]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(the-case-content)

[the-case-content]
<p><b>Update, 8 November night:</b> the whole chain on this page - every statement proven, including THE SALE LOCATION IS OLD MILL ROAD - has since been earned by typed commands only (no evidence or markers poked), and it ends in the win: see <a href="the-walkthrough.html">The walkthrough, Monday to the win</a>. The proof of the sale location needs the burning note examined before it burns to ashes (about 10:24am on Wednesday); the cure is DROP then a plain TAKE (fact 'The burning note...').</p><p>The solution, as the game stores it (#R$9965). Say a statement to Inspector Lestrade (SAY TO LESTRADE "MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF"); if all its evidence holds it is proven and its routine runs, otherwise it waits and is proven later, as soon as the evidence is complete (#R$BC46). Evidence is "seen" (the player has seen both things), "examined" (has examined both), or a marker that only the code named can set. Proven or not, the statements decide the endings (#R$9EA2, #R$9E85).</p><p>Confirmed in the emulator: MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF refused without its evidence and accepted with it; the Major's two statements proven together once his confession marker is set; TRICIA FENDER KILLED MRS JONES only ever waiting. And the whole chain (tools/experiments/solve_test.py, with every piece of evidence poked in, the #R$9993 marker poked in, and Lestrade parked on the idle loop at #R$F05C so that he stays to listen): MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF, MRS JONES KILLED TRICIA FENDER, PHIPPS HAS PLANS ("Very good Holmes, Basil will likely try to sell them..."), THE SALE LOCATION IS OLD MILL ROAD, TRICIA FENDER HAD PLANS ("I do not see the importance of that") and TRICIA FENDER BLACKMAILED MRS BROWN are each proven in turn; MAJOR FFOULKES VISITED THE OPIUM DEN, said on its own, gets "Thats fine Holmes but what does it prove". The rest is read from the table and the code.</p><p>Markers set by shared actions 3 and 22 come from Tricia Fender's and Mrs Jones's own action lists, which are used when that person is the one acting (#R$C933). <b>Tricia Fender is Mrs Jones in disguise.</b> Ask her about the bloodstained clothes while they are in the room (SAY TO TRICIA FENDER "TELL ME ABOUT BLOODSTAINED CLOTHES") and shared action 3 sets #R$998F and turns her actor-table entry into thing 95, Mrs Jones (#R$E03A): "Oh my god, you found out, I am really Mrs Jones." Now Mrs Jones is an actor, and SAY TO MRS JONES "TELL ME ABOUT YOUR ALIBI" (or WHAT HAPPENED) runs shared action 22 and sets #R$9993 - both confirmed in the emulator (the scratchpad test of 8 November evening). An earlier version of this page called #R$9993 unreachable; that was wrong.</p><p>What the proofs are for. Proving PHIPPS HAS PLANS and THE SALE LOCATION IS OLD MILL ROAD sends Lestrade to Old Mill Road in the police cab (#R$E7E7). Phipps waits there on Wednesday from 12:10 until about 3:20pm and bolts if you turn up after 3pm; he then makes his way to platform one at Victoria (#R$EE2B), and if you are there with Watson and Lestrade when he walks in, you win (#R$9E48 - staged in the emulator, and reached by play with the statements poked proven: see #R$E7E7). If the statements are not proven, the papers end the game at 10am on Thursday (#R$9EA2). See <a href="reference/facts.html#the_last_scene">How the game is won</a>.</p><table class="disassembly"><tr><th>Record</th><th>Statement</th><th>Evidence</th><th>When proven</th></tr><tr><td>#R$9965</td><td>MAJOR PERCIVAL FFOULKES IS INNOCENT</td><td><ul><li>#R$996B: the statement MAJOR PERCIVAL FFOULKES VISITED OPIUM DEN, proven in its own right (tell it to Lestrade); its evidence follows</li><li>#R$996F: a marker - set by the Major's confession in the opium den (#R$F103)</li></ul></td><td>#R$9A3D: "VERY GOOD HOLMES, I SHALL RETURN TO LEATHER HEAD TOMORROW TO HAVE ANOTHER LOOK AROUND." - sets #R$9E87($9E88), so Lestrade will not arrest the Major (#R$9E85)</td></tr><tr><td>#R$9973</td><td>MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF</td><td><ul><li>#R$9979: female corpse and sandstone bridge examined</li><li>#R$997D: large rock and large rock seen</li></ul></td><td>#R$9A4B: "SO MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF, EXCELLENT HOLMES, ANOTHER BRILLIANT CONCLUSION."</td></tr><tr><td>#R$9981</td><td>MRS JONES KILLED TRICIA FENDER</td><td><ul><li>#R$9987: body and bookcase examined</li><li>#R$998B: bloodstained clothes and bloodstained clothes examined</li><li>#R$998F: a marker - set by #R$E1CB in shared action 3, which Tricia Fender performs when asked to tell about the bloodstained clothes away from them</li><li>#R$9993: a marker - set by #R$E199 in shared action 22, which Mrs Jones performs when asked what happened or about her alibi - she becomes an actor once Tricia Fender, asked about the bloodstained clothes, turns out to be her (#R$E03A)</li></ul></td><td>#R$9A59: "BRILLIANT HOLMES, I DO NOT KNOW HOW YOU DO IT.&#x27;. HE CALLS FOR A CONSTABLE WHO LEADS THE VERY DISTRESSED MRS JONES OUT OF THE ROOM INTO THE DEPTHS OF SCOTLAND YARD." - Mrs Jones is led away (moved off stage)</td></tr><tr><td>#R$9997</td><td>PHIPPS HAS PLANS</td><td><ul><li>#R$999D: unfinished note and folder examined</li><li>#R$99A1: letters and ripped coded baker note examined</li><li>#R$99A5: torn coded ?0f8 note and torn coded ?0f8 note examined</li></ul></td><td>#R$9A13: "VERY GOOD HOLMES, BASIL WILL LIKELY TRY TO SELL THEM, WE MUST FIND WHERE THE SALE LOCATION IS." - only if both murders are already proven; otherwise it is un-proven again</td></tr><tr><td>#R$99A9</td><td>SALE LOCATION IS OLD MILL ROAD</td><td><ul><li>#R$99AF: a marker - set by proving PHIPPS HAS PLANS (#R$9A13)</li><li>#R$99B3: burning note and burning note examined</li><li>#R$99B7: a marker - already set at the start; cleared when the burning note burns (#R$9CA2)</li></ul></td><td>#R$99FC: "RIGHT THEN HOLMES WE MUST HURRY IF WE ARE TO CATCH THEM."</td></tr><tr><td>#R$99BB</td><td>TRICIA FENDER KILLED MRS BROWN</td><td><ul><li>#R$99C1: a marker - nothing sets it, so this can never be proven</li></ul></td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>#R$99C5</td><td>TRICIA FENDER KILLED MRS JONES</td><td><ul><li>#R$99CB: a marker - nothing sets it, so this can never be proven</li></ul></td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>#R$99CF</td><td>PHIPPS KILLED MRS BROWN</td><td><ul><li>#R$99D5: a marker - nothing sets it, so this can never be proven</li></ul></td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>#R$99D9</td><td>PHIPPS KILLED MRS JONES</td><td><ul><li>#R$99DF: a marker - nothing sets it, so this can never be proven</li></ul></td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>#R$99E3</td><td>TRICIA FENDER HAD PLANS</td><td><ul><li>#R$99E9: bank account ?01d book and note examined</li><li>#R$99ED: unfinished note and folder examined</li></ul></td><td>#R$9A05: "I DO NOT SEE THE IMPORTANCE OF THAT HOLMES." - also establishes the evidence for TRICIA FENDER BLACKMAILED MRS BROWN</td></tr><tr><td>#R$99F1</td><td>TRICIA FENDER BLACKMAILED MRS BROWN</td><td><ul><li>#R$99F7: a marker - set by proving TRICIA FENDER HAD PLANS (#R$9A05)</li></ul></td><td>#R$9A0A: "I DO NOT SEE THE IMPORTANCE OF THAT HOLMES."</td></tr></table>

[Page:the-endgame]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(the-endgame-content)

[the-endgame-content]
<p><b>Update:</b> this page describes the first staged win, with help (statements poked, Watson placed). The same ending has since been reached with typed commands only from a fresh boot: see the page <a href="the-walkthrough.html">The walkthrough, Monday to the win</a> and the fact 'The winning line from Wednesday 10:53am, typed'.</p><p>The game was won in the emulator by playing this line from Holmes's rooms at 11am on Wednesday (tools/experiments/chase.py, stages A B2 C2 D E F2 G3). Help it had: the case statements poked proven (the Mrs Jones marker #R$9993 is earnable - see The case), the two flags a player earns earlier (#R$9D90, Lestrade's introduction; #R$9E87($9E88), the Major - both since earned without pokes, see A playthrough without pokes) and Watson put beside you. Every command below was typed. Times are the game clock after each step.</p><table class="disassembly"><tr><th>Time</th><th>Where</th><th>Commands</th><th>What happens</th></tr><tr><td>11:00am Wed</td><td>Holmes's rooms</td><td>OPEN YOUR DOOR, WEST, DOWN, OPEN FRONT DOOR, SOUTH</td><td>Baker Street (5)</td></tr><tr><td>11:09</td><td>Baker Street</td><td>HAIL CAB, CLIMB INTO HANSOM CAB, SAY TO CABBIE &quot;GO TO KINGS CROSS ROAD&quot;</td><td>12 minutes and a fare (#R$9B7F, #R$9B25) (#R$9AF4)</td></tr><tr><td>11:25</td><td>Kings Cross Road</td><td>CLIMB OUT, NORTHEAST four times</td><td>Platform three (22), with Lestrade arriving too</td></tr><tr><td>12:17</td><td>Platform three</td><td>CLIMB INTO TRAIN</td><td>The Leatherhead line, every 90 minutes (#R$F278)</td></tr><tr><td>1:32pm</td><td>Leatherhead platform one</td><td>CLIMB OUT, SOUTH, SOUTH</td><td>Main Street (50) at 1:36; the police cab is waiting</td></tr><tr><td>1:38</td><td>Main Street</td><td>CLIMB INTO POLICE CAB in the minute Lestrade does (his script on #R$EA69)</td><td>You, Watson and Lestrade fit; Straker is left out (#R$E7E7)</td></tr><tr><td>3:13</td><td>Old Mill Road</td><td>(wait)</td><td>Phipps bolts at 3:14: &#x27;As you enter Basil Phipps whips his horses...&#x27; (#R$EE2B)</td></tr><tr><td>4:47</td><td>Main Street</td><td>CLIMB OUT, NORTH three times</td><td>Phipps catches the 4:55 to London; Lestrade says Basil has eluded you</td></tr><tr><td>4:56</td><td>Leatherhead platform two</td><td>SAY TO LESTRADE &quot;FOLLOW ME&quot;</td><td>The first moment his script is not busy (#R$EAD0)</td></tr><tr><td>5:00</td><td></td><td>SOUTH three times, CLIMB INTO POLICE CAB</td><td>Lestrade and Watson follow you in</td></tr><tr><td>5:07</td><td>Main Street</td><td>SAY TO POLICE MAN &quot;GO FURIOUSLY TO KINGS CROSS ROAD&quot;</td><td>Verb #R$9ACB(30): 74 minutes, not 94 (#R$9ACB)</td></tr><tr><td>6:22</td><td>Kings Cross Road</td><td>CLIMB OUT, NORTHEAST, NORTHEAST</td><td>Kings Cross platform one (20)</td></tr><tr><td>6:28</td><td>Platform one</td><td>CLIMB INTO TRAIN</td><td>The underground train: Paddington, then Victoria (#R$F21D)</td></tr><tr><td>6:40</td><td>Victoria platform one</td><td>(wait)</td><td>A German agent is waiting (#R$EFF6)</td></tr><tr><td>6:42</td><td></td><td></td><td>&#x27;Basil Phipps enters.&#x27; - and the winning ending (#R$9E48)</td></tr></table><p>Why each piece matters: Phipps only bolts if you are at Old Mill Road between 3pm and about 3:16 (#R$EE2B), and only the police cab goes there; Lestrade's script is busy from the moment he sets off until he stands on platform two at 4:56 (#R$E7E7); the next Leatherhead train after Phipps's is 90 minutes behind (#R$F278); plain GO TO in the police cab arrives at 6:42, as Basil does, and both hansom cabs are on the road then. GO FURIOUSLY TO and the underground train leave two minutes to spare. The last scene itself is #R$9E48.</p>

[Page:the-playthrough]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(the-playthrough-content)

[the-playthrough-content]
<p>Everything on this page was typed into the emulator from a fresh boot. The commands are in <code>tools/experiments/playthrough/monday_to_major.cmds</code> and <code>tools/experiments/replay_log.py</code> replays them (the game is deterministic). It stops at <b>Tuesday 12:14am</b>. The game was finished later: parts 2 and 3 and the full walkthrough page carry on.</p><table><tr><th>Clock</th><th>What happened</th></tr><tr><td>Mon 8:02</td><td>Start in Holmes's sitting room. Lamp taken; WEAR CHINA MANS DISGUISE and TAKE OFF in the closet (see the fact about the disguise); SAY TO WATSON "FOLLOW ME".</td></tr><tr><td>8:26-9:59</td><td>OPEN YOUR DOOR, WEST, DOWN, OPEN FRONT DOOR, SOUTH, HAIL CAB, CLIMB INTO HANSOM CAB, SAY TO CABBIE "GO TO KINGS CROSS ROAD"; the ride takes about 80 minutes if the cab really moves (see the stale-quotes fact); on arrival PAY 10D TO CABBIE (never GIVE MONEY).</td></tr><tr><td>10:45-12:00</td><td>Four NORTHEAST to platform three, the steam train at 10:45, Leather Head platform one at 12:00.</td></tr><tr><td>12:27</td><td>At Browns Front Gate Lestrade and Straker arrive and Lestrade introduces Straker (#R$9D90 = 1); the gate is passable from then on.</td></tr><tr><td>12:42-12:58</td><td>Room 88: female corpse and sandstone bridge examined closely, crumpled note taken and read ("Meet me by the bridge at half past twelve... P. F.").</td></tr><tr><td>1:25-1:51</td><td>Jones's house. Lestrade, Straker and the Major are in the library at 1:43; SAY TO LESTRADE "MAJOR PERCIVAL FFOULKES IS INNOCENT" makes the statement wait. Body, bookcase and the bloodstained clothes (labelled "Tricia. Fender.") examined closely.</td></tr><tr><td>2:22-2:51</td><td>Brown's study: drawer, false bottom, bank account book and note examined closely.</td></tr><tr><td>3:15-4:30</td><td>The 3:25 train from platform two, shared with Lestrade, to Kings Cross platform four.</td></tr><tr><td>4:47-11:52pm</td><td>Hansom cab to Slater Street. (My wait loop overshot: the cab, shared with Lestrade, was at Parliament Street by 11:31pm; a second ride, 27 minutes, got to Slater Street at 11:52.)</td></tr><tr><td>Tue 12:00-12:14</td><td>The den, the confession and Lestrade's "Very good Holmes": the Major is cleared (#R$9E87($9E88) = 1).</td></tr></table><p>Evidence flags at the end (read from memory, only to confirm): female corpse, sandstone bridge, body, bookcase, bloodstained clothes, bank account book and note all examined; Straker introduced; Major cleared. Not yet: the large rock (Tuesday, see the fact about it), the unfinished note and folder (Tricia's lounge, Tuesday 8:10am-2pm), the letters (Basil's safe at Cobden Lane, not on Monday), the two coded notes and the burning note (Basil's Camden Street house), and the markers #R$998F and #R$9993.</p><h3>What the run corrected</h3><ul><li>The wait loop of the first attempt ran through a whole evening because its stop word was split by the line wrap: stop words must be one word that cannot wrap.</li><li>The 4:55pm Leatherhead train does arrive at platform four at 6:00pm, as the timetable fact says.</li><li>Lestrade rides the 3:25 train from Leatherhead to Kings Cross with the player.</li><li>The 1am Tuesday ending (#R$9E85) really happens: waiting in Camden Street at 1:07am on a line that had not cleared the Major ended the game with Lestrade's "that wraps up this case".</li><li>The opium den and the Major's innocence are reachable without pokes, but only with the disguise.</li></ul><h3>Where it was blocked (since solved)</h3><p>PHIPPS HAS PLANS needs the ripped and torn coded notes in Basil's Camden Street house. Walking in as Holmes fails; the way in is the china man's disguise through the window at 10:03pm on Tuesday (part 2). The idea of sending Lestrade in was never needed.</p>

[Page:the-playthrough-2]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(the-playthrough-2-content)

[the-playthrough-2-content]
<p>Continuing the position of A playthrough without pokes (Tuesday 12:50am), by typed commands: wait in Camden Street; Phipps arrives at 9:35am; at 10:03pm the window opens; WEAR CHINA MANS DISGUISE, NORTHEAST, collect the ripped and torn coded notes as described in the fact about Basil's Camden Street house. That run waited through Tuesday, so it missed what Tuesday is for (Tricia on stage 8:05am-2pm; the large rock; the letters), and it is a test of the entry, not the final line. The route to try next: Monday as before but carrying the bloodstained clothes away from Jones's house, Tuesday morning Tricia, then Leatherhead for the rock and letters, then back to Camden Street before 10pm.</p>

[Page:the-walkthrough]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(the-walkthrough-content)

[the-walkthrough-content]
<p>Two routes win the game, both from a fresh start by typing commands only. This page follows one of them from Monday 8:02am to the arrest at Victoria on Wednesday 6:43pm: 372 typed lines in 32 stretches, every one listed. In each table, <em>Step</em> is the line number, <em>Type</em> is exactly what is typed, <em>Clock after</em> is the game clock once the command has run, <em>Where</em> is the room you are in (&ldquo;in disguise&rdquo; while the china man's disguise is on) and the last column is the game's own reply, shortened. &times;N means the line is repeated N times until the reply shows the quoted text. The game is deterministic, so typing these lines from a fresh start gives the same result.</p><p>The winning log is <code>v12_clean_win.cmds</code> (372 lines); <code>v13_shadow_lestrade.cmds</code> is a second route that differs from 10:53am on Wednesday (see the trivia entry about it). Replay one with <code>python3 tools/experiments/replay_log.py tools/experiments/playthrough/v12_clean_win.cmds</code> (about 20 seconds), or with <code>replay_verbose.py</code> to print every reply. The rows below were produced from a replay of that log by <code>tools/experiments/walkthrough_steps.py</code>. The log was shortened by <code>tools/experiments/minimize_cmds.py</code>, which only tried to remove LOOK, INVENTORY and error-reply lines; every line was afterwards tested on its own by <code>tools/experiments/ablate_cmds.py</code> and the results are in the last column.</p><h2>How exact the route is</h2><p>Typed exactly as listed from a fresh start, these 372 lines win every time. The last column of each table comes from a check: the game was started again from scratch and the route typed in with that one line left out and everything else unchanged. <strong>39 lines still win without it</strong> and <strong>333 lose</strong> (the game ends another way, or the final scene never comes). &ldquo;Loses&rdquo; says only that the exact sequence breaks; it cannot tell a line that matters to the story from one that only keeps the clock and the game&rsquo;s random numbers in step, because leaving out one line moves every later minute.</p><ul><li><strong>Lines that can go.</strong> Taking out all 39 of the lines that win alone, at once, loses. Taking them out one after another, and keeping each removal only if the route still won, removed 29 lines in all, leaving a 343-line route that still wins from a fresh start. The lines removed were: 2 <code>WAIT</code>, 98 <code>OPEN WOODEN DOOR</code>, 101 <code>OPEN WOODEN DOOR</code>, 111 <code>OPEN WOODEN DOOR</code>, 150 <code>WEAR CHINA MANS DISGUISE</code>, 167 <code>WAIT</code>, 189 <code>OPEN FRONT DOOR</code>, 191 <code>OPEN SITTINGROOM DOOR</code>, 195 <code>TAKE LETTERS</code>, 215 <code>WAIT</code>, 220 <code>PAY 10D TO CABBIE</code>, 236 <code>OPEN TRICIAS FRONT DOOR</code>, 248 <code>SAY TO LESTRADE</code>, 249 <code>SAY TO LESTRADE</code>, 255 <code>SAY TO LESTRADE &quot;THE SALE LOCATION IS OLD MILL ROAD&quot;</code>, 268 <code>LOOK</code>, 277 <code>TAKE RIPPED CODED BAKER NOTE</code>, 278 <code>TAKE RIPPED CODED NOTE</code>, 282 <code>OPEN PLAIN DOOR</code>, 287 <code>TAKE TORN CODED NOTE</code>, 306 <code>TAKE NOTE FROM FIREPLACE</code>, 308 <code>EXAMINE CLOSELY BURN NOTE</code>, 309 <code>DROP BURN NOTE</code>, 319 <code>PAY 10D TO CABBIE</code>, 322 <code>TALK TO LESTRADE</code>, 325 <code>HAIL CAB</code>, 328 <code>SAY TO CABBIE &quot;GO TO KINGS CROSS ROAD&quot;</code>, 330 <code>PAY 10D TO CABBIE</code>, 370 <code>CLIMB OUT</code>. The other 10 (steps 3, 4, 5, 93, 94, 168, 226, 227, 228, 261) each win alone but lost once the others were gone.</li><li><strong>Timing is exact.</strong> Putting a one-minute INVENTORY in before step 31 or before step 251 still wins; before step 101, 151, 201, 301 or 341 it loses. So a single minute more or less in the middle of the route changes the outcome.</li><li><strong>Failed commands.</strong> A command that fails costs no time by itself, but each failure adds 3 to a count kept for the turn; when the count passes 10 the character gives up and a minute passes. Three failures in a row therefore cost one minute (steps 118-120).</li><li><strong>What this does not show.</strong> It does not say why a particular line is needed. Where the reason is known, it is given in the notes under the stretch; where it is not (the garden loop at steps 52-59), the note says so.</li></ul><h2>The route in 32 stretches</h2><ol><li><a href="#walk1">Take the lamp and get Watson moving</a> <small>(steps 1-6, Mon 8:01am to Mon 8:22am)</small></li><li><a href="#walk2">Pick up the china man&#x27;s disguise</a> <small>(steps 7-11, Mon 8:22am to Mon 8:29am)</small></li><li><a href="#walk3">Baker Street to Kings Cross Road by cab</a> <small>(steps 12-22, Mon 8:29am to Mon 10:04am)</small></li><li><a href="#walk4">Kings Cross to Leatherhead by steam train</a> <small>(steps 23-30, Mon 10:04am to Mon 12:02pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk5">Leatherhead to Brown&#x27;s front gate; Lestrade arrives</a> <small>(steps 31-37, Mon 12:02pm to Mon 12:26pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk6">The bridge: corpse, bridge, crumpled note</a> <small>(steps 38-51, Mon 12:26pm to Mon 12:58pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk7">Back round the garden and along to Jones&#x27;s house</a> <small>(steps 52-61, Mon 12:58pm to Mon 1:18pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk8">Jones&#x27;s house: the library, the bookcase, the clothes</a> <small>(steps 62-87, Mon 1:18pm to Mon 2:06pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk9">Brown&#x27;s house: the study</a> <small>(steps 88-115, Mon 2:06pm to Mon 2:59pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk10">Back to Leather Head platform two</a> <small>(steps 116-125, Mon 2:59pm to Mon 3:11pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk11">Platform two to Kings Cross by train</a> <small>(steps 126-137, Mon 3:11pm to Mon 4:45pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk12">Kings Cross Road to Slater Street by cab</a> <small>(steps 138-144, Mon 4:45pm to Mon 5:13pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk13">The opium den: the Major&#x27;s confession</a> <small>(steps 145-153, Mon 5:13pm to Tue 12:03am)</small></li><li><a href="#walk14">Slater Street to Kings Cross platform four (Tuesday 1:30am)</a> <small>(steps 154-165, Tue 12:03am to Tue 1:30am)</small></li><li><a href="#walk15">Wait for the 7:30am train; to Leatherhead</a> <small>(steps 166-172, Tue 1:30am to Tue 9:04am)</small></li><li><a href="#walk16">The bridge again: the gun in the stream</a> <small>(steps 173-186, Tue 9:04am to Tue 9:35am)</small></li><li><a href="#walk17">Basil&#x27;s house at Cobden Lane: the wall safe</a> <small>(steps 187-205, Tue 9:35am to Tue 10:23am)</small></li><li><a href="#walk18">Platform two to Kings Cross by train</a> <small>(steps 206-214, Tue 10:23am to Tue 12:15pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk19">Kings Cross Road to Portman Street by cab</a> <small>(steps 215-221, Tue 12:15pm to Tue 12:57pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk20">Tricia Fender&#x27;s house</a> <small>(steps 222-237, Tue 12:57pm to Tue 1:37pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk21">Portman Street to Scotland Yard</a> <small>(steps 238-247, Tue 1:37pm to Tue 1:57pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk22">Scotland Yard: the statements</a> <small>(steps 248-255, Tue 1:57pm to Tue 2:07pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk23">Scotland Yard to Basil&#x27;s front yard; wait for the window</a> <small>(steps 256-269, Tue 2:07pm to Tue 10:03pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk24">Inside Basil&#x27;s house in disguise (Tuesday night)</a> <small>(steps 270-295, Tue 10:03pm to Tue 10:51pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk25">Wednesday morning: wait outside</a> <small>(steps 296-304, Tue 10:51pm to Wed 10:06am)</small></li><li><a href="#walk26">Basil&#x27;s bedroom: the burning note</a> <small>(steps 305-312, Wed 10:06am to Wed 10:24am)</small></li><li><a href="#walk27">Camden Street to Scotland Yard: the sale location</a> <small>(steps 313-323, Wed 10:24am to Wed 10:43am)</small></li><li><a href="#walk28">To Leatherhead with Lestrade</a> <small>(steps 324-343, Wed 10:43am to Wed 1:36pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk29">The police cab to Old Mill Road</a> <small>(steps 344-348, Wed 1:36pm to Wed 3:14pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk30">Back to the main street</a> <small>(steps 349-353, Wed 3:14pm to Wed 4:49pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk31">Find Lestrade and take the police cab again</a> <small>(steps 354-363, Wed 4:49pm to Wed 5:06pm)</small></li><li><a href="#walk32">Kings Cross Road to Victoria: the arrest</a> <small>(steps 364-372, Wed 5:06pm to Wed 6:43pm)</small></li></ol><h3 id="walk1">1. Take the lamp and get Watson moving</h3><p><small>Steps 1-6, Mon 8:01am to Mon 8:22am.</small> Take the oil lamp, then WAIT until Watson reads out the case (8:16am). SAY TO WATSON &quot;FOLLOW ME&quot; so that he comes along; he is with you in every cab and train from here.</p><div class="tablewrap"><table class="steps"><tr><th>Step</th><th>Type</th><th>Clock after</th><th>Where</th><th>The game replies</th><th>Without this line</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td><code>TAKE OIL LAMP</code></td><td>Mon 8:02am</td><td>Your Room</td><td>You take the oil lamp.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><code>WAIT</code></td><td>Mon 8:06am</td><td>Your Room</td><td>You wait.</td><td>still wins</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><code>WAIT</code></td><td>Mon 8:11am</td><td>Your Room</td><td>You wait.</td><td>still wins</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><code>WAIT</code></td><td>Mon 8:16am</td><td>Your Room</td><td>You wait.</td><td>still wins</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><code>WAIT</code></td><td>Mon 8:16am</td><td>Your Room</td><td>You wait. With an excited look on his face Watson says &quot; Well here is a case that may interest you, it seems that two close friends, a Mrs Brown and a Mrs Jones, were murdered last night in separate incidents although apparently ...</td><td>still wins</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><code>SAY TO WATSON &quot;FOLLOW ME&quot;</code></td><td>Mon 8:22am</td><td>Your Room</td><td>You talk to Watson. Watson climbs out of watsons armchair.</td><td>loses</td></tr></table></div><h3 id="walk2">2. Pick up the china man&#x27;s disguise</h3><p><small>Steps 7-11, Mon 8:22am to Mon 8:29am.</small> Open the plain door, go north into the small closet, WEAR the china man&#x27;s disguise and TAKE it OFF again, then back south. The disguise is carried from now on (the inventory at step 51 lists it).</p><div class="tablewrap"><table class="steps"><tr><th>Step</th><th>Type</th><th>Clock after</th><th>Where</th><th>The game replies</th><th>Without this line</th></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><code>OPEN PLAIN DOOR</code></td><td>Mon 8:23am</td><td>Your Room</td><td>You open the plain door.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><code>NORTH</code></td><td>Mon 8:25am</td><td>Small Closet</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td><code>WEAR CHINA MANS DISGUISE</code></td><td>Mon 8:26am</td><td>in disguise</td><td>You wear the china mans disguise.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td><code>TAKE OFF CHINA MANS DISGUISE</code></td><td>Mon 8:27am</td><td>Small Closet</td><td>The china man takes off the china mans disguise.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td><code>SOUTH</code></td><td>Mon 8:29am</td><td>Your Room</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr></table></div><h3 id="walk3">3. Baker Street to Kings Cross Road by cab</h3><p><small>Steps 12-22, Mon 8:29am to Mon 10:04am.</small> Out of the flat and the front door, HAIL CAB, climb in, and SAY TO CABBIE &quot;GO TO KINGS CROSS ROAD&quot;. The ride takes about 80 minutes (8:41 to 10:01am), so the LOOK is repeated until the cab arrives. PAY 10D TO CABBIE, then climb out.</p><p><em>Note:</em> Here the fare comes to 6d: paying 10d gets 4d back (the page on trains and cabs explains how the fare is worked out).</p><div class="tablewrap"><table class="steps"><tr><th>Step</th><th>Type</th><th>Clock after</th><th>Where</th><th>The game replies</th><th>Without this line</th></tr><tr><td>12</td><td><code>OPEN YOUR DOOR</code></td><td>Mon 8:30am</td><td>Your Room</td><td>You open your door.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td><code>WEST</code></td><td>Mon 8:32am</td><td>Hall</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td><code>DOWN</code></td><td>Mon 8:34am</td><td>Entrance Hallway</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td><code>OPEN FRONT DOOR</code></td><td>Mon 8:35am</td><td>Entrance Hallway</td><td>You open the front door.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td><code>SOUTH</code></td><td>Mon 8:37am</td><td>Baker Street</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td><code>HAIL CAB</code></td><td>Mon 8:38am</td><td>Baker Street</td><td>You hail the hansom cab. The cabbie remarks to you &quot; Good morning guv&#x27;nor. &quot;.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td><code>CLIMB INTO HANSOM CAB</code></td><td>Mon 8:40am</td><td>Baker Street</td><td>You climb into the hansom cab. Watson climbs into the hansom cab.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td><code>SAY TO CABBIE &quot;GO TO KINGS CROSS ROAD&quot;</code></td><td>Mon 8:41am</td><td>Hansom Cab</td><td>You talk to the cabbie. You are in a hansom cab with Watson and the cabbie. Watson is carrying the daily chronicle. You are travelling the streets of London in a hansom cab, the sun shines through the windows onto your face.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td><code>LOOK</code> &times;80 <small>(repeated until the reply shows &ldquo;Kings Cross Road. To&rdquo;)</small></td><td>Mon 10:01am</td><td>Kings Cross Road</td><td>You are in a hansom cab with Watson and the cabbie. Watson is carrying the daily chronicle. The hansom cab is in Kings Cross Road. Visible exits are northeast.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td><code>PAY 10D TO CABBIE</code></td><td>Mon 10:02am</td><td>Kings Cross Road</td><td>You pay 10d. To the cabbie. The cabbie pays 4d. To you.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td><code>CLIMB OUT</code></td><td>Mon 10:04am</td><td>Kings Cross Road</td><td>You climb out of the hansom cab. Watson climbs out of the hansom cab.</td><td>loses</td></tr></table></div><h3 id="walk4">4. Kings Cross to Leatherhead by steam train</h3><p><small>Steps 23-30, Mon 10:04am to Mon 12:02pm.</small> Four NORTHEAST moves up through the platforms to platform three, wait for the steam train (it enters at 10:45), climb in at 10:47, and ride to Leather Head platform one (12:00 noon). Climb out.</p><div class="tablewrap"><table class="steps"><tr><th>Step</th><th>Type</th><th>Clock after</th><th>Where</th><th>The game replies</th><th>Without this line</th></tr><tr><td>23</td><td><code>NORTHEAST</code></td><td>Mon 10:06am</td><td>Kings Cross Station</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td><code>NORTHEAST</code></td><td>Mon 10:08am</td><td>Platform One</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td><code>NORTHEAST</code></td><td>Mon 10:10am</td><td>Platform Two</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td><code>NORTHEAST</code></td><td>Mon 10:12am</td><td>Platform Three</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td><code>LOOK</code> &times;33 <small>(repeated until the reply shows &ldquo;steam train&rdquo;)</small></td><td>Mon 10:45am</td><td>Platform Three</td><td>You are at platform three of Kings cross station. Visible exits are southwest northeast. You can see Watson. Watson is carrying the daily chronicle. A steam train enters.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>28</td><td><code>CLIMB INTO TRAIN</code></td><td>Mon 10:47am</td><td>Platform Three</td><td>You climb into the steam train. Watson climbs into the steam train.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>29</td><td><code>LOOK</code> &times;73 <small>(repeated until the reply shows &ldquo;Leather&rdquo;)</small></td><td>Mon 12:00pm</td><td>Platform One</td><td>You are in a steam train with Watson. Watson is carrying the daily chronicle. You are riding in a steam train through the picturesque English countryside. You are in a steam train with Watson. Watson is carrying the daily ...</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td><code>CLIMB OUT</code></td><td>Mon 12:02pm</td><td>Platform One</td><td>You climb out of the steam train. Watson climbs out of the steam train.</td><td>loses</td></tr></table></div><h3 id="walk5">5. Leatherhead to Brown&#x27;s front gate; Lestrade arrives</h3><p><small>Steps 31-37, Mon 12:02pm to Mon 12:26pm.</small> South through the station to the main street, a quick look into the police station, then WEST to Browns Front Gate. LOOK until Inspector Lestrade arrives (12:25pm); he introduces Chief Constable Straker.</p><div class="tablewrap"><table class="steps"><tr><th>Step</th><th>Type</th><th>Clock after</th><th>Where</th><th>The game replies</th><th>Without this line</th></tr><tr><td>31</td><td><code>SOUTH</code></td><td>Mon 12:04pm</td><td>Leatherhead Station</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>32</td><td><code>SOUTH</code></td><td>Mon 12:06pm</td><td>Main Street</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>33</td><td><code>NORTHEAST</code></td><td>Mon 12:08pm</td><td>Leatherhead Police Station</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>34</td><td><code>SOUTHWEST</code></td><td>Mon 12:10pm</td><td>Main Street</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>35</td><td><code>WEST</code></td><td>Mon 12:10pm</td><td>Browns Front Gate</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>36</td><td><code>LOOK</code> &times;14 <small>(repeated until the reply shows &ldquo;Lestrade&rdquo;)</small></td><td>Mon 12:25pm</td><td>Browns Front Gate</td><td>You are at Browns Front Gate. To the south there is a local police man. Visible exits are east. You can see Watson, Chief Constable Straker and a local police man. Watson is carrying the daily chronicle. Inspector Lestrade ...</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>37</td><td><code>LOOK</code></td><td>Mon 12:26pm</td><td>Browns Front Gate</td><td>You are at Browns Front Gate. Visible exits are south east. You can see Inspector Lestrade, Watson, Chief Constable Straker and a local police man. Inspector Lestrade is carrying some money. Watson is carrying the daily ...</td><td>loses</td></tr></table></div><h3 id="walk6">6. The bridge: corpse, bridge, crumpled note</h3><p><small>Steps 38-51, Mon 12:26pm to Mon 12:58pm.</small> Down the garden paths to the east side of the sandstone bridge (12:39pm). EXAMINE CLOSELY the female corpse and the bridge, TAKE the crumpled note and EXAMINE CLOSELY it (&quot;Meet me by the bridge at half past twelve...&quot;). The rock is not here on Monday.</p><div class="tablewrap"><table class="steps"><tr><th>Step</th><th>Type</th><th>Clock after</th><th>Where</th><th>The game replies</th><th>Without this line</th></tr><tr><td>38</td><td><code>SOUTH</code></td><td>Mon 12:29pm</td><td>Garden Path</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>39</td><td><code>LOOK</code></td><td>Mon 12:30pm</td><td>Garden Path</td><td>You are on a garden path. Visible exits are north south southwest east. You can see Watson. Watson is carrying the daily chronicle.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>40</td><td><code>SOUTHWEST</code></td><td>Mon 12:32pm</td><td>Winding Garden Path</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>41</td><td><code>SOUTH</code></td><td>Mon 12:34pm</td><td>Winding Garden Path</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>42</td><td><code>SOUTHWEST</code></td><td>Mon 12:36pm</td><td>West Side</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>43</td><td><code>LOOK</code></td><td>Mon 12:37pm</td><td>West Side</td><td>You are on the west side. To the east there is a sandstone bridge. To the northwest there is a deep stream. Visible exits are north northeast. You can see Watson. Watson is carrying the daily chronicle.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>44</td><td><code>EAST</code></td><td>Mon 12:39pm</td><td>East Side</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>45</td><td><code>LOOK</code></td><td>Mon 12:39pm</td><td>East Side</td><td>You are on the east side. To the west there is a sandstone bridge. To the north there is a deep stream. Visible exits are southeast. You can see Watson and a female corpse. Watson is carrying the daily chronicle. On the female ...</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>46</td><td><code>EXAMINE FEMALE CORPSE</code></td><td>Mon 12:41pm</td><td>East Side</td><td>You examine the female corpse. You see the body of a middle aged woman. Her face is streaked with dried blood.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>47</td><td><code>EXAMINE CLOSELY FEMALE CORPSE</code></td><td>Mon 12:46pm</td><td>East Side</td><td>You closely examine the female corpse. You see the body of a middle aged woman. Her face is streaked with dried blood. Examination of the right hand reveals dark speckled markings that resemble powder burns.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>48</td><td><code>EXAMINE CLOSELY SANDSTONE BRIDGE</code></td><td>Mon 12:51pm</td><td>East Side</td><td>You closely examine the sandstone bridge. You see a sandstone bridge. Close inspection reveals a small chip in the stone work that is evidently quite new. It looks like a heavy metallic object has struck it.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>49</td><td><code>TAKE CRUMPLED NOTE</code></td><td>Mon 12:52pm</td><td>East Side</td><td>You take the crumpled note.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>50</td><td><code>EXAMINE CLOSELY NOTE</code></td><td>Mon 12:57pm</td><td>East Side</td><td>You closely examine the crumpled note. It reads &quot; Meet me by the bridge at half past twelve. I have some very important news. Signed. P. F. &quot;.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>51</td><td><code>INVENTORY</code></td><td>Mon 12:58pm</td><td>East Side</td><td>You are carrying a china mans disguise, an oil lamp, your money and a crumpled note.</td><td>loses</td></tr></table></div><h3 id="walk7">7. Back round the garden and along to Jones&#x27;s house</h3><p><small>Steps 52-61, Mon 12:58pm to Mon 1:18pm.</small> A loop of garden paths (SOUTHEAST, EAST, NORTH, NORTH, NORTH, NORTHWEST, WEST, NORTH) brings you back to the front gate by 1:14pm; then EAST along the main street to Jones Front Gate.</p><p><em>Note:</em> Each of the eight moves is needed (checked one at a time). Replacing the loop with the direct way back (WEST, NORTH, NORTH, NORTHEAST, NORTH, as on Tuesday), with a WAIT and a LOOK added so that you reach the gate at the same minute, loses. The reason the loop is needed is not known.</p><div class="tablewrap"><table class="steps"><tr><th>Step</th><th>Type</th><th>Clock after</th><th>Where</th><th>The game replies</th><th>Without this line</th></tr><tr><td>52</td><td><code>SOUTHEAST</code></td><td>Mon 1:00pm</td><td>Winding Garden Path</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>53</td><td><code>EAST</code></td><td>Mon 1:02pm</td><td>South Side</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>54</td><td><code>NORTH</code></td><td>Mon 1:04pm</td><td>North Side</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>55</td><td><code>NORTH</code></td><td>Mon 1:06pm</td><td>Winding Garden Path</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>56</td><td><code>NORTH</code></td><td>Mon 1:08pm</td><td>Winding Garden Path</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>57</td><td><code>NORTHWEST</code></td><td>Mon 1:10pm</td><td>Winding Garden Path</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>58</td><td><code>WEST</code></td><td>Mon 1:12pm</td><td>Garden Path</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>59</td><td><code>NORTH</code></td><td>Mon 1:14pm</td><td>Browns Front Gate</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>60</td><td><code>EAST</code></td><td>Mon 1:16pm</td><td>Main Street</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>61</td><td><code>EAST</code></td><td>Mon 1:18pm</td><td>Jones Front Gate</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr></table></div><h3 id="walk8">8. Jones&#x27;s house: the library, the bookcase, the clothes</h3><p><small>Steps 62-87, Mon 1:18pm to Mon 2:06pm.</small> OPEN FRONT DOOR, the sitting room, the solid door and into the library (1:31pm). EXAMINE CLOSELY the body and the bookcase (it swings open on a small room). Lestrade, Straker and the Major come in; SAY TO LESTRADE &quot;MAJOR PERCIVAL FFOULKES IS INNOCENT&quot; and he asks for proof. In the small room, EXAMINE CLOSELY and TAKE the bloodstained clothes (labelled &quot;Tricia. Fender.&quot;), then out through the house to Jones Front Gate (2:06pm).</p><div class="tablewrap"><table class="steps"><tr><th>Step</th><th>Type</th><th>Clock after</th><th>Where</th><th>The game replies</th><th>Without this line</th></tr><tr><td>62</td><td><code>SOUTH</code></td><td>Mon 1:20pm</td><td>Jones Front Door</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>63</td><td><code>LOOK</code></td><td>Mon 1:21pm</td><td>Jones Front Door</td><td>You are at Jones Front Door. To the south there is the front door. Visible exits are north. You can see Watson. Watson is carrying the daily chronicle.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>64</td><td><code>OPEN FRONT DOOR</code></td><td>Mon 1:22pm</td><td>Jones Front Door</td><td>You open the front door.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>65</td><td><code>SOUTH</code></td><td>Mon 1:22pm</td><td>Jones Entrance Hall</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>66</td><td><code>LOOK</code></td><td>Mon 1:25pm</td><td>Jones Entrance Hall</td><td>You are in Jones Entrance Hall. To the north there is the front door. To the southeast there is the diningroom door. To the west there is the sittingroom door. Above there is the stairway. You can see Watson. Watson is carrying ...</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>67</td><td><code>OPEN SITTINGROOM DOOR</code></td><td>Mon 1:26pm</td><td>Jones Entrance Hall</td><td>You open the sittingroom door.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>68</td><td><code>WEST</code></td><td>Mon 1:28pm</td><td>Sitting Room</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>69</td><td><code>OPEN SOLID DOOR</code></td><td>Mon 1:29pm</td><td>Sitting Room</td><td>You open the solid door.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>70</td><td><code>SOUTH</code></td><td>Mon 1:31pm</td><td>Library</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>71</td><td><code>LOOK</code></td><td>Mon 1:32pm</td><td>Library</td><td>You are in a library. To the west there is a bookcase. To the north there is a solid 