he game replies</th><th>Without this line</th></tr><tr><td>354</td><td><code>NORTH</code></td><td>Wed 4:51pm</td><td>Leatherhead Station</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>355</td><td><code>NORTH</code></td><td>Wed 4:52pm</td><td>Platform One</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>356</td><td><code>NORTH</code></td><td>Wed 4:54pm</td><td>Platform Two</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>357</td><td><code>LOOK</code></td><td>Wed 4:55pm</td><td>Platform Two</td><td>You are at platform two of Leather Head station. Visible exits are south. You can see Inspector Lestrade and Watson. Inspector Lestrade is carrying some money. Watson is carrying the daily chronicle. Inspector Lestrade says to ...</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>358</td><td><code>SAY TO LESTRADE &quot;FOLLOW ME&quot;</code></td><td>Wed 4:57pm</td><td>Platform Two</td><td>You talk to Inspector Lestrade.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>359</td><td><code>SOUTH</code></td><td>Wed 4:59pm</td><td>Platform One</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>360</td><td><code>SOUTH</code></td><td>Wed 5:01pm</td><td>Leatherhead Station</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>361</td><td><code>SOUTH</code></td><td>Wed 5:03pm</td><td>Main Street</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>362</td><td><code>CLIMB INTO POLICE CAB</code></td><td>Wed 5:05pm</td><td>Main Street</td><td>You climb into the police cab. Inspector Lestrade climbs into the police cab. Watson climbs into the police cab.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>363</td><td><code>SAY TO POLICE MAN &quot;GO FURIOUSLY TO KINGS CROSS ROAD&quot;</code></td><td>Wed 5:06pm</td><td>Police Cab</td><td>You talk to the local police man. You are in a police cab with Inspector Lestrade, Watson and a local police man. Inspector Lestrade is carrying some money. Watson is carrying the daily chronicle. You are in a cab travelling ...</td><td>loses</td></tr></table></div><h3 id="walk32">32. Kings Cross Road to Victoria: the arrest</h3><p><small>Steps 364-372, Wed 5:06pm to Wed 6:43pm.</small> LOOK until Kings Cross Road (6:20pm): Basil and the german agent are in two hansom cabs and tell the cabbies to go to Buckingham Palace Road. CLIMB OUT, NORTHEAST x2 to platform one, CLIMB INTO TRAIN at once (it leaves at 6:29), ride to Victoria platform one (6:41pm), CLIMB OUT, WAIT: Basil pulls a gun, Watson pushes you aside, Lestrade captures them, and the game is won.</p><div class="tablewrap"><table class="steps"><tr><th>Step</th><th>Type</th><th>Clock after</th><th>Where</th><th>The game replies</th><th>Without this line</th></tr><tr><td>364</td><td><code>LOOK</code> &times;75 <small>(repeated until the reply shows &ldquo;Cross Road. &rdquo;)</small></td><td>Wed 6:20pm</td><td>Kings Cross Road</td><td>You are in a police cab with Inspector Lestrade, Watson and a local police man. Inspector Lestrade is carrying some money. Watson is carrying the daily chronicle. The police cab is in Kings Cross Road. Visible exits are ...</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>365</td><td><code>CLIMB OUT</code></td><td>Wed 6:23pm</td><td>Kings Cross Road</td><td>You climb out of the police cab. Inspector Lestrade climbs out of the police cab. Watson climbs out of the police cab.</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>366</td><td><code>NORTHEAST</code></td><td>Wed 6:25pm</td><td>Kings Cross Station</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>367</td><td><code>NORTHEAST</code></td><td>Wed 6:27pm</td><td>Platform One</td><td></td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>368</td><td><code>CLIMB INTO TRAIN</code></td><td>Wed 6:29pm</td><td>Steam Train</td><td>You climb into the steam train. Watson climbs into the steam train. Inspector Lestrade climbs into the steam train. The train pulls out of the station. You are in a steam train with Inspector Lestrade and Watson. Inspector ...</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>369</td><td><code>LOOK</code> &times;12 <small>(repeated until the reply shows &ldquo;Victoria&rdquo;)</small></td><td>Wed 6:41pm</td><td>Platform One</td><td>You are in a steam train with Inspector Lestrade and Watson. Inspector Lestrade is carrying some money. Watson is carrying the daily chronicle. You are travelling along the London underground railway in a steam train. You are in ...</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>370</td><td><code>CLIMB OUT</code></td><td>Wed 6:43pm</td><td>Platform One</td><td>You climb out of the steam train. Inspector Lestrade climbs out of the steam train. Watson climbs out of the steam train. Basil Phipps enters. A german agent enters.</td><td>still wins</td></tr><tr><td>371</td><td><code>WAIT</code></td><td>Wed 6:43pm</td><td>Platform One</td><td>You wait. As Basil enters he sees you and pulls a gun. With suprising agility Watson pushes you out of the way of Basils first shot allowing Lestrade time to pull his gun and capture the culprits. As incredible as it may</td><td>loses</td></tr><tr><td>372</td><td><code>WAIT</code></td><td>Wed 6:43pm</td><td>Platform One</td><td>seem you have solved this case. You truly deserve the title of worlds greatest detective. PLAY SAVED GAME TAPE TO BE LOADED .</td><td>loses</td></tr></table></div><p>Not done: TRICIA FENDER BLACKMAILED MRS BROWN was never said (not needed). The route is one that works, not the only one.</p>

[Page:the-endings-played]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(the-endings-played-content)

[the-endings-played-content]
<p>Each ending below was reached from a fresh boot by typing only, with no pokes. The command files and the replies are in <code>tools/experiments/playthrough/endings/</code> (<code>*.cmds</code>, and <code>*.transcript.txt</code> from <code>tools/experiments/replay_verbose.py</code>). The game is deterministic, so each replays exactly. The earlier trivia entry 'How the case can end' described several of these from the code or with pokes; this page replaces that.</p><table><tr><th>File</th><th>How</th><th>Ending text (as printed)</th></tr><tr><td>end1_monday_3pm</td><td>Do nothing: WAIT in the sitting room to 3pm Monday (never meet Lestrade with Straker).</td><td>"I do not think you are doing too well, surely Sherlock Holmes could do a better job than you have done so far."</td></tr><tr><td>end2_major_arrested_tuesday_1am</td><td>Meet Straker at Browns Front Gate on Monday at 12:27, then wait. Without clearing the Major the game ends at 1:04am Tuesday.</td><td>Lestrade: "Well Holmes that wraps up this case, sorry you were not of more help but you cannot solve them all you know." Despite his assurances you cannot help feeling there should be more to this case.</td></tr><tr><td>end3a_papers_nothing_proven</td><td>Clear the Major (to 12:14am Tuesday), then wait to 10:00am Thursday.</td><td>"Not only have you failed to solve the crimes at hand but the morning papers carry a report of Mr Browns lost plans being found and sold to a foreign power... disgraced for letting this happen."</td></tr><tr><td>end3b_papers_both_murders_proven</td><td>As the winning route to 2:12pm Tuesday (Mrs Brown and Mrs Jones statements proven, Tricia had plans), then wait in Scotland Yard to 10:00am Thursday.</td><td>"You have done extremely well to solve the case at hand but the morning papers carry a report of Mr Browns lost plans being found and sold to a foreign power..."</td></tr><tr><td>end4a_shot_wall_safe_monday</td><td>OPEN WALL SAFE in Basil's library at Cobden Lane on Monday (12:15pm in this run).</td><td>Basil enters: "Holmes, your interference is becoming annoying." Pulling a gun he shoots you. Perhaps you should try again.</td></tr><tr><td>end4b_shot_window_as_holmes</td><td>Tuesday 10:03pm, NORTHEAST through the window as Holmes (no disguise).</td><td>The same shot, as soon as you are in the bedroom with Basil.</td></tr><tr><td>end4c_shot_bedroom_wednesday_950</td><td>Enter the bedroom in the china man's disguise on Wednesday morning and wait there.</td><td>The game ends at 9:50am (the shot is fired at whoever is in the room).</td></tr><tr><td>end5_victoria_without_lestrade</td><td>The winning route, but on the 6:29 train from Kings Cross without Lestrade (he follows a turn behind and misses it).</td><td>"As Basil enters he sees you and pulls a gun. With his usual skill his first shot disposes of you. Perhaps you should try again."</td></tr><tr><td>end6_leave_lestrade_on_platform_two</td><td>The winning route up to 4:55pm on platform two at Leatherhead, then no FOLLOW ME: just WAIT. Lestrade says "...has eluded us Holmes." at 4:56 and leaves the platform; the game runs on to Thursday.</td><td>The same morning-papers text as end3b, at 10:00am Thursday.</td></tr><tr><td>end7_eluded_at_scotland_yard</td><td>As end6 but ride Lestrade's own train and cab and walk into Scotland Yard at 8:02pm, then wait.</td><td>Lestrade at 8:04pm: "I am afraid that Basil has eluded us Holmes." He goes west; at 10:00am Thursday the papers ending ("You have done extremely well...").</td></tr><tr><td>end8_victoria_without_watson</td><td>The winning route, but SAY TO WATSON "WAIT" in place of the idle LOOK on platform two at 4:54pm.</td><td>The same death as end5: "With his usual skill his first shot disposes of you."</td></tr><tr><td>v13_shadow_lestrade (in playthrough/)</td><td>A second winning route: after the sale-location statement at 10:53am stay with Lestrade instead of travelling alone. WEST to Parliament Street, CLIMB INTO HANSOM CAB while Lestrade is in it (10:57), Kings Cross Road 11:11, platforms one to three, the 12:15pm train from platform three with him and Watson, Leatherhead platform one 1:30pm. From there the lines are the same as v12.</td><td>The winning text, at Victoria 6:43pm (replay prints it in about twenty seconds).</td></tr><tr><td>v11_full_win</td><td>The winning route.</td><td>"...As incredible as it may seem you have solved this case. You truly deserve the title of worlds greatest detective."</td></tr></table><p>Watson missing, Lestrade present: <code>end8_victoria_without_watson</code>. Telling Watson to WAIT in the cab, on the Victoria train, or fifteen minutes before leaving does not leave him behind (the order lapses and he follows); at Kings Cross Road it costs the minute Lestrade needs for the 6:29 train. The order works if it replaces the idle LOOK on platform two at 4:54pm: Watson stays, Lestrade still follows and every later time is unchanged, but at Victoria the first shot from Basil kills you, so the win needs Watson as well as Lestrade (the win text has Watson push you out of the way). Before this, three attempts without that trick were recorded in <code>endings/attempt_watson_missing_victoria.transcript.txt</code>; that file is kept as history.</p>

[Page:state-bits]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(state-bits-content)

[state-bits-content]

<p>Almost everything that can change about a person or object in Sherlock is a bit in its record. This page lists them: what each bit means, which things have it, and what the game does with it. The bytes below are offsets in an entity record (the things listed by the index at #R$8B6F).</p>

<h3>Asleep, unconscious, dead</h3>
<p>These are not three bits. Each is a combination of three parts of the record:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>The person bit</b> (+5 bit 6) is cleared, so the character stops being someone you can talk to.</li>
<li><b>A state word</b> (+9/+10, the first adjective slot of the name) is replaced by a word such as SLEEPING or UNCONSCIOUS, so the thing is listed as "a sleeping Phipps" or "an unconscious local police man". #R$F1C1 writes it, together with the article nibble below.</li>
<li><b>The article nibble</b> (the high nibble of +8) is changed so that the name reads properly with the new word: 1 is "a", 2 is "an", 8 is no article.</li>
</ul>
<table class="sherlock">
<tr><th>State</th><th>Who and when</th><th>What changes</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Asleep</b></td><td>The gardener, the cook and Strachan at 9pm (#R$9ED1, #R$9EC1); Phipps in the night script (#R$EFB3).</td><td>The state word becomes SLEEPING ($07F0), the article nibble becomes 1, and the person bit is cleared. The gardener and cook also go off stage, and Strachan is moved to Daphne's room (74). Waking puts the person bit back and clears the word.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Unconscious</b></td><td>The local police man, when the Major knocks him out (#R$F1A8).</td><td>The state word becomes UNCONSCIOUS ($0910), the article nibble becomes 2, and the person bit is cleared. Straker reviving him (#R$ED39) puts back LOCAL ($050B), nibble 1 and the person bit.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Dead</b></td><td>The female corpse (46) and the body (72).</td><td>The thing is not a person: bit 6 is clear. A check that needs the person bit and does not find it prints the word DEAD from the flag table at #R$C471.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Burning, singed, burnt</b></td><td>The note Phipps burns (85).</td><td>BURNING ($0157) is replaced by SINGED ($07D5) when it is taken, or BURNT ($015B) when the countdown at #R$9CA1 runs out (#R$9CA2).</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>The flag byte, +5</h3>
<p>Eight bits of condition. The flag-word tables at #R$C471 (a bit that is needed but missing) and #R$C481 (a bit that is present but forbidden) give the words that "THE ... IS ..." messages print.</p>
<table class="sherlock">
<tr><th>Bit</th><th>Mask</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Details</th></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>$80</td><td><b>Revealed (visible)</b></td><td>Set on everything the player can see. A thing with this bit clear is hidden: #R$C821 says it cannot be seen, and EXAMINE needs it set. #R$9C56 sets it and #R$9C60 clears it. The torn note, the false bottom and the large rock start hidden and are revealed by examining the place that holds them; the steam trains are hidden until #R$9C41 moves one, which sets the bit.</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>$40</td><td><b>Person (alive and able to act)</b></td><td>Set on every character who is in play. What a person holds is introduced with "&lt;name&gt; IS CARRYING", and a person can be talked to. Clearing it is how the game makes someone <b>asleep</b> or <b>unconscious</b> (#R$EFB3, #R$9ED1, #R$F1A8). The flag-word table at #R$C471 names the missing bit DEAD, so a check that needs a living person and finds the bit clear would say "IS DEAD". There is no separate "dead" bit: the dead are things with this bit clear, such as the female corpse (46) and the body (72).</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>$20</td><td><b>Open</b></td><td>Set when a door, window, drawer or similar thing is open. OPEN sets it and CLOSE clears it (the two shared actions at #R$DE9D and #R$DEAF); OPEN refuses a thing that has it set ("IS OPEN") and CLOSE refuses one that has it clear ("IS CLOSED"). The containment walk at #R$C86E lets what is inside a holder be seen only when the holder has this bit or bit 3 set. The cabs and trains have it set, and so do the large rock and the burning note.</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>$10</td><td><b>On (alight)</b></td><td>Set on the oil lamp when it is lit; the flag-word tables call the opposite OFF and this ON. The cabs and trains have it set. The light test at #R$D078 needs a thing in reach that is on and full.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>$08</td><td><b>Contents can be seen</b></td><td>The second way, besides bit 5, that a holder lets the things inside it be seen (#R$C86E). It is not set on any thing at the start of the game, and there is no word for it in the flag tables.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>$04</td><td><b>Full</b></td><td>Set on the oil lamp, the cabs, the trains and the deep stream. The flag-word tables call the opposite EMPTY and this FULL. The lamp must be both on and full to give light.</td></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>$02</td><td><b>Not used by any check found</b></td><td>Set only on the deep stream (59). No verb record, flag table or light test reads it.</td></tr>
<tr><td>0</td><td>$01</td><td><b>Locked</b></td><td>Set on the doors and windows that start locked (the front doors, the windows). The flag tables call the opposite UNLOCKED and this LOCKED. OPEN refuses a thing that has it set ("IS LOCKED"); the script helpers at #R$F203 and #R$F210 clear and set it.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Common values: $80 an ordinary visible thing, $A0 a visible thing that is open (or a person who is asleep or unconscious), $E0 a person in play, $81 a locked door, $B4 a cab (visible, open, on and full), $34 a train that is out of sight.</p>

<h3>How a verb tests these bits</h3>
<p>A verb's action record can carry a pair of masks and modes for the direct and indirect objects' +5 bytes (#R$C74E). Mode 0 needs every mask bit set, 1 needs at least one, 2 needs none, 3 not all. EXAMINE needs bit 7; OPEN needs bits 5 and 0 clear; CLOSE needs bit 5 set. On failure the offending bits go to #R$CB79 and #R$CB7B prints "THE ... IS ..." with the word from the table.</p>

<h3>The second flag byte, +6</h3>
<table class="sherlock">
<tr><th>Bit</th><th>Mask</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Details</th></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>$80</td><td><b>Seen</b></td><td>Set on every thing that has been listed in a room description (#R$D526 sets it as it names each one). A piece of case evidence of the plain kind needs both of its things seen (#R$BE91).</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>$40</td><td><b>Examined</b></td><td>Set by EXAMINE (#R$DF5B). Evidence of the second kind needs both things examined.</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>$20</td><td><b>Countable</b></td><td>Set on the money things (27, 28, 32 and 35). COUNT needs it (#R$E31C).</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>The type byte, +4</h3>
<table class="sherlock">
<tr><th>Bits</th><th>Mask</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Details</th></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>$80</td><td><b>Needs light</b></td><td>Set on the player, the china man, the old man and the cabbies; an actor with it set can be "too dark to see" (#R$D078). It is also set on the oil lamp and the vehicles.</td></tr>
<tr><td>6-4</td><td>$70</td><td><b>Kind of passenger</b></td><td>GET INTO a cab needs the thing at the cab's +3 and the actor to share one of these three bits (#R$E3B8): Lestrade, Watson, Straker, the police men, Tricia Fender and Mrs Jones have $10; the Major, Strachan, Phipps, the gardener, the cook, the maid and the German agent have $40; the player has $10 too, and the china man and the old man have $20.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2-0</td><td>$07</td><td><b>How it holds things</b></td><td>0: cannot hold things. 1 IN, 2 ON, 3 BEHIND, 4 UNDER, 5 TIED TO: the word printed by #R$D5D3 when it lists what a thing holds. PUT IN refuses a thing whose bits 0-2 are all clear (#R$DED9).</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>The case table, +3 of a statement</h3>
<p>The statements and evidence of the case (the table at #R$9965) are four- or six-byte records; their byte 3 is a set of flags.</p>
<table class="sherlock">
<tr><th>Bit</th><th>Mask</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Details</th></tr>
<tr><td>0</td><td>$01</td><td><b>Waiting</b></td><td>Holmes has told this statement and it has not yet been proven; Lestrade asks about it (#R$BC46, #R$9DD8). Cleared when it is proven.</td></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>$02</td><td><b>Proven (done)</b></td><td>The statement or piece of evidence has been established. Routines such as #R$9A74 set it. Once set it stays set.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>$04</td><td><b>Has children</b></td><td>The evidence records that follow it, up to one with bit 3, are its sub-evidence; all must pass (#R$BF08).</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>$08</td><td><b>Last in its group</b></td><td>Closes a level of sub-evidence (#R$BE72).</td></tr>
<tr><td>4-5</td><td>$30</td><td><b>A routine follows</b></td><td>The record is six bytes, not four: the last two are the address of the routine that runs when it is proven (the Lestrade and Watson replies, #R$9A3D and so on). Bit 5 also marks the three "killed" statements: Watson matches those outright, while others he accepts only on a 50 per cent roll, and the waiting-statement check skips them.</td></tr>
<tr><td>6-7</td><td>$C0</td><td><b>Kind of test</b></td><td>00: both things seen; 01: both examined; 10: only true once something else has marked it done; 11: no test of its own, only its group (#R$BE91).</td></tr>
</table>


[Page:rom_symbols]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(rom_symbols-content)

[rom_symbols-content]
<p>The game uses a few places outside its own code: ROM routines, screen memory and the system-variable area. Descriptions link here when they mention one of them.</p>
<table class="rom"><tr><th>Address</th><th>Name</th><th>Kind</th><th>What the game uses it for</th></tr>
<tr id="a04C2"><td><a id="a04C2"></a>$04C2</td><td>SA-BYTES</td><td>ROM</td><td>The ROM routine that writes a block of bytes to tape. The save routine uses it for the saved game.</td></tr>
<tr id="a0556"><td><a id="a0556"></a>$0556</td><td>LD-BYTES</td><td>ROM</td><td>The ROM routine that reads a block of bytes from tape (the game load and the header read).</td></tr>
<tr id="a0F12"><td><a id="a0F12"></a>$0F12</td><td>COPY-LINE code</td><td>ROM</td><td>The part of the ROM printer routine that prints one pixel line; the screen-copy routine joins it here.</td></tr>
<tr id="a22AA"><td><a id="a22AA"></a>$22AA</td><td>PIXEL-ADDRESS</td><td>ROM</td><td>The ROM routine that turns pixel coordinates (B = y, C = x) into the screen byte address in HL and the bit position in A.</td></tr>
<tr id="a4000"><td><a id="a4000"></a>$4000</td><td>Display file</td><td>Screen</td><td>The start of the screen bitmap: 6,144 bytes, eight pixel lines per character row.</td></tr>
<tr id="a5800"><td><a id="a5800"></a>$5800</td><td>Attribute file</td><td>Screen</td><td>The start of the colour attributes: 768 bytes, one per character cell ($5800 to $5AFF).</td></tr>
<tr id="a5B00"><td><a id="a5B00"></a>$5B00</td><td>Typed line</td><td>Workspace</td><td>Where the line the player types is kept before it is split into words.</td></tr>
<tr id="a5B81"><td><a id="a5B81"></a>$5B81</td><td>Token list</td><td>Workspace</td><td>The list of words made from the typed line. A repeated command reuses the one from the previous command.</td></tr>
<tr id="a5C08"><td><a id="a5C08"></a>$5C08</td><td>LAST_K</td><td>System variable</td><td>The last key pressed. The keyboard routine takes a new key from here.</td></tr>
<tr id="a5C0B"><td><a id="a5C0B"></a>$5C0B</td><td>Line buffer</td><td>System variable area</td><td>Used by the game as the buffer where typed characters are collected, and as the tape header workspace.</td></tr>
<tr id="a5C3A"><td><a id="a5C3A"></a>$5C3A</td><td>ERR_NR</td><td>System variable</td><td>The error-number byte; the start of the standard system-variable area that IY is pointed at.</td></tr>
<tr id="a5C3B"><td><a id="a5C3B"></a>$5C3B</td><td>FLAGS</td><td>System variable</td><td>A set of ROM flag bits.</td></tr>
<tr id="a5C3C"><td><a id="a5C3C"></a>$5C3C</td><td>TV_FLAG</td><td>System variable</td><td>The ROM's output flags. The game stores a value here to switch printing to the screen on or off for a message.</td></tr>
<tr id="a5C42"><td><a id="a5C42"></a>$5C42</td><td>Spelling buffer</td><td>System variable area</td><td>Used by the game as the buffer where a dictionary word is spelled out from its kept letters, new letters and suffix.</td></tr>
</table>

[Page:trains-cabs]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(trains-cabs-content)

[trains-cabs-content]

<p>Sherlock has two kinds of public transport. <b>Trains</b> run to a timetable whether or not you are there: eight steam trains circle the underground stations and run the Leatherhead line. <b>Hansom cabs</b> are the opposite: they stand off stage until somebody hails one, and they go nowhere unless they are told to. Both are ordinary things that hold other things, so a passenger is simply something inside the vehicle, and when the vehicle moves everything in it moves too.</p>

<h3>The short answers</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>How does a timetable work?</b> Each train is an actor (#R$9860) running a short plan script that says "put the train in room X, then wait N minutes", over and over. The timetable <i>is</i> that script: the waits add up to a 30-minute circuit for the underground and a 3-hour circuit for the Leatherhead line. Where each train starts in the cycle is just which instruction its actor-table entry points at.</li>
<li><b>How does a train get from station to station?</b> One action record (#R$E4C9) puts the train in the next room with the move routine at #R$DD0E, and makes the train visible at a platform and invisible between stations. There is no track and no distance: a journey is a number of minutes spent in a "between stations" room.</li>
<li><b>Do cabs move when they are not hailed?</b> No. An unhailed cab is in room 0, off stage, and its cabbie only runs a do-nothing loop (#R$F2B2). A cab moves only when somebody hails it. In the first minutes of the game Lestrade hails cab 30 in Parliament Street and rides it to Kings Cross Road; until 3pm that is the only cab movement in a game where the player does nothing. After that Phipps, the Major and the German agent take cabs as part of their flights.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The things and rooms involved</h3>
<table class="sherlock">
<tr><th style="min-width:9em">What</th><th>Thing or room numbers</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
<tr><td>Underground trains</td><td>things 36 to 41 (actors 36 to 41)</td><td>Three run one way round the platform-one stations and three the other way round the platform-two stations.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Leatherhead trains</td><td>things 42 and 43 (actors 42 and 43)</td><td>Two trains on the same three-hour circuit, half a circuit apart.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hansom cabs and cabbies</td><td>cab 30 with cabbie 31; cab 33 with cabbie 34</td><td>The cabbie is always the next number after his cab (#R$9B50 relies on it). Both cabbies are actors (#R$9860).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Police cab</td><td>thing 109, room 105 (#R$8B65)</td><td>Driven by the local police man. It has only three routes (#R$9AA3).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rooms for a train in motion</td><td>room 2 (#R$84F5) for the underground; rooms 3 (#R$84FF) and 4 (#R$8509) for the Leatherhead line</td><td>These are the "between stations" rooms. A passenger's own room number becomes the room the train is in.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Room for a cab in motion</td><td>room 1 (#R$84EB)</td><td>The hansom cab: the cab is put here for the length of the ride, then moved to the destination.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Station rooms</td><td>Aldergate 15 (platforms 16, 17); Kings Cross 19 (platforms 20, 21 underground and 22, 23 Leatherhead); Paddington 25 (26, 27); Victoria 29 (30, 31); Leatherhead 53 (54, 55)</td><td>A station room has no description of its own; the trains stop only in the platform rooms.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>How a timetable is written</h3>
<p>Every actor has an eight-byte record in the actor table (#R$9860): its thing number, the position in its plan script (+1/+2), the plan registers, and the minute at which it next acts (+6/+7). Once per turn the main loop (#R$A064) walks the table; an actor whose minute has come runs one step of its script (#R$CC78), and the step's duration decides when it next acts. A train's script is built from two instructions only:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>DO 140 room, minutes</b>, four bytes such as <code>$02,$8C,$10,$03</code>, is "put the train in room $10 and wait 3 minutes". It is a call of the sub-script at #R$F2A5, which runs the move record (#R$E4C9) and then the wait record (#R$DDBE).</li>
<li><b>RUN #R$DDBE minutes</b>, five bytes such as <code>$07,$BE,$DD,$3C,$FF</code>, is a plain wait (here 60 minutes) with the train left where it is.</li>
</ul>
<p>A <code>GOTO</code> (<code>$0E</code>) at the end sends the script back to its start, and a <code>PASS</code> (<code>$0F</code>) is a one-minute pause. Because the waits are the only thing that spends time, adding up the minutes gives the timetable exactly.</p>

<h4>The underground: two circuits of 30 minutes</h4>
<p>Six trains share two scripts. The first circuit (#R$F21D to #R$F243) calls at the platform-one stations in the order Aldergate, Kings Cross, Paddington, Victoria; the second (#R$F246 to #R$F275) goes the other way round the platform-two stations: Kings Cross, Aldergate, Victoria, Paddington. Each train is a different distance round the same script because its actor-table entry points at a different instruction, so the three trains on a circuit are spread round it: ten minutes apart on the platform-one circuit, and nine, nine and twelve minutes apart on the platform-two circuit.</p>
<table class="sherlock">
<tr><th>Step</th><th>Platform-one circuit: where the train is</th><th>Minutes</th><th>Start of the day</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Aldergate platform one (#R$85AE)</td><td>3</td><td>actor 36 starts here</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>between stations (room 2, #R$84F5)</td><td>3</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Kings Cross platform one (#R$85E8)</td><td>3</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>between stations (room 2, #R$84F5)</td><td>1</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>waits where it is (in the same room as before)</td><td>2</td><td>actor 38 starts here</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Paddington platform one (#R$8642)</td><td>4</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>between stations (room 2, #R$84F5)</td><td>4</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>waits where it is (in the same room as before)</td><td>1</td><td>actor 37 starts here</td></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td>Victoria platform one (#R$867C)</td><td>4</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>between stations (room 2, #R$84F5)</td><td>5</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td></td><td><b>Whole circuit</b></td><td><b>30</b></td><td></td></tr>
</table>
<table class="sherlock">
<tr><th>Step</th><th>Platform-two circuit: where the train is</th><th>Minutes</th><th>Start of the day</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>waits where it is (in the same room as before)</td><td>2</td><td>actor 39 starts here</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Kings Cross platform two (#R$85F8)</td><td>3</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>between stations (room 2, #R$84F5)</td><td>3</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Aldergate platform two (#R$85BE)</td><td>3</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>between stations (room 2, #R$84F5)</td><td>1</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>waits where it is (in the same room as before)</td><td>4</td><td>actor 41 starts here</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>Victoria platform two (#R$868C)</td><td>4</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>between stations (room 2, #R$84F5)</td><td>1</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td>waits where it is (in the same room as before)</td><td>4</td><td>actor 40 starts here</td></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>Paddington platform two (#R$8652)</td><td>4</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>11</td><td>between stations (room 2, #R$84F5)</td><td>1</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td></td><td><b>Whole circuit</b></td><td><b>30</b></td><td></td></tr>
</table>
<p>From the start of the game (8:00am) a train reaches each platform-one platform every ten minutes. Each platform-two platform gets three trains in every 30 minutes, nine, nine and twelve minutes apart. A train stays three or four minutes. The first arrivals:</p>
<table class="sherlock">
<tr><th>Platform</th><th>Trains arrive at</th></tr>
<tr><td>Aldergate platform one (#R$85AE)</td><td>8:00am, 8:10am, 8:20am, 8:30am, 8:40am, 8:50am ...</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kings Cross platform one (#R$85E8)</td><td>8:06am, 8:16am, 8:26am, 8:36am, 8:46am, 8:56am ...</td></tr>
<tr><td>Paddington platform one (#R$8642)</td><td>8:02am, 8:12am, 8:22am, 8:32am, 8:42am, 8:52am ...</td></tr>
<tr><td>Victoria platform one (#R$867C)</td><td>8:01am, 8:11am, 8:21am, 8:31am, 8:41am, 8:51am ...</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kings Cross platform two (#R$85F8)</td><td>8:02am, 8:11am, 8:20am, 8:32am, 8:41am, 8:50am ...</td></tr>
<tr><td>Aldergate platform two (#R$85BE)</td><td>8:08am, 8:17am, 8:26am, 8:38am, 8:47am, 8:56am ...</td></tr>
<tr><td>Victoria platform two (#R$868C)</td><td>8:04am, 8:16am, 8:25am, 8:34am, 8:46am, 8:55am ...</td></tr>
<tr><td>Paddington platform two (#R$8652)</td><td>8:04am, 8:13am, 8:25am, 8:34am, 8:43am, 8:55am ...</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The ride from one platform to the next is three or five minutes, depending on the stretch (the platform-one train takes 3 from Aldergate to Kings Cross, 3 on to Paddington, 5 on to Victoria and 5 back to Aldergate).</p>

<h4>The Leatherhead line: one circuit of three hours</h4>
<p>The two Leatherhead trains run the same script (#R$F278 to #R$F2A2) from different starting points. A train stops at Kings Cross platform four, shunts to platform three, then spends 65 minutes on the line in room 3, and does the same at Leatherhead (platforms one and two) before the long run back.</p>
<table class="sherlock">
<tr><th>Step</th><th>Where the train is</th><th>Minutes</th><th>Start of the day</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>waits where it is (in the same room as before)</td><td>60</td><td>actor 42 starts here</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Kings Cross platform four (#R$8618)</td><td>10</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>between stations (room 4, #R$8509)</td><td>5</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Kings Cross platform three (#R$8608)</td><td>10</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>between stations (room 3, #R$84FF)</td><td>5</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>waits where it is (in the same room as before)</td><td>60</td><td>actor 43 starts here</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>Leatherhead platform one (#R$87FC)</td><td>10</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>between stations (room 4, #R$8509)</td><td>5</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td>Leatherhead platform two (#R$880C)</td><td>10</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>between stations (room 3, #R$84FF)</td><td>5</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td></td><td><b>Whole circuit</b></td><td><b>180</b></td><td></td></tr>
</table>
<p>The two trains are half a circuit apart, so a train arrives at each platform every 90 minutes:</p>
<table class="sherlock">
<tr><th>Platform</th><th>Trains arrive at</th></tr>
<tr><td>Kings Cross platform four (#R$8618)</td><td>9:00am, 10:30am, 12:00pm, 1:30pm, 3:00pm, 4:30pm, 6:00pm, 7:30pm ...</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kings Cross platform three (#R$8608)</td><td>9:15am, 10:45am, 12:15pm, 1:45pm, 3:15pm, 4:45pm, 6:15pm, 7:45pm ...</td></tr>
<tr><td>Leatherhead platform one (#R$87FC)</td><td>9:00am, 10:30am, 12:00pm, 1:30pm, 3:00pm, 4:30pm, 6:00pm, 7:30pm ...</td></tr>
<tr><td>Leatherhead platform two (#R$880C)</td><td>9:15am, 10:45am, 12:15pm, 1:45pm, 3:15pm, 4:45pm, 6:15pm, 7:45pm ...</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The journey from Kings Cross platform three to Leatherhead takes 65 minutes. A passenger who stays on the train is carried on: at the end of a stop the train moves to the other platform of the same station, so riding on from Leatherhead platform one takes you to platform two, five minutes later, by way of the between-stations room 4 (you cannot get out there). This timetable decides the endgame chase. Phipps boards the train that leaves Leatherhead platform two at 4:55pm, and the next one is 90 minutes behind it (#R$E7E7).</p>

<h3>How a train moves from station to station</h3>
<p>The move is one action record, "put a train in a room" (#R$E4C9, internal verb #R$F2A5(140)). It does four things:</p>
<ol>
<li>Takes the room number from the direct object (#R$A032($A034)). If it is room 2, 3 or 4, the train is about to leave a station, so it prints THE TRAIN PULLS OUT OF THE STATION. (#R$7611) and uses the routine that <i>clears</i> the train's revealed bit (+5 bit 7, #R$9C60). Otherwise it uses the routine that <i>sets</i> it (#R$9C56). A train is therefore visible at a platform and invisible between stations: the visibility test (#R$C821) refuses anything without that bit.</li>
<li>Moves the train, with everything inside it, to the room (#R$DD0E). The move updates the +15 room byte of the train and of each passenger, and the player's own room, so a passenger's room number follows the train: 23, then 4, then 22, then 3 on the first stage of the Leatherhead run.</li>
<li>Clears bit 6 of the new room's record (the "visited" bit), so the room description is shown in full again.</li>
<li>Returns "done"; the script then waits the stated minutes.</li>
</ol>
<p>Everything a train prints is shown only when the player can see it. Before each actor's turn the engine switches printing off (#R$CC61) and switches it on only if the actor and the player can see each other (#R$C821), so a train pulling out on the other side of London is silent. When a train arrives at your platform the move narration prints A STEAM TRAIN ENTERS.</p>

<h4>Getting on and off</h4>
<p>CLIMB INTO TRAIN works like getting into any container, and needs the train to be visible, which means it is at your platform. While you are aboard the room description reads "You are in a steam train" plus the platform or, between stations, "riding in a steam train through the picturesque English countryside". GET OUT OF TRAIN is refused between stations (the message is YOU CANNOT GET OUT OF THE STEAM TRAIN.); at a platform it works. Characters who follow you, such as Watson and Lestrade, climb in after you with the "follow" script (#R$E476), and a train that carries Lestrade is described with him in it.</p>

<h3>Hansom cabs</h3>
<h4>Where a cab is, and how it is hailed</h4>
<p>At the start of the game both cabs are off stage, in room 0. The HAIL action (#R$E2C7, shared action #R$AA71 entry 11) works only if all of these hold (the pre-check at #R$E298):</p>
<ul>
<li>the direct object is cab 30 or cab 33;</li>
<li>the room you are in is one of the ten cab stands, which means there is a cab journey from here (#R$9C07);</li>
<li>the cab is in room 0, so it is not already on a job; and</li>
<li>the cabbie is free (#R$CFB9).</li>
</ul>
<p>The action then puts the cab in your room (#R$DD0E), remembers which cab it is (#R$E2C6), and starts the cabbie on the fare script at #R$F2B6 with you and the cab as its two objects (#R$E2EE).</p>

<h4>What the cabbie does</h4>
<p>The cabbie is an actor with the script at #R$F2B2. Its first two instructions are a <code>PASS</code> and a <code>GOTO</code> back to itself, so an unhailed cabbie does nothing for ever. HAIL gives him a second entry point to run, with these stages:</p>
<ol>
<li><b>Greeting and waiting.</b> He says GOOD MORNING GUV'NOR (#R$6BAC) and starts a countdown of 4 minutes plus a random 0 to 4 (#R$E52F). Each minute he checks whether you are there (#R$F2C2); if you are, he moves on to the next stage. If you are not, he ticks the countdown (#R$E57A), and when it runs out he prints WITH A PRACTISED FLICK OF THE REINS, YOU MOVES ON DOWN THE STREET IN SEARCH OF OTHER FARES. (#R$6BF6) and puts the cab back in room 0 (#R$F376).</li>
<li><b>Waiting for a destination.</b> While you are there he waits for a "go to" order, re-asking WELL ARE WE GOIN' ANYWHERE GUV'NOR? (#R$6C1B) whenever a countdown (5 plus a random 0 to 3 minutes) runs out, and then starting again from the first stage. An order is SAY TO CABBIE "GO TO ..." or "GO FURIOUSLY TO ...".</li>
<li><b>The ride.</b> See below.</li>
<li><b>Payment.</b> When the ride ends the cabbie names the fare (RIGHT GUV'NOR THAT WILL BE 10D., #R$E6BC). If you stay in the cab without paying he repeats it about every six minutes (a countdown of 5 plus a random 0 to 2 minutes), and the cab never drives off with you. If you get out without paying the cabbie, who now appears to be getting quite agitated, says WHAT ABOUT ME MONEY GUV'NOR? (message #R$6BC7), and if you then walk away he shouts after you (#R$E70E, message #R$6C54): YOU BLOODY SNOB, WATCH OUT IF WE EVER MEET AGAIN. Then he puts the cab back in room 0 (#R$F376) and goes back to his idle loop. Nothing else follows from not paying: he does not chase you, and nothing in the cabbie's script punishes you further. Once the account is settled, or change is given, the script moves on the same way.</li>
</ol>
<p>Whether a fare is owed is kept in the cabbie's purse: the ride makes his balance negative by the fare, so "negative" means a fare is owed, "positive" means change is due and zero means settled (#R$E63E, #R$E654, #R$E66F).</p>

<h4>The ride</h4>
<p>A ride is two shared actions, one for each speed. GO TO (#R$9AF4, shared action 14) and GO FURIOUSLY TO (#R$9AE1, shared action 15) both do the same things:</p>
<ol>
<li>Check that the cab can get there from where it stands (#R$9B50): look for a journey between the two rooms in the journey table (#R$9B7F, searched either way round by #R$9C07). If there is none, the cabbie says SORRY GUV'NOR I CANNOT GET THERE FROM 'ERE.</li>
<li>Price the ride (#R$9B25): the fare is the rate times (the journey's minutes divided by 5, rounded down, plus 1). GO TO charges 2d a step and GO FURIOUSLY TO 6d. Baker Street to Parliament Street is 20 minutes, so 2 &times; 5 = 10d. The fare is taken off the cabbie's purse.</li>
<li>Set the length of the action to the journey's minutes. Going furiously takes two minutes off, so the ride is two minutes quicker but three times dearer.</li>
<li>Put the cab in room 1 (the hansom cab) and make it invisible (#R$9C60), then hand the ride to the vehicle sub-script at #R$F3AD, which, when the minutes have passed, moves the cab and everything in it to the destination (#R$9C41) and makes it visible again.</li>
</ol>
<p>So a cab ride does not pass through the streets in between: the cab sits in room 1 for the journey time, then appears at the destination.</p>

<h4>The journey table</h4>
<p>There are ten cab stands, all street rooms, and every pair has an entry: 45 in all (#R$9B7F). Leatherhead and Old Mill Road are not in it. They are reached by train and by the police cab. The minutes for each pair:</p>
<table class="sherlock">
<tr><th></th><th>Portman St</th><th>Baker St</th><th>Parliament St</th><th>Slater St</th><th>Aldergate St</th><th>Kings Cross Rd</th><th>Bishops Rd</th><th>Buckingham Palace Rd</th><th>Camden St</th><th>Sidmouth St</th></tr>
<tr><th>Portman St</th><td>&ndash;</td><td>3</td><td>7</td><td>20</td><td>19</td><td>18</td><td>7</td><td>12</td><td>6</td><td>13</td></tr>
<tr><th>Baker St</th><td>3</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>20</td><td>25</td><td>26</td><td>12</td><td>6</td><td>15</td><td>24</td><td>9</td></tr>
<tr><th>Parliament St</th><td>7</td><td>20</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>18</td><td>18</td><td>12</td><td>19</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>15</td></tr>
<tr><th>Slater St</th><td>20</td><td>25</td><td>18</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>3</td><td>20</td><td>30</td><td>20</td><td>17</td><td>25</td></tr>
<tr><th>Aldergate St</th><td>19</td><td>26</td><td>18</td><td>3</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>13</td><td>28</td><td>17</td><td>9</td><td>11</td></tr>
<tr><th>Kings Cross Rd</th><td>18</td><td>12</td><td>12</td><td>20</td><td>13</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>11</td><td>16</td><td>18</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><th>Bishops Rd</th><td>7</td><td>6</td><td>19</td><td>30</td><td>28</td><td>11</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>15</td><td>24</td><td>13</td></tr>
<tr><th>Buckingham Palace Rd</th><td>12</td><td>15</td><td>2</td><td>20</td><td>17</td><td>16</td><td>15</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>12</td><td>19</td></tr>
<tr><th>Camden St</th><td>6</td><td>24</td><td>4</td><td>17</td><td>9</td><td>18</td><td>24</td><td>12</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>22</td></tr>
<tr><th>Sidmouth St</th><td>13</td><td>9</td><td>15</td><td>25</td><td>11</td><td>3</td><td>13</td><td>19</td><td>22</td><td>&ndash;</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Fares at the normal rate are 2d for each full five minutes plus 2d: 3 minutes costs 2d, 6 to 9 minutes 4d, 20 minutes 10d, 30 minutes 14d.</p>

<h4>The police cab</h4>
<p>The police cab (thing 109, with the room 105 inside) is driven by the local police man and has only three routes (#R$9AA3): Old Mill Road to Main Street, Main Street to Old Mill Road and Main Street to Kings Cross Road. A plain GO TO (#R$9A99) takes 20 minutes longer than GO FURIOUSLY TO (shared action 2): the run out to Old Mill Road takes 114 minutes by plain GO TO and 94 going furiously. It is the only way to Old Mill Road, which is why the endgame needs it.</p>

<h3>Do cabs move when they are not hailed?</h3>
<p>No. A cab that has not been hailed is off stage in room 0 and cannot be seen or reached. Its cabbie runs the do-nothing loop at #R$F2B2, so no cabbie ever leaves on his own and no cab ever wanders the streets. The same holds after a ride: when the fare has been dealt with the cab goes back to room 0. A cab is only ever in a street because someone hailed it, and that someone can be a character as well as you: Lestrade hails cab 30 at the start of the game (the action is the same HAIL, run by actor 2) and rides it from Parliament Street to Kings Cross Road, arriving at about 8:16am, after which the cab goes back to room 0. Other characters use cabs the same way: Phipps and the Major each run the cab-hailing sub-script in their own plans, and the German agent runs Phipps's flight code, which includes it, and Lestrade's own cab sub-script is at #R$EBA4. Cab 33 stays in room 0 until somebody hails it.</p>
<p>The trains are different in exactly this respect: they never stand idle, and a train you cannot see (between stations, or at a platform in another part of London) is still running its script.</p>


[Page:trivia-by-theme]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(trivia-by-theme-content)

[trivia-by-theme-content]
<p>All 78 trivia entries, grouped by what they are about. Each link goes to the full entry on the <a href="reference/facts.html">Trivia</a> page, which lists them in this same order.</p>
<ul><li><a href="#winning">Winning the game</a></li><li><a href="#case">The case, the evidence and the endings</a></li><li><a href="#world">The world: people, places and things</a></li><li><a href="#parser">The parser and the keyboard</a></li><li><a href="#engine">Engine internals</a></li><li><a href="#corrections">Corrections along the way</a></li></ul>
<h2 id="winning">Winning the game</h2><p>The route that wins, why each step is where it is, and the timings that make or break it.</p><ul>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#the_last_scene">How the game is won (and was won, in the emulator)</a><small>The only winning text in the game is at #R$6AF3, and it is printed in one place: Phipps&#x27;s script, when he walks onto platform one at Victoria and finds...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#the_win_typed">The winning line from Wednesday 10:53am, typed</a><small>SAY TO LESTRADE &quot;THE SALE LOCATION IS OLD MILL ROAD&quot; in Scotland Yard at 10:53am (the singed note examined, PHIPPS HAS PLANS already proven) and...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#second_winning_route">A second winning route: shadow Lestrade from Scotland Yard</a><small>tools/experiments/playthrough/v13_shadow_lestrade.cmds (368 lines) wins too.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#replay_is_deterministic">The game is deterministic: the same typed commands always give the same game</a><small>The whole unpoked win, 444 typed lines starting from a fresh boot, was replayed from the file and ended in exactly the same state (clock 6:43pm, room...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#tuesday_ordering">Tuesday&#x27;s order of work that fits the clock</a><small>Played without pokes: 12:55am cab to Kings Cross Road; 7:30am train; 9:00 Leatherhead; 9:20 rock with Lestrade; letters from Basil&#x27;s library safe by...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#burning_note_typed">The burning note: TAKE NOTE FROM FIREPLACE, then drop and plain TAKE it</a><small>Played without pokes on Wednesday.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#followers_lag_one_turn">Lestrade and Watson follow one turn behind you, so a train can leave without them</a><small>Seen at Kings Cross platform one on Wednesday evening.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#train_goes_on_to_platform_two">Riding the Leatherhead train past your stop costs the police cab</a><small>The steam train from Kings Cross platform four reaches Leather Head platform one at 1:30pm (if it left at noon), and then is &#x27;shunted between...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#police_cab_seats">The police cab fits four: you, Watson, Lestrade and the constable - not Chief Constable Straker</a><small>In the main street at Leather Head on Wednesday: if you climb in the minute before Lestrade, the cab holds you, Watson and the local police man and...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#ordering_the_cab_strands_lestrade">Giving the police cab your own orders leaves Lestrade behind</a><small>At 4:47pm, back in the main street with Lestrade, Watson and the constable aboard, SAY TO POLICE MAN &quot;GO FURIOUSLY TO KINGS CROSS ROAD&quot; made the cab...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#phipps_shoots_the_bedroom">Basil&#x27;s bedroom is deadly at 9:50am on Wednesday, even in disguise</a><small>Waiting in the bedroom (room 35) in the china man&#x27;s disguise through 9:50am on Wednesday gets you shot: the game ends.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#window_exit_bedroom">The bedroom window works both ways: SOUTHWEST to the front yard, WEST to Camden Street</a><small>The same window you enter by on Tuesday evening (NORTHEAST from the front yard, room 102) lets you leave on Wednesday morning without passing through...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#messenger_note_watched">The messenger who delivers a note to Basil is shown only if you are watching</a><small>On Wednesday morning, at about 9:50, Phipps&#x27;s script (#R$EEE9) checks whether you are in Camden Street or Basil&#x27;s front yard (or the china man is).</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#train_circle_timings">The underground train: Kings Cross platform one at 6:29, Victoria platform one at 6:41, Paddington at 6:43</a><small>The steam trains on the underground circle run on a fixed timetable.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#lestrade_orders_tried">Which orders Lestrade takes on platform two (and when)</a><small>Played on 9 November from Wednesday 4:55pm on Leatherhead platform two, after the chase.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#lestrade_cab_clash">Lestrade rides the same cab: keep out of cabs at 12:20-12:45am and 7:05-7:20am</a><small>Lestrade hails a cab twice in the night: Scotland Yard side at about 12:20am (Slater Street to Parliament Street) and from Parliament Street at...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#cab_change_and_fares">Fares seen in the run: 2d from Camden Street to Parliament Street, 6d from Parliament Street to Kings Cross Road</a><small>Paying with PAY 10D TO CABBIE, the cabbie gave change each time (&#x27;You pay 10d.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#cab_fares">Cab fares</a><small>A hansom cab ride costs 2d for every five minutes of the journey, plus 2d: the journey times are in the table at #R$9B7F and the fare is worked out at...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#stale_quotes_cabbie">Quoted speech is queued: a cabbie can ignore a destination until old quotes are flushed</a><small>Every quotation typed in a SAY TO is added to a list at #R$9FEC (#R$B049); the SAY that runs takes the FIRST node off it (#R$B86A).</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#pay_not_give_money">Pay a cabbie with PAY 10D TO CABBIE, not GIVE MONEY TO CABBIE</a><small>GIVE MONEY hands over the whole purse (thing 27) to the cabbie, who keeps it; after that INVENTORY shows no money, PAY says DON&#x27;T BE SILLY, and the...</small></li>
</ul>
<h2 id="case">The case, the evidence and the endings</h2><p>What the game counts as proof, what ends it early, and how it can end.</p><ul>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#three_endings">How the case can end</a><small>The game ends through records the characters&#x27; scripts run, all finishing at #R$9E74.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#meet_lestrade_by_3pm">Be introduced to Lestrade by 3pm on Monday, or the game ends</a><small>Confirmed in the emulator: a player who only types WAIT from the start is told at 2:59pm on Monday &#x27;I do not think you are doing too well, surely...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#the_case_table">The solution is a table of statements and evidence</a><small>The case lives at #R$9965: eleven statements of the form &#x27;thing / relation / thing&#x27;, each with the evidence it needs and a routine to run when it is...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#watson_remembers">Watson remembers what you tell him about the case</a><small>Telling Watson a statement about the case (SAY TO WATSON &quot;MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF&quot;) adds it to a list of three-byte statements at #R$9FF2($9FF4), and he...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#watson_connections">Watson&#x27;s connections</a><small>Tell Watson something about the case and he either praises you or, by chance, wonders aloud about &#x27;some connection between&#x27; two things, picked from...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#catch_the_major">To clear the Major, catch him at the opium den</a><small>MAJOR PERCIVAL FFOULKES IS INNOCENT can only be proven through a second statement, MAJOR PERCIVAL FFOULKES VISITED OPIUM DEN, whose only evidence is...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#tricia_is_mrs_jones">Tricia Fender is Mrs Jones in disguise - and that is how the last marker is earned</a><small>Shared action 3 (#R$E1A3, in Tricia Fender&#x27;s own action list) is not just a question.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#blackmail_statement">TRICIA FENDER BLACKMAILED MRS BROWN does nothing but get a shrug</a><small>Said to Lestrade in Scotland Yard on Tuesday after the other statements, he answers &#x27;I do not see the importance of that Holmes.&#x27; and the only change...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#early_sale_statement_unneeded">Saying THE SALE LOCATION IS OLD MILL ROAD on Tuesday afternoon is not needed</a><small>The first attempt at 2:13pm on Tuesday (note not yet examined) only gets &#x27;I do not believe...&#x27; and was in the route by habit.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#wall_safe_trap">Opening Basil&#x27;s wall safe on Monday gets you shot</a><small>The wall safe in Basil&#x27;s library at Cobden Lane in Leatherhead (thing 89, room 58) holds the letters.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#large_rock_tuesday">The large rock: EXAMINE CLOSELY DEEP STREAM, on Tuesday, east of the bridge</a><small>(Corrected by the next fact: Lestrade must be in the room too.) Read from the code: the record at #R$E0AD (shared action 16, EXAMINE CLOSELY the deep...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#large_rock_needs_lestrade">The rock needs Lestrade in the room: Tuesday morning, east side of the bridge</a><small>Corrects the fact on the rock above.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#disguise_needs_a_trick">The disguise cannot be picked up with the lamp - but WEAR then TAKE OFF carries it</a><small>In Holmes&#x27;s closet at the start, TAKE CHINA MANS DISGUISE answers &#x27;I SEE NO CHINA MANS DISGUISE THAT YOU CAN TAKE&#x27; while the player carries the oil...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#basils_london_house">Basil&#x27;s Camden Street house: what was observed, and the wall in the way</a><small>Observed with nobody interfering and in the playthrough: the front door (thing 91) and the bedroom window (thing 79) start locked.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#the_game_goes_on">The game does not wait for you</a><small>The player&#x27;s script reads each line with a time limit (#R$D898, #R$A536: 3,000 keyboard polls).</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#bedtime">The household goes to bed at 9pm</a><small>At 9pm Daphne Strachan goes to her room and the cook and the gardener leave the game for the night, each marked SLEEPING and no longer counted as a...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#bring_a_lamp">It gets dark at 8pm</a><small>Outdoors between 8pm and 6am the player cannot see without a light: LOOK on Baker Street at 9pm says &#x27;SORRY IT IS TOO DARK.&#x27; unless the oil lamp from...</small></li>
</ul>
<h2 id="world">The world: people, places and things</h2><p>The cast, the map, and the odd ways the world is built.</p><ul>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#cast_list">The cast and objects, read from the game&#x27;s own records</a><small>110 entities, named from their own word slots: you, Watson, Inspector Lestrade, Chief Constable Straker, Strachan, Phipps, Major Percival Ffoulkes,...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#map_decoded_and_walked">The map: 106 rooms, 206 exits, every one tried</a><small>The room table at #R$840B gives 106 rooms.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#police_men_are_doors">The police men are doors</a><small>The two local police men at Browns and Jones front gates are listed in the room table as the &#x27;door&#x27; of the exit they guard.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#lestrade_hails_a_cab">Inspector Lestrade hails a cab every turn from the very start</a><small>Logging every action attempt shows actor 2 - Inspector Lestrade, who starts in room 11 - trying HAIL on the hansom cab (object 30) and then GET INTO...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#plan_scripts">Every character, the player included, runs a script</a><small>Sherlock&#x27;s characters are not driven by code but by scripts in a small language of fifteen opcodes (one unused): do a command, run an action record,...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#waiting_for_the_hour">Characters keep appointments by failing until the time is right</a><small>Verb #R$F1D6(136) succeeds only when the clock&#x27;s hour equals its argument, counted 1-24: #R$F1D6 turns 13-24 into 1-12 with &#x27;p&#x27;, and 1-12 into 1-12 with &#x27;a&#x27;,...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#busy_characters">Characters can be too busy to do what you ask</a><small>Bit 6 of a plan-script instruction marks it as busy: a character on such an instruction answers a request with &#x27;Sorry, I cannot do that now.&#x27; (#R$CBA7).</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#thirteen_pictures">Only fourteen rooms have a picture</a><small>The picture table at #R$F60C lists 14 of the game&#x27;s rooms, and two of them (platforms one and two) share a picture, so there are 13 pictures in all,...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#scenery_words">Scenery</a><small>Rooms can mention things that are not objects - the &#x27;two broad windows&#x27; of Holmes&#x27;s sitting room, say.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#examine_closely">EXAMINE CLOSELY finds hidden things that EXAMINE does not</a><small>Verb #R$E135(20) is EXAMINE CLOSELY (in that word order: EXAMINE CLOSELY TRASH PILE; &#x27;EXAMINE TRASH PILE CLOSELY&#x27; gets &#x27;WHAT ?&#x27;).</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#follow_climbs_in">FOLLOW WATSON puts you in Watson&#x27;s armchair</a><small>Confirmed in the emulator: FOLLOW WATSON at the start answers &#x27;You climb into watsons armchair.&#x27;, the player&#x27;s container changes from 255 (none) to...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#go_furiously">GO FURIOUSLY TO</a><small>The verb table has two GO TOs: verb #R$9A99(29), GO TO, and verb #R$9ACB(30), GO FURIOUSLY TO (MADLY and QUICKLY are synonyms of FURIOUSLY).</small></li>
</ul>
<h2 id="parser">The parser and the keyboard</h2><p>How typed commands are understood, and what the words really mean.</p><ul>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#verb_system_decoded">The whole verb system, decoded from the game&#x27;s own tables</a><small>Typed commands are matched against 54 verb patterns at #R$8259; the verb number is simply the matched pattern&#x27;s position.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#dictionary_front_coded">The dictionary stores each word as an edit of the previous one</a><small>Each of the 592 words keeps some letters from the word before it and adds a few new 5-bit letters, with common endings (S, ES, ING, ED, LY...) taken...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#synonyms">Synonyms in the dictionary</a><small>Twenty-nine dictionary records stand for another word: their count byte has bit 7 set and two more bytes give the record to use instead, which the...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#abbreviations">Short words</a><small>Commands can be abbreviated: the tokenizer (#R$D9C5, #R$DA7D) matches what you type against the start of each dictionary word.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#object_flag_words">The game&#x27;s own error words name the object flags</a><small>When an action needs an object&#x27;s flag to be set or clear and it is not, #R$CB7B prints &#x27;THE IS&#x27; and a word from one of two tables, one per flag bit:...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#quit_means_load">QUIT does not quit</a><small>QUIT and LOAD share one handler in the parser&#x27;s table of special words (#R$AF6B): both end the game the way a game-over message does and ask for a...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#pounds_shillings_pence">The parser understands pounds, shillings and pence</a><small>Typed sums of money are pre-decimal: £1, 3S, 6D, 2/6 or 1/- all work, and are converted to pence ((pounds x 20 + shillings) x 12 + pence, #R$DB67).</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#cursor_keys">One-key moves</a><small>On an empty command line the cursor keys are compass moves: the line editor (#R$D978) turns the codes for left, right, down and up into W, E, S and N...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#repeat_key">&#x27;@&#x27; repeats the last command</a><small>Typing &#x27;@&#x27; (SYMBOL SHIFT and 2) on an empty command line does the last command again: the line editor (#R$D99E) sets a flag that makes the word-list...</small></li>
</ul>
<h2 id="engine">Engine internals</h2><p>How the program itself works: actor table, scripts, dispatch and printing.</p><ul>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#boot_actor_table">The #R$9860 actor-table walk is reentrant, not boot-only</a><small>First confirmed in the emulator simply as &#x27;execution reaches this during boot&#x27; - true, but incomplete.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#actor_table_record_size">The actor table at #R$9860 has confirmed 8-byte records</a><small>Confirmed two ways: (1) #R$CC0E&#x27;s own search step advances by literally LD DE,$0008; ADD IY,DE between candidates, and (2) separately, the...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#action_dispatcher_found">Found the main per-turn action dispatcher (computed JP (HL))</a><small>#R$CB2C saves IX/IY, jumps through HL via a plain JP (HL) at #R$CB40, then restores IX/IY.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#verb_table_found">Found the table at #R$AA71 - but it&#x27;s not simply &#x27;verb number&#x27;</a><small>Direct-indexed (not searched): entry N sits at #R$AA71 + (N-1)*2, a plain 16-bit value.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#object_id_catalog">Built a real object/actor id catalog via the visibility test</a><small>Breakpointing #R$C821 (confirmed as an &#x27;is this visible here&#x27; test) during known commands gives a stable, repeated id for things that are actually...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#dispatch_mechanisms_unified">Unified three previously-separate findings into one system</a><small>#R$C9C0 (the per-candidate walk found early on), #R$CA64 (the per-turn command loop), and #R$CB2C (the computed-jump dispatcher) are confirmed to be one...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#aa71_purpose_confirmed">Found what #R$AA71 is actually for: a shared action library</a><small>Breakpointing #R$C960 (the #R$AA71 lookup) across boot, the intro, and ten real commands shows it fires selectively.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#aa71_action_library_expanded">Mapped a real family of shared actions in #R$AA71&#x27;s library</a><small>Breakpointing #R$C960 across many commands, and logging which actor triggered each hit, confirms the player&#x27;s own SIT IN, GET INTO, GET OUT OF and...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#print_char_verified">PLOT-CHAR (#R$C388) recovers real game text without a ROM</a><small>Confirmed in the emulator: setting a breakpoint on #R$C388 and reading A each time (rather than trying to render real pixels, which needs the ROM font...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#messages_rendered">Every fixed message, rendered by the game itself</a><small>The message printer at #R$A59F is a bytecode interpreter: dictionary word references, packed 5-bit letters, literal punctuation and 29 control codes.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#unused_hang_opcode">Plan opcode 12 would hang the game</a><small>The interpreter&#x27;s test for opcode 12 is CP $0C / JR Z,#R$CD08 - and #R$CD08 is that JR Z itself, so with Z set it jumps to itself for ever.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#saved_game_on_the_tape">A saved game on the original tape</a><small>Besides the loading screen and the game, the tape carries a third block, a CODE file called &#x27;SHD V2.0&#x27; for #R$840B-#R$A01A($A01F).</small></li>
</ul>
<h2 id="corrections">Corrections along the way</h2><p>Findings that were revised or withdrawn while the work went on. Kept so the record is honest.</p><ul>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#always_checked_table_found">CORRECTED: the 5 &#x27;checked every turn&#x27; entries are the player&#x27;s plan script</a><small>An earlier round described #R$9923($9927) as &#x27;five 5-byte records ($99,$13,,)&#x27;.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#verb_number_hypothesis_double_checked">The &#x27;OPEN = verb #R$DD03(6)&#x27; coincidence did not hold up - correction</a><small>While tracing the table above, one of the five commands tested (OPEN) happened to produce a command-specific address that matched the #R$AA71 table&#x27;s...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#npc_turn_processing_confirmed">The actor-table walk is per-turn NPC processing, not boot-only</a><small>A real correction to earlier work in this project: the #R$9860 actor-table walk documented in overlay_01_boot.py as part of the game&#x27;s boot sequence is...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#room_text_path_resolved">Resolved: the room-text print path, and a false alarm explained</a><small>Earlier in this project, breakpointing #R$C2BA during a real LOOK command got zero hits, and was written up honestly as an unresolved worry.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#b91f_is_setup_only">CORRECTED: #R$B91F runs every turn, for every actor</a><small>An earlier round reported #R$B91F as intro-only: 25 hits during the intro, none during LOOK.</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#dictionary_matching_confirmed">Corrected: the original dictionary-matching path WAS the real one</a><small>Earlier in this project, a test of the #R$DA7D comparison routine and the #R$A94D word-expansion routine both appeared to show this wasn&#x27;t the code path...</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#dictionary_to_verb_number_corrected">CORRECTION: &#x27;TAKE is verb #5, traced to handler&#x27; does not hold up</a><small>A previous round claimed to have traced TAKE all the way from typed input to a confirmed handler at #R$AA71&#x27;s entry #5 (#R$E003).</small></li>
<li><a href="reference/facts.html#get_actor_room_confirmed">#R$D495 confirmed as &#x27;get actor A&#x27;s current room number&#x27;</a><small>Confirmed empirically: called with A=0 while the player was in the sitting room, got $08 back; moved the player north into the small closet in the...</small></li>
</ul>

